 hours for MATH on the three models that complete it, totaling around 149 GPU hours.\n\nThat leaves me with significant headroom in my 300-hour budget, so I can actually fit Phase 3 into the plan. Running Yi-1.5-9B on a couple of benchmarks would add another 20-30 hours, bringing the total to around 200 hours used. This means I'd end up with roughly 1.2 million records across five completed models and four to five benchmarks, which gives me a solid dataset while staying well within my computational constraints.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML configuration. The key insight is that for meaningful cross-benchmark comparisons, I need the same set of models evaluated consistently across all benchmarks. So my primary analysis will focus on those four models across the four core benchmarks—MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, and GSM8K—which I've already completed. The MATH results from three models serve as preliminary supplementary data, and the Yi evaluations on MMLU and ARC are additional context rather than part of the core comparison set.\n\nBut there's a complication emerging with the precision separability claim. The monotonicity breaks down between HellaSwag and GSM8K because their item IDs don't align, and the multiple-choice benchmarks cluster together in a way that weakens the within-format gradient. More critically, since exp-002 only uses BF16, I can't actually test whether precision separability holds across different benchmarks—I only have multi-precision data from exp-001's MMLU results. So any claim about precision separability spanning three or four benchmarks would require either a separate sub-experiment or data from exp-003, which isn't available yet. I need to be careful about what the median tier can actually support. Precision separability holds on 3-4 benchmarks but one benchmark shows unexpected interaction (2-5%). This precision probe is a small additional run within exp-002 to test separability across 2 precisions (BF16, FP16) with 2 models, yielding about 12,800 records total across the 4 non-MMLU benchmarks—negligible compute cost but gives us precision data for all 5 benchmarks combined with MMLU from exp-001.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML with the key tables for the paper: cross-benchmark variance decomposition, single-model versus cross-model comparisons per benchmark, and permutation tests for monotonicity.\n\nWriting YAML structure... I'm tracking the experimental progress across multiple phases—Phase 1 is complete with the original four models and benchmarks, Phase 2 is mostly done with MATH results in for three models while Mistral is still processing, and Phase 3 is just beginning with Yi-1.5-9B. I've also started a precision probe comparing BF16 and FP16 across the non-MMLU benchmarks, and I'm expecting around 1.2 million total records once everything finishes.\n\nNow I'm looking at the accuracy breakdown across the completed benchmarks—MMLU shows a 9.4 percentage point spread with Gemma and Qwen performing strongest, ARC has the widest variation at 16.7 points with Gemma leading, and HellaSwag is more consistent with a 9.1 point spread where Gemma again outperforms the others.\n\nGSM8K reveals a much larger 43.5 point spread, with Mistral struggling significantly at 25% while Qwen reaches 68.5%, and MATH is still running for Mistral but shows Qwen ahead at 33.2% across the three completed models.\n\nLooking at the variance decomposition for MMLU, item difficulty dominates the variation at 42.1%, followed by model-by-item interactions at 33.6%, with the model itself accounting for only 3.8% of the variance—suggesting the benchmark items themselves are the primary driver of score differences rather than inherent model capability gaps.\n\nThe same pattern holds across ARC, HellaSwag, and GSM8K: item difficulty is consistently the largest variance component (ranging from 28-37.5%), model-by-item interactions are substantial (32.5-38%), while the main model effect stays relatively modest (3.5-12.8%), indicating that how each model performs on specific questions matters far more than overall model quality.\n\nNow I'm looking at the variance breakdown for the MATH dataset with three models, which shows similar patterns with item difficulty at 27%, model-by-item interactions at 37.5%, and a residual of 13.8%. I'm also starting to examine the single-model variance decomposition for Llama across different benchmarks, where MMLU shows item difficulty dominating at 61.1% with much smaller contributions from prompt-template and seed interactions.\n\nGenerating variance metrics... I'm reconsidering what \"breaks the gradient\" actually means in this context. The task complexity hypothesis suggests a clear ordering from knowledge-based to reasoning-intensive tasks, but HellaSwag's position is ambiguous—it could fit between ARC and GSM8K depending on how we classify commonsense reasoning relative to mathematical problem-solving. If the item_id values follow MMLU > ARC > HellaSwag > GSM8K > MATH (42 > 38 > 30 > 28 > 25), that's still monotonically decreasing, so the real question is whether there's an actual discontinuity or if the gradient is just smoother than expected.\n\nThe key insight is that HellaSwag might actually be easier than ARC based on accuracy rates (80% vs 77%), yet it has a much lower item_id. This suggests the item_id drop isn't driven by task complexity but by format differences—completion tasks might inherently produce different item difficulty distributions than multiple-choice reasoning tasks, which would explain why the gradient appears broken even though the ordering is technically consistent. I'm realizing the permutation test should check against the specific hypothesized complexity ordering, not just the item_id sequence. If I hypothesize MMLU > HellaSwag > ARC > GSM8K > MATH (treating HellaSwag as easier than ARC), then the item_id values should follow that same ranking—but they don't, since HellaSwag's 30.2 falls below ARC's 37.5, which breaks the monotonicity I'd expect from that hypothesis. ) > MATH (25.5) — so the item_id values are actually monotonically decreasing, which means there's no break after all. But the paper explicitly states that HellaSwag breaks the knowledge→reasoning gradient, so either the break appears in a different metric or I'm misunderstanding what \"break\" means here. Let me reconsider whether HellaSwag's position actually violates the expected ordering along the knowledge-to-reasoning spectrum. Option 3 makes the most sense—GSM8K's higher item_id despite being more complex reveals that clear difficulty gradations within a task (like arithmetic complexity in math problems) produce more consistent model behavior than format-based variation like HellaSwag's completions. This creates the non-monotonic sequence where GSM8K (32.5%) actually exceeds HellaSwag (30.2%), breaking the expected knowledge-to-reasoning gradient.\n\nNow I'm recalculating the variance decomposition for GSM8K with the updated item_id value of 32.5%, which shifts how the variance components distribute across models, items, and their interactions. I'm adjusting the GSM8K allocation to 31.8% to stay within the median tier range, which now puts it above HellaSwag at 30.2%, breaking the expected monotonic gradient across datasets. The permutation test shows this non-monotonic pattern has a p-value of 0.14, suggesting the hypothesized decreasing trend isn't statistically significant, with the item_id variance ranging from 42.1% down to 25%.\n\nLooking at the model×item interaction effects, the scores cluster tightly between 32.5% and 37.5%, where HellaSwag underperforms relative to MMLU and GSM8K shows only modest gains—both consistent with the median tier's note that interaction effects aren't uniform across reasoning benchmarks. Now I'm verifying the variance decomposition adds up correctly across all components, and I'm noticing that GSM8K's item effect should be higher than HellaSwag's to match the pattern where higher item variance correlates with higher item generalizability—so I'm adjusting those G_item values to maintain consistency. The challenge is that GSM8K's substantial model and model×item variance makes it harder to hit G≥0.80 even with more items, since you're averaging across many model conditions and can't easily add more models to reduce that error. So the D-study budget might actually need to be higher for GSM8K than HellaSwag despite its higher item_id, because the model-related variance is baked into the design at 4 models and harder to control through item count alone. Now I'm calculating the model×item contribution for each dataset and using those to set reasonable item counts within the 350-650 range—HellaSwag at 530, GSM8K at 555, MATH at 625, ARC at 450, and MMLU at 414. Time to actually write out the YAML configuration.\n\nFor GSM8K, the single-model variance should be around 58% with a cross-model drop of about 26 percentage points down to 31.8%, which aligns with the model×item effect absorbing most of that variance. HellaSwag shows a smaller drop of 17.8 percentage points from 48% to 30.2%, but the model×item variance there is 32.5%—the discrepancy exists because these percentages are relative to total variance, not independent components.\n\nThe key insight is that the denominator changes between single and cross-model analyses since cross-model adds model, model×item, and other interaction terms, so the item percentage naturally shrinks even when absolute item variance stays similar. The numbers are internally consistent enough for this analysis, so I'm moving forward with finalizing the YAML output with the variance decomposition numbers across all benchmarks. Generating variance table... Now I'm compiling the full YAML configuration, starting with the exp-002 single model results for Llama across all benchmarks, where item_id accounts for the majority of variance in each dataset. Now I'm verifying the performance drops when moving from single to cross-item evaluation across all benchmarks, and I'm seeing consistent degradation in the 18-23 percentage point range.\n\nLooking at the ranking, GSM8K and MATH show larger drops than the others, which suggests these benchmarks have stronger model-by-item interactions—models tend to disagree more on which items are difficult.\n\nFor the format analysis, I'm comparing multiple-choice benchmarks against completion-based ones to see if format influences how much item difficulty varies across models.\n\nThe MC format shows a 10.4 percentage point advantage over free-form formats, and this difference is statistically significant. Within each format, there's some gradient—MC benchmarks differ by about 4.6 points while free-form ones differ by 6.3 points—but with only two benchmarks per format, I can't draw firm conclusions about whether the within-format ordering is meaningful.\n\nI have what I need for this experiment now, so I'm moving on to write up the remaining experiments more efficiently.\n\nFor exp-003, I'm planning a multi-hardware test across different GPU architectures to see if the precision separability effect holds consistently. I'd expect hardware interactions to be minimal, with the main finding being that the effect generalizes across different hardware setups.\n\nFor exp-004, I'm building on the completed exp-001 and partially done exp-002 to run a comprehensive analysis with bootstrap confidence intervals and variance component comparisons. The bootstrap resampling will give me uncertainty estimates for each component, and I'll compare REML and Henderson III methods to check if they converge on similar variance decompositions.\n\nNow I'm looking at how different measurement frameworks compare—IRT suggests far fewer items than G-theory for achieving reliability, and I'm seeing that fixing versus randomizing temperature and ordering has minimal impact on the variance structure. When I shift to analyzing log probabilities instead of binary scores, item effects become much more prominent at 50-58%, while model-by-item interactions shrink slightly. My power analysis confirms I have enough statistical power to detect meaningful interaction effects given the scale of the benchmark, and the D-study projections show that even the full MMLU and ARC datasets would yield very high generalizability coefficients.\n\nThe cost implications are substantial though—moving from the current single-prompt, single-seed approach with 200 items to the recommended design with multiple prompts, seeds, and 500 items would increase expenses by 10-30×. For the 14B model experiments, I'm expecting a similar variance pattern to the smaller models, though more capable models might show slightly less item-level variance and greater divergence across architectures on harder items.\n\nThe fine-tuning study would likely reveal a fundamentally different variance structure where seed becomes the dominant factor rather than item selection, with learning rate interactions and data shuffling playing much larger roles. LayerCast's precision format variations appear to have minimal impact on the overall variance decomposition, making it a lower priority. The MATH benchmark work has already been consolidated.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML output to comprehensively document each experiment, including predicted results and table formats. I need to verify the Yi-1.5-9B benchmark numbers and integrate them into the cross-model comparison with the other four models.\n\nWriting YAML configuration... Writing YAML config now...\n\nLet me verify the cross-benchmark item_id percentages are consistent: MMLU around 42% should decrease across ARC, HellaSwag, and GSM8K, and my values (42.1, 37.5, 30.2, 31.8) follow that pattern reasonably well, with GSM8K at the upper boundary of the expected range. Now I'm checking the remaining metrics against their target ranges. For model_x_item_range_pct, my values span from 32.5 to 37.5 percentage points across the benchmarks, which fits within the 35-42 range. The cross-model gradient for MMLU drops from 0.82 to around 0.755 on GSM8K, staying within expectations. I have 5 models and 4 fully completed benchmarks with one partial, which satisfies the completion requirements.\n\nLooking at the precision separability across benchmarks, the values range from less than 0.1% on MMLU and ARC up to 3.2% on GSM8K, all within the 0-5.0% target. The D_study items show a range of 414 to 625, fitting the 350-650 bracket. The format gradient appears weakly preserved as expected, and I'm now checking the final phase completion status.\n\nAll the metrics are validating correctly, so I'm ready to generate the YAML output—this should be quite detailed, probably 400-600 lines given the granularity needed. Before I start writing, let me think through what the precision probe data looks like across the non-MMLU benchmarks. The design uses 2 models across 2 precisions with fixed temperature, 2 prompts, 2 seeds, and a single ordering, giving 16 conditions per benchmark with 200 items each.\n\nLooking at the results, the precision differences vary significantly by benchmark—ARC shows almost no effect at 0.1 percentage points, HellaSwag is minimal at 0.3 percentage points, but GSM8K jumps to 1.7 percentage points because the multi-step reasoning amplifies those small floating-point variations.\n\nThis is a key insight: precision separability actually holds for multiple-choice tasks where the model just picks an option, but breaks down when the model has to generate reasoning chains where each step compounds the differences. MATH wasn't tested due to insufficient models, so GSM8K is the anomaly that reveals this pattern.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML systematically with all the sections I need.\n\nWait, I should clarify one thing about the experimental design: exp-001 already collected MMLU data across multiple precisions and temperatures, so exp-002 can just reuse that BF16 subset rather than recollecting it. The actual new data collection in exp-002 focuses on the other benchmarks with the streamlined precision setup.\n\nNow I'm calculating the GPU hours needed for the new benchmarks. ARC and HellaSwag together would take about 20 hours, GSM8K around 130 hours, and MATH Phase 2 roughly 320 hours—totaling about 470 hours, which exceeds the 300-hour budget. I need to find a way to trim this down.\n\nLooking at the median tier approach, I've already completed ARC and HellaSwag, finished GSM8K, and have MATH halfway done across 4 models. That puts me at about 310 hours total with overhead, slightly over budget, but I'm wondering if MATH might actually run faster with batching on the RTX PRO 6000's 96GB memory.\n\nIf I can push throughput to 75 items per minute instead of 12, each MATH model drops to around 12.8 hours, bringing all four models to about 51 hours total. That would put Phase 1 and 2 at roughly 201 hours combined, leaving about 100 hours for Phase 3 to run Yi and OLMo on some benchmarks. This timeline feels more realistic given what I've already completed.\n\nLooking at what's actually done: Phase 1 and 2 are finished across the four original models, Yi has completed three benchmarks with GSM8K and MATH still pending, and OLMo finished MMLU. I'm at around 246 hours total. Rather than track every partial completion, I should just define a clean completion state for this experiment tier. Now I'm finalizing the numbers for the YAML configuration. Let me break down the record counts precisely: Phase 1 gave us 921,600 records across the four models and benchmarks, Phase 2 added 230,400 more, and Phase 3 is partially complete with Yi covering three benchmarks for 172,800 records and OLMo contributing one benchmark's worth. This puts us at roughly 1.6M total records with Phase 3 sitting around 60% done.\n\nI'm also thinking through how adding more models like Yi and OLMo affects the MMLU analysis—the item_id attribution drops from 42.1% with four models down to around 40.5% with six models since we're spreading the variance across more model-item combinations, and the model-item interaction share lands near 35.2%.\n\nThe D-study projections are holding up well here. The paper predicted G=0.848 for five models, and if I'm seeing actual values around 0.850 with five models and 0.862 with six, that's a solid validation of the methodology. With six models, I'd expect G_item to fall somewhere between the five-model estimate of 0.848 and the eight-model projection of 0.893, so around 0.865 seems reasonable.\n\nI'm going to include this validation in the supplementary analysis for exp-002, then move into writing the full output as YAML. I need to be careful that each experiment's result_rationale clearly explains the reasoning behind the findings.\n\nWriting YAML output... Writing YAML structure...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":30480,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7213f7b-2e18-414c-bcc7-5947fa0bd98a","uuid":"03f79126-1de0-4707-8369-aefc198efff8"}
