{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018hM2WgXQKm8Kx5ABCBjnEF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\ttier: median\n2\tnarrative_guidance: 'Soften \"systematic gradient\" to \"benchmark-conditional variance\n3\t  structure\" or \"task-type clustering.\" Cross-benchmark pattern is 2-cluster ({MMLU,\n4\t  ARC} high item dominance vs {GSM8K, HellaSwag} lower), not a clean monotonic 4-point\n5\t  gradient. Item dominance remains dominant on knowledge benchmarks but magnitude\n6\t  is less precise than MVP suggested once model-as-facet is handled and bootstrap\n7\t  CIs computed. Separability holds on knowledge benchmarks but GSM8K shows infra×method\n8\t  ~3% under binary scoring, requiring benchmark+metric qualification on Claim 2. Bootstrap\n9\t  CIs on gradient endpoints overlap partially — directional claims hold, magnitude\n10\t  claims are imprecise. Reframe hook: \"the variance structure itself depends on what\n11\t  you''re benchmarking\" rather than a clean ordering. FP32 degradation is real (not\n12\t  engine confound), position as known finding (Yuan 2025) with novel item-level G-theory\n13\t  breakdown. Model-as-facet absorption (21% on MMLU) reframes prior single-model studies\n14\t  as having overestimated pure item dominance — turn this into a feature, not a weakness.\n15\t  Target AAAI with adjusted framing; if exp-008 (MATH) is feasible and strengthens\n16\t  the clustering to 3-tier, include it; otherwise ACL ARR October is a comfortable\n17\t  fallback.\n18\t\n19\t  '\n20\timagined_experiments:\n21\t- name: Full-scale MMLU precision study (confound-fixed)\n22\t  plan_id: exp-001\n23\t  description: '4 models × 3 precisions (FP32/BF16/FP16, all V0 enforce_eager) × 6\n24\t    prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items (stratified).\n25\t    1,036,800 total observations.\n26\t\n27\t    '\n28\t  expected_results:\n29\t    total_observations: 1036800\n30\t    total_conditions: 5184\n31\t    accuracy_fp32: 0.655\n32\t    accuracy_bf16: 0.684\n33\t    accuracy_fp16: 0.681\n34\t    accuracy_gap_fp32_bf16_pp: 2.9\n35\t    accuracy_gap_bf16_fp16_pp: 0.3\n36\t    accuracy_by_model:\n37\t      qwen3_8b:\n38\t        fp32: 0.695\n39\t        bf16: 0.71\n40\t        fp16: 0.706\n41\t      llama31_8b:\n42\t        fp32: 0.6\n43\t        bf16: 0.672\n44\t        fp16: 0.669\n45\t      gemma2_9b:\n46\t        fp32: 0.638\n47\t        bf16: 0.66\n48\t        fp16: 0.658\n49\t      mistral_7b:\n50\t        fp32: 0.688\n51\t        bf16: 0.694\n52\t        fp16: 0.691\n53\t    item_id_variance_binary: 0.52\n54\t    residual_variance_binary: 0.14\n55\t    precision_x_item_binary: 0.05\n56\t    prompt_x_item_binary: 0.058\n57\t    temperature_x_item_binary: 0.055\n58\t    seed_x_item_binary: 0.028\n59\t    precision_main_binary: 0.03\n60\t    temperature_main_binary: 0.015\n61\t    prompt_main_binary: 0.008\n62\t    ordering_main_binary: 0.002\n63\t    precision_x_temperature_binary: 0.002\n64\t    precision_x_prompt_binary: 0.001\n65\t    precision_x_seed_binary: 0.001\n66\t    other_binary: 0.09\n67\t    residual_textmatch: 0.36\n68\t    prompt_x_item_textmatch: 0.33\n69\t    temperature_main_textmatch: 0.14\n70\t    precision_x_item_textmatch: 0.04\n71\t    precision_x_temperature_textmatch: 0.022\n72\t    temperature_x_item_textmatch: 0.03\n73\t    item_id_textmatch: 0.03\n74\t    precision_main_textmatch: 0.025\n75\t    other_textmatch: 0.023\n76\t    g_correct_binary: 0.37\n77\t    g_correct_textmatch: 0.97\n78\t    item_id_rank_corr_across_models: 0.82\n79\t    fp32_gap_model_range_pp:\n80\t    - 1.5\n81\t    - 7.2\n82\t  result_rationale: 'V0 enforce_eager for all precisions eliminates the 0.22pp engine\n83\t    confound (bf16_eager_disentangle) but the FP32 accuracy gap PERSISTS at ~2.9pp\n84\t    average — confirming the disentangle conclusion that 97.4% of the gap was genuine\n85\t    precision effect. FP32 degradation is strongly model-dependent: Llama-3.1-8B shows\n86\t    7.2pp gap (consistent with MVP''s 8.5pp on this model) while Qwen3-8B shows only\n87\t    1.5pp, likely because Qwen''s training used mixed-precision strategies that are\n88\t    more robust to FP32 inference rounding. Precision main effect at 3% is real but\n89\t    dwarfed by item_id at 52%. Precision:item at 5% means certain items (those near\n90\t    decision boundaries) are disproportionately affected — this is the novel item-level\n91\t    breakdown that extends Yuan et al. (2025). Item_id at 52% is lower than MVP''s\n92\t    58.4% because 200 stratified items (vs 50) include more mid-difficulty items that\n93\t    reduce between-item variance, and pooling across 4 models absorbs some item-specific\n94\t    variance into residual. Residual at 14% is inflated because model effects are\n95\t    pooled — exp-004 will decompose this. G=0.37 replicates MVP''s 0.357, confirming\n96\t    item dominance makes G≥0.90 unreachable without massive item expansion. Text-match\n97\t    scoring replicates the qualitative shift: prompt:item dominates (33%), precision×temperature\n98\t    at 2.2% (consistent with MVP''s 1.3% but larger due to more conditions), driven\n99\t    by floating-point non-associativity in generation.\n100\t\n101\t    '\n102\t  tables:\n103\t  - name: 'Table 1: MMLU Variance Decomposition — Binary Correct (Basic, Model Pooled)'\n104\t    columns:\n105\t    - Source\n106\t    - '% Total Variance'\n107\t    rows:\n108\t    - - item_id\n109\t      - 52.0%\n110\t    - - residual (includes model effects)\n111\t      - 14.0%\n112\t    - - prompt:item\n113\t      - 5.8%\n114\t    - - temperature:item\n115\t      - 5.5%\n116\t    - - precision:item\n117\t      - 5.0%\n118\t    - - precision (main)\n119\t      - 3.0%\n120\t    - - seed:item\n121\t      - 2.8%\n122\t    - - temperature (main)\n123\t      - 1.5%\n124\t    - - prompt (main)\n125\t      - 0.8%\n126\t    - - infra×method (all)\n127\t      - 0.4%\n128\t    - - ordering\n129\t      - 0.2%\n130\t    - - other 2-way / 3-way\n131\t      - 9.0%\n132\t  - name: 'Table 2: MMLU Variance Decomposition — Text Exact Match (Basic)'\n133\t    columns:\n134\t    - Source\n135\t    - '% Total Variance'\n136\t    rows:\n137\t    - - residual\n138\t      - 36.0%\n139\t    - - prompt:item\n140\t      - 33.0%\n141\t    - - temperature (main)\n142\t      - 14.0%\n143\t    - - precision:item\n144\t      - 4.0%\n145\t    - - temperature:item\n146\t      - 3.0%\n147\t    - - item_id\n148\t      - 3.0%\n149\t    - - precision (main)\n150\t      - 2.5%\n151\t    - - precision×temperature\n152\t      - 2.2%\n153\t    - - other\n154\t      - 2.3%\n155\t  - name: 'Table 3: Per-Model Accuracy by Precision (V0 enforce_eager)'\n156\t    columns:\n157\t    - Model\n158\t    - FP32\n159\t    - BF16\n160\t    - FP16\n161\t    - FP32-BF16 Gap\n162\t    rows:\n163\t    - - Qwen3-8B-Instruct\n164\t      - '0.695'\n165\t      - '0.710'\n166\t      - '0.706'\n167\t      - 1.5pp\n168\t    - - Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\n169\t      - '0.600'\n170\t      - '0.672'\n171\t      - '0.669'\n172\t      - 7.2pp\n173\t    - - Gemma-2-9B-IT\n174\t      - '0.638'\n175\t      - '0.660'\n176\t      - '0.658'\n177\t      - 2.2pp\n178\t    - - Mistral-7B-v0.3\n179\t      - '0.688'\n180\t      - '0.694'\n181\t      - '0.691'\n182\t      - 0.6pp\n183\t    - - Average\n184\t      - '0.655'\n185\t      - '0.684'\n186\t      - '0.681'\n187\t      - 2.9pp\n188\t  figures_description:\n189\t  - 'Stacked bar: binary vs text-match variance decomposition side by side, showing\n190\t    metric-conditional shift from item-dominated (binary) to prompt-dominated (text-match).'\n191\t  - 'Heatmap: FP32 degradation (pp) by model × item difficulty quartile, showing model-dependent\n192\t    and difficulty-dependent precision sensitivity.'\n193\t  - 'Scatter: item-level accuracy variance vs item difficulty, colored by MMLU subject\n194\t    category.'\n195\t  analysis: 'Key finding: FP32 degradation is REAL, not an engine confound. The bf16_eager_disentangle\n196\t    result is confirmed at scale — V0 enforce_eager changes nothing substantial. This\n197\t    reframes the contribution: we provide the first item-level G-theory decomposition\n198\t    of precision effects, showing that precision:item (5%) exceeds precision main\n199\t    (3%). The model-dependence of FP32 degradation (1.5-7.2pp) is novel and suggests\n200\t    training-time precision choices affect inference robustness. For the paper: position\n201\t    FP32 degradation as supporting Yuan et al. (2025) with novel item×model granularity,\n202\t    NOT as a primary finding. Separability on MMLU is confirmed: infra×method = 0.4%\n203\t    binary, 2.2% text-match (precision×temperature only). CIs deferred to exp-004.\n204\t\n205\t    '\n206\t- name: Item resampling stability test\n207\t  plan_id: exp-001b\n208\t  description: '3 independent stratified samples of 200 MMLU items (seeds 0, 7, 99)\n209\t    × Llama-3.1-8B × 3 precisions × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 54 conditions/sample,\n210\t    32,400 obs/sample.\n211\t\n212\t    '\n213\t  expected_results:\n214\t    item_id_sample_0: 0.49\n215\t    item_id_sample_7: 0.55\n216\t    item_id_sample_99: 0.44\n217\t    item_id_mean: 0.493\n218\t    item_id_sd: 0.055\n219\t    item_id_cv: 0.19\n220\t    g_coeff_sample_0: 0.36\n221\t    g_coeff_sample_7: 0.32\n222\t    g_coeff_sample_99: 0.39\n223\t    accuracy_sample_0: 0.668\n224\t    accuracy_sample_7: 0.641\n225\t    accuracy_sample_99: 0.679\n226\t    mean_item_difficulty_sample_0: 0.5\n227\t    mean_item_difficulty_sample_7: 0.42\n228\t    mean_item_difficulty_sample_99: 0.54\n229\t    sd_item_difficulty_sample_0: 0.28\n230\t    sd_item_difficulty_sample_7: 0.32\n231\t    sd_item_difficulty_sample_99: 0.25\n232\t    rank_order_top3_sources_preserved: true\n233\t    residual_cv: 0.11\n234\t    prompt_item_cv: 0.09\n235\t  result_rationale: 'CV of 19% reflects meaningful item-sampling uncertainty. MMLU\n236\t    has 14,042 items across 57 subjects with heterogeneous difficulty; stratified\n237\t    sampling with 200 items (1.4%) leaves substantial sampling variance especially\n238\t    in the tails. Sample 7 draws more hard items (mean difficulty 0.42), inflating\n239\t    between-item variance to 55% because the difficulty spread is wider (SD 0.32).\n240\t    Sample 99 draws more mid-difficulty items (mean 0.54, SD 0.25), compressing item_id\n241\t    to 44% because items cluster around the center. Despite magnitude variation, the\n242\t    qualitative hierarchy is preserved: item_id ranks #1 in all 3 samples, residual\n243\t    #2, prompt:item #3. This stability-in-rank with instability-in-magnitude is the\n244\t    key finding: the headline claim \"item dominance ~50%\" needs error bars, reported\n245\t    as \"44-55% across independent 200-item stratified draws.\" The G-coefficient varies\n246\t    inversely (0.32-0.39) because higher item variance = lower generalizability of\n247\t    the benchmark score. This directly motivates D-study item-count recommendations\n248\t    with CIs, not point estimates — if your headline variance component shifts ±5pp\n249\t    with a different item draw, your optimal resource allocation shifts too.\n250\t\n251\t    '\n252\t  tables:\n253\t  - name: 'Table 4: Resampling Stability of Variance Components'\n254\t    columns:\n255\t    - Source\n256\t    - Sample 0\n257\t    - Sample 7\n258\t    - Sample 99\n259\t    - Mean\n260\t    - CV\n261\t    rows:\n262\t    - - item_id\n263\t      - 49.0%\n264\t      - 55.0%\n265\t      - 44.0%\n266\t      - 49.3%\n267\t      - '0.19'\n268\t    - - residual\n269\t      - 17.5%\n270\t      - 15.0%\n271\t      - 19.5%\n272\t      - 17.3%\n273\t      - '0.11'\n274\t    - - prompt:item\n275\t      - 6.5%\n276\t      - 6.0%\n277\t      - 7.0%\n278\t      - 6.5%\n279\t      - '0.09'\n280\t    - - temp:item\n281\t      - 5.8%\n282\t      - 5.2%\n283\t      - 6.5%\n284\t      - 5.8%\n285\t      - '0.10'\n286\t    - - prec:item\n287\t      - 5.0%\n288\t      - 5.5%\n289\t      - 4.2%\n290\t      - 4.9%\n291\t      - '0.12'\n292\t    - - precision\n293\t      - 3.0%\n294\t      - 3.5%\n295\t      - 2.8%\n296\t      - 3.1%\n297\t      - '0.10'\n298\t    - - other\n299\t      - 13.2%\n300\t      - 9.8%\n301\t      - 16.0%\n302\t      - 13.0%\n303\t      - —\n304\t  - name: 'Table 5: Per-Sample Summary Statistics'\n305\t    columns:\n306\t    - Metric\n307\t    - Sample 0\n308\t    - Sample 7\n309\t    - Sample 99\n310\t    rows:\n311\t    - - N items\n312\t      - '200'\n313\t      - '200'\n314\t      - '200'\n315\t    - - Mean item difficulty\n316\t      - '0.50'\n317\t      - '0.42'\n318\t      - '0.54'\n319\t    - - SD item difficulty\n320\t      - '0.28'\n321\t      - '0.32'\n322\t      - '0.25'\n323\t    - - Overall accuracy\n324\t      - '0.668'\n325\t      - '0.641'\n326\t      - '0.679'\n327\t    - - G (binary)\n328\t      - '0.36'\n329\t      - '0.32'\n330\t      - '0.39'\n331\t    - - item_id %\n332\t      - 49.0%\n333\t      - 55.0%\n334\t      - 44.0%\n335\t  figures_description:\n336\t  - 'Forest plot: item_id % with bootstrap 95% CIs across 3 resamples, showing overlapping\n337\t    but distinct intervals. Horizontal shaded band marks the MVP point estimate (58.4%)\n338\t    for comparison.'\n339\t  - 'Histogram overlay: item difficulty distribution for each sample, showing how\n340\t    stratification seed shifts the difficulty profile despite identical stratification\n341\t    ratios.'\n342\t  analysis: 'The 19% CV is the paper''s best defense against \"you just got lucky with\n343\t    your items.\" It quantifies the item-sampling uncertainty that plagues every single-draw\n344\t    benchmark study. Rank ordering is robust (item_id always #1) but precise magnitude\n345\t    depends on which items you drew — explaining why Mesting (2026) and Brittlebench\n346\t    (2026) reached different headline numbers on different item sets. For the paper:\n347\t    always report item_id as a range (44-55%) not a point. D-study projections from\n348\t    exp-004 should include CIs that account for this resampling instability.\n349\t\n350\t    '\n351\t- name: Multi-benchmark replication (GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag)\n352\t  plan_id: exp-002\n353\t  description: '4 models × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures\n354\t    × 4 orderings × 200 items on GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Reduced crossing\n355\t    vs exp-001.\n356\t\n357\t    '\n358\t  expected_results:\n359\t    total_observations_per_benchmark: 384000\n360\t    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.575\n361\t    gsm8k_item_id_binary: 0.4\n362\t    gsm8k_residual_binary: 0.15\n363\t    gsm8k_temp_x_item_binary: 0.1\n364\t    gsm8k_seed_x_item_binary: 0.07\n365\t    gsm8k_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.065\n366\t    gsm8k_temperature_binary: 0.04\n367\t    gsm8k_prec_x_temp_binary: 0.022\n368\t    gsm8k_prec_x_seed_binary: 0.005\n369\t    gsm8k_prec_x_prompt_binary: 0.003\n370\t    gsm8k_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.03\n371\t    gsm8k_precision_main_binary: 0.01\n372\t    gsm8k_prec_x_item_binary: 0.015\n373\t    gsm8k_other_binary: 0.093\n374\t    gsm8k_g_correct_binary: 0.42\n375\t    arc_accuracy: 0.625\n376\t    arc_item_id_binary: 0.5\n377\t    arc_residual_binary: 0.12\n378\t    arc_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.085\n379\t    arc_temp_x_item_binary: 0.055\n380\t    arc_seed_x_item_binary: 0.04\n381\t    arc_prec_x_item_binary: 0.025\n382\t    arc_temperature_binary: 0.025\n383\t    arc_precision_binary: 0.01\n384\t    arc_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.005\n385\t    arc_other_binary: 0.115\n386\t    arc_g_correct_binary: 0.35\n387\t    hellaswag_accuracy: 0.712\n388\t    hellaswag_item_id_binary: 0.35\n389\t    hellaswag_residual_binary: 0.18\n390\t    hellaswag_temp_x_item_binary: 0.095\n391\t    hellaswag_seed_x_item_binary: 0.085\n392\t    hellaswag_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.075\n393\t    hellaswag_temperature_binary: 0.045\n394\t    hellaswag_prec_x_item_binary: 0.015\n395\t    hellaswag_precision_binary: 0.005\n396\t    hellaswag_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.003\n397\t    hellaswag_other_binary: 0.127\n398\t    hellaswag_g_correct_binary: 0.48\n399\t    gradient_pattern: '2-cluster: {MMLU 52%, ARC 50%} vs {GSM8K 40%, HellaSwag 35%}'\n400\t    gradient_spread_pp: 17\n401\t    within_cluster_ordering_reliable: false\n402\t    gsm8k_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.035\n403\t    arc_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.022\n404\t    hellaswag_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.016\n405\t    infra_x_method_textmatch_max: 0.035\n406\t  result_rationale: 'The 2-cluster pattern emerges because variance structure tracks\n407\t    cognitive demand, not benchmark labels. Knowledge-retrieval benchmarks (MMLU,\n408\t    ARC) show high item dominance (50-52% basic) because item difficulty is the primary\n409\t    driver — items test factual knowledge that is either present or absent in the\n410\t    model, and this binary state dominates across conditions. ARC clusters with MMLU\n411\t    despite being labeled \"science reasoning\" because most ARC items are effectively\n412\t    factual recall (what happens when ice melts?) rather than multi-step inference.\n413\t    Reasoning benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag) show lower item dominance (35-40%) because\n414\t    temperature and seed interact strongly with items: multi-step reasoning and commonsense\n415\t    inference have multiple valid processing paths, and sampling randomness determines\n416\t    which path is taken, shifting variance from item_id to interaction terms. GSM8K\n417\t    item dominance at 40% is above the hypothesized 20-30% because even math reasoning\n418\t    has trivially easy items (single-step arithmetic) and impossible items (complex\n419\t    multi-step for 8B models), creating a floor of item-driven variance. The critical\n420\t    median-tier finding: GSM8K shows infra×method = 3.0% under binary scoring (precision×temperature\n421\t    2.2% + precision×seed 0.5% + precision×prompt 0.3%). This breaks universal separability.\n422\t    The mechanism: math generation produces longer chains-of-thought where BF16 vs\n423\t    FP16 accumulation differences compound; temperature controls how aggressively\n424\t    alternative reasoning paths are explored; the precision×temperature combination\n425\t    can push probability distributions past decision boundaries, flipping the final\n426\t    numerical answer. Binary scoring captures this because the extracted number differs.\n427\t    Other benchmarks show infra×method <0.5% binary — the effect is GSM8K-specific.\n428\t    Under text-match, GSM8K prec×temp reaches 3.5% because exact-string comparison\n429\t    of multi-step outputs amplifies floating-point divergence. The gradient spread\n430\t    of 17pp (MMLU 52% vs HellaSwag 35%, basic) is within the target range [10-24pp];\n431\t    after model-as-facet correction in exp-004 it will be similar (48% vs 31% = 17pp).\n432\t\n433\t    '\n434\t  tables:\n435\t  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition — Binary (Basic, Model\n436\t      Pooled)'\n437\t    columns:\n438\t    - Source\n439\t    - MMLU\n440\t    - GSM8K\n441\t    - ARC\n442\t    - HellaSwag\n443\t    rows:\n444\t    - - item_id\n445\t      - 52.0%\n446\t      - 40.0%\n447\t      - 50.0%\n448\t      - 35.0%\n449\t    - - residual\n450\t      - 14.0%\n451\t      - 15.0%\n452\t      - 12.0%\n453\t      - 18.0%\n454\t    - - temp:item\n455\t      - 5.5%\n456\t      - 10.0%\n457\t      - 5.5%\n458\t      - 9.5%\n459\t    - - seed:item\n460\t      - 2.8%\n461\t      - 7.0%\n462\t      - 4.0%\n463\t      - 8.5%\n464\t    - - prompt:item\n465\t      - 5.8%\n466\t      - 6.5%\n467\t      - 8.5%\n468\t      - 7.5%\n469\t    - - prec:item\n470\t      - 5.0%\n471\t      - 1.5%\n472\t      - 2.5%\n473\t      - 1.5%\n474\t    - - precision (main)\n475\t      - 3.0%\n476\t      - 1.0%\n477\t      - 1.0%\n478\t      - 0.5%\n479\t    - - temperature (main)\n480\t      - 1.5%\n481\t      - 4.0%\n482\t      - 2.5%\n483\t      - 4.5%\n484\t    - - infra×method (total)\n485\t      - 0.4%\n486\t      - 3.0%\n487\t      - 0.5%\n488\t      - 0.3%\n489\t    - - other\n490\t      - 10.0%\n491\t      - 12.0%\n492\t      - 13.5%\n493\t      - 14.7%\n494\t  - name: 'Table 7: Cross-Benchmark Summary (Basic)'\n495\t    columns:\n496\t    - Benchmark\n497\t    - Task Type\n498\t    - item_id %\n499\t    - G (binary)\n500\t    - infra×method\n501\t    - Cluster\n502\t    rows:\n503\t    - - MMLU\n504\t      - Knowledge\n505\t      - 52.0%\n506\t      - '0.37'\n507\t      - 0.4%\n508\t      - High\n509\t    - - ARC-Challenge\n510\t      - Knowledge/Science\n511\t      - 50.0%\n512\t      - '0.35'\n513\t      - 0.5%\n514\t      - High\n515\t    - - GSM8K\n516\t      - Math reasoning\n517\t      - 40.0%\n518\t      - '0.42'\n519\t      - 3.0%\n520\t      - Low\n521\t    - - HellaSwag\n522\t      - Commonsense\n523\t      - 35.0%\n524\t      - '0.48'\n525\t      - 0.3%\n526\t      - Low\n527\t  - name: 'Table 8: GSM8K Infra×Method Interaction Breakdown (Binary)'\n528\t    columns:\n529\t    - Interaction\n530\t    - '% Total Variance'\n531\t    - 95% CI (bootstrap, exp-004)\n532\t    rows:\n533\t    - - precision×temperature\n534\t      - 2.2%\n535\t      - deferred\n536\t    - - precision×seed\n537\t      - 0.5%\n538\t      - deferred\n539\t    - - precision×prompt\n540\t      - 0.3%\n541\t      - deferred\n542\t    - - Total infra×method\n543\t      - 3.0%\n544\t      - deferred\n545\t    note: Only GSM8K exceeds 1% threshold. MMLU 0.4%, ARC 0.5%, HellaSwag 0.3%.\n546\t  figures_description:\n547\t  - 'Stacked bar (signature figure): 4-benchmark variance decomposition, bars sorted\n548\t    by item_id descending, showing 2-cluster visual. Item_id bars in dark blue, interaction\n549\t    terms in lighter shades, precision/temperature main in warm tones.'\n550\t  - 'Scatter: item_id % vs benchmark task-type ordinal (knowledge → reasoning), with\n551\t    error bars from exp-001b resampling on MMLU. Shows cluster structure, not monotonic\n552\t    trend.'\n553\t  - 'Heatmap: pairwise Spearman correlation of per-item variance profiles across 4\n554\t    benchmarks, showing r>0.7 within clusters and r<0.3 between clusters.'\n555\t  analysis: 'This is the core finding. The 2-cluster pattern is clear and the direction\n556\t    is robust, but it is NOT the clean monotonic gradient originally hypothesized.\n557\t    ARC clusters with MMLU (not intermediate) — this is actually a stronger finding\n558\t    than a gradient: variance structure tracks cognitive demand, and ARC''s \"science\n559\t    reasoning\" label is misleading. The GSM8K infra×method at 3% binary is the median\n560\t    tier''s key complication for Claim 2: separability is benchmark-conditional, not\n561\t    universal. The paper must add \"on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks\" or \"when math\n562\t    reasoning is excluded\" to the separability claim. Under text-match, GSM8K reaches\n563\t    3.5% (within target [1.5%, 4.5%]). Practical implication: practitioners evaluating\n564\t    math reasoning on GSM8K should fix both precision AND temperature to minimize\n565\t    infra×method confounding.\n566\t\n567\t    '\n568\t- name: Multi-hardware G-study\n569\t  plan_id: exp-003\n570\t  description: '4 models × 2 GPU types (RTX PRO 6000 + second GPU) × 2 precisions\n571\t    (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items.\n572\t\n573\t    '\n574\t  expected_results:\n575\t    total_observations: 1152000\n576\t    hardware_main_binary: 0.004\n577\t    hardware_x_precision_binary: 0.008\n578\t    hardware_x_temperature_binary: 0.003\n579\t    hardware_x_seed_binary: 0.002\n580\t    hardware_x_prompt_binary: 0.001\n581\t    hardware_x_methodology_total_binary: 0.014\n582\t    hardware_x_item_binary: 0.018\n583\t    total_hardware_related_binary: 0.036\n584\t    hardware_main_textmatch: 0.012\n585\t    hardware_x_precision_textmatch: 0.022\n586\t    hardware_x_methodology_total_textmatch: 0.032\n587\t    hardware_x_item_textmatch: 0.028\n588\t    total_hardware_related_textmatch: 0.072\n589\t    item_id_with_hardware_binary: 0.5\n590\t    g_correct_binary_gpu1: 0.37\n591\t    g_correct_binary_gpu2: 0.36\n592\t    cross_gpu_gap_bf16_mean_pp: 0.12\n593\t    cross_gpu_gap_fp16_mean_pp: 0.75\n594\t    cross_gpu_gap_bf16_max_pp: 0.2\n595\t    cross_gpu_gap_fp16_max_pp: 1.1\n596\t    model_ranking_preserved_across_gpus: true\n597\t    hardware_variance_fp16_subset: 0.022\n598\t    hardware_variance_bf16_subset: 0.003\n599\t  result_rationale: 'Hardware main effect at 0.4% binary is negligible because both\n600\t    GPUs implement the same IEEE 754 operations; differences arise only from CUDA\n601\t    kernel scheduling nondeterminism and memory subsystem timing. The critical finding\n602\t    is hardware×precision at 0.8% binary: FP16 is more hardware-sensitive than BF16\n603\t    (cross-GPU gap 0.75pp vs 0.12pp), because FP16''s narrower dynamic range (5-bit\n604\t    exponent vs BF16''s 8-bit) makes accumulation order hardware-dependent — consistent\n605\t    with Yuan et al. (2025). When subsetting to FP16-only data, hardware explains\n606\t    2.2% of variance (within median narrative''s \"1-3% on one precision\"). Under text-match,\n607\t    hardware effects roughly double (total 7.2%) because different GPU kernel orderings\n608\t    produce different final tokens in long generations, amplified by exact-match scoring.\n609\t    Hardware×item at 1.8% binary means certain items near decision boundaries are\n610\t    hardware-sensitive — the same items that are precision-sensitive (prec:item) are\n611\t    also hardware-sensitive, consistent with a shared mechanism of logit perturbation\n612\t    near thresholds. Model rankings are fully preserved across GPUs (Kendall tau =\n613\t    1.0 at all condition combinations) — hardware effects are far too small to alter\n614\t    comparative model evaluation. Total hardware-related variance at 3.6% binary is\n615\t    manageable but nonzero, requiring \"approximately separable\" language.\n616\t\n617\t    '\n618\t  tables:\n619\t  - name: 'Table 9: Hardware Facet Variance Components'\n620\t    columns:\n621\t    - Source\n622\t    - Binary %\n623\t    - Text-match %\n624\t    rows:\n625\t    - - hardware (main)\n626\t      - 0.4%\n627\t      - 1.2%\n628\t    - - hardware×precision\n629\t      - 0.8%\n630\t      - 2.2%\n631\t    - - hardware×temperature\n632\t      - 0.3%\n633\t      - 0.6%\n634\t    - - hardware×seed\n635\t      - 0.2%\n636\t      - 0.4%\n637\t    - - hardware×prompt\n638\t      - 0.1%\n639\t      - 0.3%\n640\t    - - hardware×item\n641\t      - 1.8%\n642\t      - 2.8%\n643\t    - - Total hardware-related\n644\t      - 3.6%\n645\t      - 7.5%\n646\t  - name: 'Table 10: Cross-GPU Accuracy by Model and Precision'\n647\t    columns:\n648\t    - Model\n649\t    - GPU1-BF16\n650\t    - GPU2-BF16\n651\t    - Δ(pp)\n652\t    - GPU1-FP16\n653\t    - GPU2-FP16\n654\t    - Δ(pp)\n655\t    rows:\n656\t    - - Qwen3-8B\n657\t      - '0.710'\n658\t      - '0.709'\n659\t      - '0.1'\n660\t      - '0.706'\n661\t      - '0.700'\n662\t      - '0.6'\n663\t    - - Llama-3.1-8B\n664\t      - '0.672'\n665\t      - '0.672'\n666\t      - '0.0'\n667\t      - '0.669'\n668\t      - '0.661'\n669\t      - '0.8'\n670\t    - - Gemma-2-9B\n671\t      - '0.660'\n672\t      - '0.659'\n673\t      - '0.1'\n674\t      - '0.658'\n675\t      - '0.649'\n676\t      - '0.9'\n677\t    - - Mistral-7B\n678\t      - '0.694'\n679\t      - '0.693'\n680\t      - '0.1'\n681\t      - '0.691'\n682\t      - '0.680'\n683\t      - '1.1'\n684\t  - name: 'Table 11: Hardware Effect Conditioned on Precision'\n685\t    columns:\n686\t    - Metric\n687\t    - BF16 Only\n688\t    - FP16 Only\n689\t    rows:\n690\t    - - Hardware % total variance\n691\t      - 0.3%\n692\t      - 2.2%\n693\t    - - Mean cross-GPU gap (pp)\n694\t      - '0.12'\n695\t      - '0.75'\n696\t    - - Max cross-GPU gap (pp)\n697\t      - '0.2'\n698\t      - '1.1'\n699\t  figures_description:\n700\t  - 'Paired dot plot: per-model accuracy on GPU1 vs GPU2, BF16 (nearly overlapping)\n701\t    vs FP16 (visible gap), connected by lines to show model-specific hardware sensitivity.'\n702\t  - 'Bar chart: hardware-related variance by scoring metric, showing binary→text-match\n703\t    doubling.'\n704\t  analysis: 'Hardware is a manageable confound, not a showstopper. Separability claim\n705\t    (Claim 2) survives with qualification: \"hardware×methodology is ≤1.4% under binary\n706\t    scoring\" — but the FP16 hardware sensitivity (2.2% when conditioned on FP16) warrants\n707\t    a Discussion paragraph. Practical recommendation: prefer BF16 for hardware-independent\n708\t    reproducibility; if FP16 is necessary for memory, acknowledge ~1pp cross-GPU uncertainty.\n709\t    The hardware×item overlap with precision×item suggests a unified \"logit perturbation\"\n710\t    mechanism — items near decision boundaries are vulnerable to both precision and\n711\t    hardware nondeterminism. This is a potential Discussion point but not a formal\n712\t    claim.\n713\t\n714\t    '\n715\t- name: Comprehensive combined analysis\n716\t  plan_id: exp-004\n717\t  description: 'Reanalysis of all exp-001/001b/002/003 data: (a) model-as-facet G-theory,\n718\t    (b) bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples), (c) D-study projections, (d) REML vs Henderson,\n719\t    (e) logprob scoring, (f) ranking stability, (g) higher-order interactions.\n720\t\n721\t    '\n722\t  expected_results:\n723\t    mmlu_item_id_model_facet: 0.48\n724\t    mmlu_model_main: 0.05\n725\t    mmlu_model_x_item: 0.21\n726\t    mmlu_residual_model_facet: 0.08\n727\t    mmlu_prompt_item_model_facet: 0.053\n728\t    mmlu_temp_item_model_facet: 0.048\n729\t    mmlu_prec_item_model_facet: 0.028\n730\t    mmlu_precision_main_model_facet: 0.025\n731\t    mmlu_temperature_main_model_facet: 0.012\n732\t    gsm8k_item_id_model_facet: 0.36\n733\t    gsm8k_model_x_item: 0.18\n734\t    gsm8k_model_main: 0.04\n735\t    arc_item_id_model_facet: 0.46\n736\t    arc_model_x_item: 0.16\n737\t    arc_model_main: 0.04\n738\t    hellaswag_item_id_model_facet: 0.31\n739\t    hellaswag_model_x_item: 0.19\n740\t    hellaswag_model_main: 0.05\n741\t    item_id_reduction_mmlu_pp: 4.0\n742\t    item_id_reduction_gsm8k_pp: 4.0\n743\t    item_id_reduction_arc_pp: 4.0\n744\t    item_id_reduction_hellaswag_pp: 4.0\n745\t    mmlu_item_id_ci:\n746\t    - 0.41\n747\t    - 0.55\n748\t    mmlu_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n749\t    gsm8k_item_id_ci:\n750\t    - 0.29\n751\t    - 0.43\n752\t    gsm8k_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n753\t    arc_item_id_ci:\n754\t    - 0.39\n755\t    - 0.53\n756\t    arc_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n757\t    hellaswag_item_id_ci:\n758\t    - 0.24\n759\t    - 0.38\n760\t    hellaswag_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n761\t    gradient_high_endpoint_ci:\n762\t    - 0.41\n763\t    - 0.55\n764\t    gradient_low_endpoint_ci:\n765\t    - 0.24\n766\t    - 0.38\n767\t    gradient_endpoint_overlap: true\n768\t    gradient_overlap_range:\n769\t    - 0.38\n770\t    - 0.41\n771\t    gradient_direction_p_value: 0.003\n772\t    gsm8k_infra_x_method_ci:\n773\t    - 0.012\n774\t    - 0.048\n775\t    mmlu_infra_x_method_ci:\n776\t    - 0.0\n777\t    - 0.012\n778\t    mmlu_model_x_item_ci:\n779\t    - 0.16\n780\t    - 0.27\n781\t    dstudy_items_g80_mmlu: 220\n782\t    dstudy_items_g90_mmlu: 420\n783\t    dstudy_items_g95_mmlu: 680\n784\t    dstudy_items_g80_gsm8k: 150\n785\t    dstudy_items_g90_gsm8k: 280\n786\t    dstudy_items_g95_gsm8k: 450\n787\t    dstudy_items_g80_arc: 200\n788\t    dstudy_items_g90_arc: 380\n789\t    dstudy_items_g95_arc: 620\n790\t    dstudy_items_g80_hellaswag: 130\n791\t    dstudy_items_g90_hellaswag: 240\n792\t    dstudy_items_g95_hellaswag: 400\n793\t    dstudy_optimal_prompts_mmlu: 3\n794\t    dstudy_optimal_seeds_mmlu: 4\n795\t    dstudy_optimal_prompts_gsm8k: 4\n796\t    dstudy_optimal_seeds_gsm8k: 6\n797\t    dstudy_items_g90_ci_mmlu:\n798\t    - 350\n799\t    - 510\n800\t    dstudy_items_g90_ci_gsm8k:\n801\t    - 220\n802\t    - 360\n803\t    dstudy_ratio_mmlu_vs_hellaswag: 1.75\n804\t    dstudy_ratio_mmlu_vs_gsm8k: 1.5\n805\t    reml_henderson_max_diff_pp: 2.1\n806\t    reml_henderson_rank_preserved: true\n807\t    mmlu_item_id_logprob: 0.55\n808\t    mmlu_g_logprob: 0.32\n809\t    gsm8k_item_id_logprob: 0.42\n810\t    model_rank_flip_pct_across_conditions: 0.12\n811\t    rank_flip_driver: temperature=1.0\n812\t    precision_causes_rank_flip: false\n813\t    three_way_interactions_total_mmlu: 0.03\n814\t    three_way_interactions_total_gsm8k: 0.05\n815\t  result_rationale: 'Model-as-facet is the most consequential reanalysis. On MMLU,\n816\t    21% of total variance is model×item — this is not \"model difficulty\" but model-SPECIFIC\n817\t    item difficulty: items that are hard for Llama but easy for Qwen due to model-specific\n818\t    knowledge gaps and training data overlap. Absorbing this from item_id (52%→48%)\n819\t    and residual (14%→8%) properly attributes variance that prior single-model studies\n820\t    would have assigned to \"item difficulty.\" The 4pp reduction is consistent across\n821\t    all benchmarks, suggesting the model-facet correction is systematic rather than\n822\t    benchmark-specific. Bootstrap CI width of 0.14 on item_id reflects both item-resampling\n823\t    instability (exp-001b CV=19%) and condition-resampling instability. The gradient\n824\t    endpoint CIs overlap narrowly: MMLU lower CI (0.41) exceeds HellaSwag upper CI\n825\t    (0.38) by only 3pp, so the gradient direction is statistically significant (p=0.003\n826\t    by permutation test) but the magnitude is imprecise — the paper should say \"10-30pp\n827\t    spread\" not \"17pp.\" D-study projections differentiate benchmarks: MMLU needs 420\n828\t    items for G≥0.90 vs HellaSwag 240, a 1.75× ratio. This is directionally useful\n829\t    but less dramatic than a clean gradient would predict — the practical message\n830\t    is \"50-75% more items for knowledge vs reasoning benchmarks,\" not \"3× more items.\"\n831\t    D-study CIs on MMLU G≥0.90 ([350, 510]) show that even the recommended item count\n832\t    is imprecise. REML and Henderson agree to within 2.1pp — methodological choice\n833\t    doesn''t affect qualitative conclusions. Logprob scoring increases item_id to\n834\t    55% on MMLU because continuous logprobs capture fine-grained difficulty more sensitively\n835\t    than binary 0/1. Model rank flips occur in 12% of condition pairs, driven entirely\n836\t    by temperature=1.0 (high-temperature conditions produce enough noise to flip rankings\n837\t    of close models); precision never causes rank flips. Three-way interactions total\n838\t    3-5% — most variance is captured by main effects and 2-way terms.\n839\t\n840\t    '\n841\t  tables:\n842\t  - name: 'Table 12: Model-as-Facet Reanalysis — All Benchmarks (Binary)'\n843\t    columns:\n844\t    - Source\n845\t    - MMLU\n846\t    - GSM8K\n847\t    - ARC\n848\t    - HellaSwag\n849\t    rows:\n850\t    - - item_id\n851\t      - 48.0%\n852\t      - 36.0%\n853\t      - 46.0%\n854\t      - 31.0%\n855\t    - - model×item\n856\t      - 21.0%\n857\t      - 18.0%\n858\t      - 16.0%\n859\t      - 19.0%\n860\t    - - model (main)\n861\t      - 5.0%\n862\t      - 4.0%\n863\t      - 4.0%\n864\t      - 5.0%\n865\t    - - residual\n866\t      - 8.0%\n867\t      - 10.0%\n868\t      - 8.0%\n869\t      - 10.0%\n870\t    - - temp:item\n871\t      - 4.8%\n872\t      - 9.0%\n873\t      - 5.0%\n874\t      - 8.5%\n875\t    - - seed:item\n876\t      - 2.0%\n877\t      - 5.5%\n878\t      - 3.0%\n879\t      - 7.0%\n880\t    - - prompt:item\n881\t      - 5.3%\n882\t      - 5.5%\n883\t      - 7.5%\n884\t      - 6.5%\n885\t    - - prec:item\n886\t      - 2.8%\n887\t      - 1.2%\n888\t      - 2.0%\n889\t      - 1.2%\n890\t    - - precision (main)\n891\t      - 2.5%\n892\t      - 0.8%\n893\t      - 0.8%\n894\t      - 0.3%\n895\t    - - temperature (main)\n896\t      - 1.2%\n897\t      - 3.5%\n898\t      - 2.2%\n899\t      - 4.0%\n900\t    - - infra×method\n901\t      - 0.4%\n902\t      - 3.0%\n903\t      - 0.5%\n904\t      - 0.3%\n905\t    - - other\n906\t      - 0.0%\n907\t      - 3.5%\n908\t      - 5.0%\n909\t      - 7.2%\n910\t    note: Rows may not sum to 100% due to rounding and higher-order terms in 'other.'\n911\t  - name: 'Table 13: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Key Variance Components'\n912\t    columns:\n913\t    - Component\n914\t    - MMLU\n915\t    - GSM8K\n916\t    - ARC\n917\t    - HellaSwag\n918\t    rows:\n919\t    - - item_id\n920\t      - '[0.41, 0.55]'\n921\t      - '[0.29, 0.43]'\n922\t      - '[0.39, 0.53]'\n923\t      - '[0.24, 0.38]'\n924\t    - - model×item\n925\t      - '[0.16, 0.27]'\n926\t      - '[0.13, 0.24]'\n927\t      - '[0.11, 0.22]'\n928\t      - '[0.14, 0.25]'\n929\t    - - infra×method\n930\t      - '[0.00, 0.012]'\n931\t      - '[0.012, 0.048]'\n932\t      - '[0.00, 0.014]'\n933\t      - '[0.00, 0.010]'\n934\t    note: 1000 bootstrap resamples (items × conditions). GSM8K infra×method CI excludes\n935\t      0.\n936\t  - name: 'Table 14: D-Study — Items Needed for Target G (BF16, optimal prompt/seed)'\n937\t    columns:\n938\t    - Benchmark\n939\t    - G≥0.80\n940\t    - G≥0.90\n941\t    - G≥0.90 CI\n942\t    - G≥0.95\n943\t    - Prompts\n944\t    - Seeds\n945\t    rows:\n946\t    - - MMLU\n947\t      - '220'\n948\t      - '420'\n949\t      - '[350, 510]'\n950\t      - '680'\n951\t      - '3'\n952\t      - '4'\n953\t    - - ARC\n954\t      - '200'\n955\t      - '380'\n956\t      - '[310, 470]'\n957\t      - '620'\n958\t      - '3'\n959\t      - '4'\n960\t    - - GSM8K\n961\t      - '150'\n962\t      - '280'\n963\t      - '[220, 360]'\n964\t      - '450'\n965\t      - '4'\n966\t      - '6'\n967\t    - - HellaSwag\n968\t      - '130'\n969\t      - '240'\n970\t      - '[190, 310]'\n971\t      - '400'\n972\t      - '4'\n973\t      - '6'\n974\t  - name: 'Table 15: Scoring Metric Comparison (MMLU, Model-as-Facet)'\n975\t    columns:\n976\t    - Source\n977\t    - Binary\n978\t    - Text-match\n979\t    - Logprob\n980\t    rows:\n981\t    - - item_id\n982\t      - 48.0%\n983\t      - 3.0%\n984\t      - 55.0%\n985\t    - - model×item\n986\t      - 21.0%\n987\t      - 1.5%\n988\t      - 18.0%\n989\t    - - prompt:item\n990\t      - 5.3%\n991\t      - 33.0%\n992\t      - 4.0%\n993\t    - - residual\n994\t      - 8.0%\n995\t      - 36.0%\n996\t      - 6.0%\n997\t    - - G coefficient\n998\t      - '0.37'\n999\t      - '0.97'\n1000\t      - '0.32'\n1001\t  - name: 'Table 16: REML vs Henderson Method I (MMLU, Binary)'\n1002\t    columns:\n1003\t    - Source\n1004\t    - Henderson %\n1005\t    - REML %\n1006\t    - Diff (pp)\n1007\t    rows:\n1008\t    - - item_id\n1009\t      - 48.0%\n1010\t      - 49.2%\n1011\t      - '+1.2'\n1012\t    - - model×item\n1013\t      - 21.0%\n1014\t      - 22.4%\n1015\t      - '+1.4'\n1016\t    - - residual\n1017\t      - 8.0%\n1018\t      - 6.1%\n1019\t      - '-1.9'\n1020\t    - - other sources\n1021\t      - 23.0%\n1022\t      - 22.3%\n1023\t      - —\n1024\t  figures_description:\n1025\t  - 'Forest plot (key figure): bootstrap 95% CIs on item_id across 4 benchmarks, ordered\n1026\t    by point estimate. 2-cluster visual: high-cluster (MMLU, ARC) separated from low-cluster\n1027\t    (GSM8K, HellaSwag) but with narrow endpoint overlap. Dashed line at 50%.'\n1028\t  - 'D-study projection curves: G coefficient vs item count for each benchmark, with\n1029\t    shaded CI bands from bootstrap. Horizontal dashed lines at G=0.80, 0.90, 0.95.\n1030\t    Shows benchmark-specific convergence rates with CIs on the crossing points.'\n1031\t  - 'Side-by-side stacked bars: basic (model-pooled) vs model-as-facet decomposition\n1032\t    for MMLU, showing 21% model×item extracted from residual (11pp) and item_id (4pp).\n1033\t    Annotation arrows.'\n1034\t  - 'Heatmap: model×item interaction strength for 4 models × 200 MMLU items, biclustered.\n1035\t    Shows Llama vs Qwen knowledge gap pattern (Llama weak on STEM, Qwen weak on humanities).'\n1036\t  analysis: 'Five key takeaways for the paper. (1) Model-as-facet reduces item_id\n1037\t    by ~4pp on ALL benchmarks — prior single-model studies systematically overestimated\n1038\t    pure item dominance. Frame as: \"even within the 7-9B parameter range, model-specific\n1039\t    knowledge gaps account for 16-21% of variance, meaning item difficulty estimates\n1040\t    from single-model studies are inflated.\" (2) Gradient direction is significant\n1041\t    (p=0.003) but magnitude CIs overlap at endpoints — use \"10-30pp spread\" in claims.\n1042\t    (3) GSM8K infra×method CI [1.2%, 4.8%] excludes zero — this is a statistically\n1043\t    robust departure from separability, requiring benchmark qualification on Claim\n1044\t    2. (4) D-study budgets differentiate benchmarks but the ratio (1.5-1.75×, not\n1045\t    3×) means the practical implication is real but less dramatic than hoped. (5)\n1046\t    Logprob scoring amplifies item_id to 55% — a third metric perspective that strengthens\n1047\t    the metric-conditional narrative.\n1048\t\n1049\t    '\n1050\t- name: 14B model scale extension\n1051\t  plan_id: exp-005\n1052\t  description: 'Qwen3-14B + DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B at BF16/FP16 × 6 prompts × 6 seeds\n1053\t    × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items on datacenter GPUs (≥48GB VRAM).\n1054\t\n1055\t    '\n1056\t  expected_results:\n1057\t    qwen3_14b_accuracy_bf16: 0.745\n1058\t    deepseek_r1_14b_accuracy_bf16: 0.728\n1059\t    item_id_14b_model_facet: 0.44\n1060\t    model_x_item_14b: 0.25\n1061\t    model_main_14b: 0.07\n1062\t    residual_14b: 0.06\n1063\t    temp_x_item_14b: 0.06\n1064\t    prompt_x_item_14b: 0.045\n1065\t    precision_main_14b: 0.003\n1066\t    g_correct_14b_binary: 0.4\n1067\t    deepseek_r1_temp_x_item: 0.085\n1068\t    qwen3_14b_temp_x_item: 0.042\n1069\t    cross_gpu_gap_14b_bf16_pp: 0.1\n1070\t    item_id_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: 4.0\n1071\t    model_x_item_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: 4.0\n1072\t    variance_hierarchy_qualitatively_preserved: true\n1073\t  result_rationale: '14B models show 44% item_id (vs 48% at 8B) because higher capacity\n1074\t    reduces floor effects — items that were impossible for 8B models become solvable\n1075\t    at 14B, compressing between-item variance. The 4pp reduction is modest, suggesting\n1076\t    item difficulty saturation is far from reached even at 14B. Model×item interaction\n1077\t    rises to 25% (from 21%) because the dense-vs-reasoning-distilled architecture\n1078\t    difference creates larger model-specific item profiles: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B\n1079\t    excels on multi-step reasoning items (distilled from R1''s reasoning traces) while\n1080\t    Qwen3-14B excels on factual knowledge items (larger pre-training corpus). This\n1081\t    architecture-driven specialization is larger than the Llama-vs-Qwen difference\n1082\t    at 8B. DeepSeek shows elevated temperature×item (8.5% vs Qwen''s 4.2%) because\n1083\t    reasoning-distilled models produce chain-of-thought that branches more at sampling\n1084\t    time — consistent with exp-002''s finding that reasoning tasks show higher temperature\n1085\t    sensitivity. Model main effect at 7% (vs 5% at 8B) reflects the larger accuracy\n1086\t    spread between dense and reasoning-distilled architectures. Precision effect remains\n1087\t    negligible (0.3pp) — precision sensitivity does not increase with scale in this\n1088\t    range. G-coefficient of 0.40 is slightly higher than 8B (0.37) because reduced\n1089\t    item variance improves relative reliability.\n1090\t\n1091\t    '\n1092\t  tables:\n1093\t  - name: 'Table 17: 8B vs 14B Variance Decomposition (MMLU, Model-as-Facet)'\n1094\t    columns:\n1095\t    - Source\n1096\t    - 8B (4 models)\n1097\t    - 14B (2 models)\n1098\t    - Δ\n1099\t    rows:\n1100\t    - - item_id\n1101\t      - 48.0%\n1102\t      - 44.0%\n1103\t      - -4.0pp\n1104\t    - - model×item\n1105\t      - 21.0%\n1106\t      - 25.0%\n1107\t      - +4.0pp\n1108\t    - - model (main)\n1109\t      - 5.0%\n1110\t      - 7.0%\n1111\t      - +2.0pp\n1112\t    - - residual\n1113\t      - 8.0%\n1114\t      - 6.0%\n1115\t      - -2.0pp\n1116\t    - - temp:item\n1117\t      - 4.8%\n1118\t      - 6.0%\n1119\t      - +1.2pp\n1120\t    - - prompt:item\n1121\t      - 5.3%\n1122\t      - 4.5%\n1123\t      - -0.8pp\n1124\t    - - precision (main)\n1125\t      - 2.5%\n1126\t      - 0.3%\n1127\t      - -2.2pp\n1128\t    - - other\n1129\t      - 5.4%\n1130\t      - 7.2%\n1131\t      - —\n1132\t  - name: 'Table 18: Per-Architecture Temperature Sensitivity (14B)'\n1133\t    columns:\n1134\t    - Model\n1135\t    - Type\n1136\t    - temp:item\n1137\t    - temp=0.0 Acc\n1138\t    - temp=1.0 Acc\n1139\t    - Spread\n1140\t    rows:\n1141\t    - - Qwen3-14B\n1142\t      - Dense\n1143\t      - 4.2%\n1144\t      - '0.752'\n1145\t      - '0.738'\n1146\t      - 1.4pp\n1147\t    - - DeepSeek-R1-14B\n1148\t      - Reasoning-distilled\n1149\t      - 8.5%\n1150\t      - '0.740'\n1151\t      - '0.715'\n1152\t      - 2.5pp\n1153\t  figures_description:\n1154\t  - 'Paired stacked bars: 8B vs 14B decomposition showing item_id compression and\n1155\t    model×item expansion. Annotation highlighting the ''scale compresses item dominance''\n1156\t    pattern.'\n1157\t  analysis: 'Scale extension confirms qualitative stability: item_id > model×item\n1158\t    > residual hierarchy holds at 14B. The 4pp item_id reduction per scale doubling\n1159\t    (8B→14B) suggests that at current model sizes, item difficulty remains the dominant\n1160\t    variance source — much larger models (70B+) would be needed to substantively alter\n1161\t    the hierarchy. The dense-vs-reasoning architecture finding is a useful Discussion\n1162\t    point: model×item increases not from more models but from more architecturally\n1163\t    diverse models. For the paper: \"variance structure is qualitatively stable across\n1164\t    7-14B but item dominance magnitude is scale- and architecture-dependent.\" Frame\n1165\t    as scope validation, not a primary finding. The precision reduction from 2.5%\n1166\t    to 0.3% at 14B (both at BF16/FP16 only, no FP32) is because the FP32 degradation\n1167\t    source is absent from this experiment.\n1168\t\n1169\t    '\n1170\t- name: Fine-tuning G-study (RoBERTa)\n1171\t  plan_id: exp-006\n1172\t  description: 'RoBERTa-large fine-tuning on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA. 6 seeds × 3 LRs\n1173\t    × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks = 1,728 total\n1174\t    runs.\n1175\t\n1176\t    '\n1177\t  expected_results:\n1178\t    sst2_accuracy_mean: 0.942\n1179\t    sst2_accuracy_sd: 0.005\n1180\t    sst2_lr_variance: 0.35\n1181\t    sst2_seed_variance: 0.25\n1182\t    sst2_data_shuffle_variance: 0.12\n1183\t    sst2_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.1\n1184\t    sst2_gpu_variance: 0.003\n1185\t    sst2_precision_variance: 0.008\n1186\t    sst2_residual: 0.159\n1187\t    mnli_accuracy_mean: 0.875\n1188\t    mnli_accuracy_sd: 0.008\n1189\t    mnli_lr_variance: 0.3\n1190\t    mnli_seed_variance: 0.22\n1191\t    mnli_data_shuffle_variance: 0.15\n1192\t    mnli_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.12\n1193\t    mnli_gpu_variance: 0.004\n1194\t    mnli_precision_variance: 0.009\n1195\t    mnli_residual: 0.177\n1196\t    rte_accuracy_mean: 0.716\n1197\t    rte_accuracy_sd: 0.032\n1198\t    rte_lr_variance: 0.2\n1199\t    rte_seed_variance: 0.16\n1200\t    rte_data_shuffle_variance: 0.28\n1201\t    rte_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.16\n1202\t    rte_gpu_variance: 0.005\n1203\t    rte_precision_variance: 0.01\n1204\t    rte_residual: 0.175\n1205\t    copa_accuracy_mean: 0.672\n1206\t    copa_accuracy_sd: 0.038\n1207\t    copa_lr_variance: 0.18\n1208\t    copa_seed_variance: 0.2\n1209\t    copa_data_shuffle_variance: 0.3\n1210\t    copa_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.14\n1211\t    copa_gpu_variance: 0.004\n1212\t    copa_precision_variance: 0.012\n1213\t    copa_residual: 0.134\n1214\t    dominant_source_large_data: learning_rate\n1215\t    dominant_source_small_data: data_shuffle\n1216\t    gpu_all_tasks_max: 0.005\n1217\t    precision_all_tasks_max: 0.012\n1218\t    hierarchy_differs_from_inference: true\n1219\t    rte_bimodal_seed_lr: true\n1220\t  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning variance structure is qualitatively different from\n1221\t    inference: hyperparameters (LR, seed, data shuffle) dominate instead of items.\n1222\t    On large datasets (SST-2, MNLI), learning rate dominates (30-35%) because the\n1223\t    optimization landscape is well-defined and LR determines the convergence basin\n1224\t    — too high overshoots, too low underfits, and the sensitivity is amplified by\n1225\t    RoBERTa''s sharp loss surface near the optimum (Dodge et al., 2020). On small\n1226\t    datasets (RTE, COPA), data shuffle dominates (28-30%) because mini-batch composition\n1227\t    has outsized influence with fewer training examples — a single misplaced hard\n1228\t    example in early batches can steer the model into a different loss basin. Seed\n1229\t    is substantial everywhere (16-25%) because weight initialization and dropout mask\n1230\t    jointly determine the optimization trajectory. The seed×LR interaction (10-16%)\n1231\t    is the fine-tuning analog of item×temperature in inference: certain seed-LR combinations\n1232\t    land in qualitatively different loss basins, producing bimodal accuracy distributions\n1233\t    on RTE (two clusters separated by ~4pp accuracy, one basin near 72% and one near\n1234\t    68%). GPU and precision effects remain negligible (<1.2%) across all tasks — the\n1235\t    variance hierarchy for infrastructure factors is consistent between fine-tuning\n1236\t    and inference, even though the methodology-factor hierarchy differs completely.\n1237\t    This validates the G-theory framework''s generality while showing that reproducibility\n1238\t    budgets cannot be transferred between evaluation modes.\n1239\t\n1240\t    '\n1241\t  tables:\n1242\t  - name: 'Table 19: Fine-Tuning Variance Decomposition Across Tasks'\n1243\t    columns:\n1244\t    - Source\n1245\t    - SST-2\n1246\t    - MNLI\n1247\t    - RTE\n1248\t    - COPA\n1249\t    rows:\n1250\t    - - learning_rate\n1251\t      - 35.0%\n1252\t      - 30.0%\n1253\t      - 20.0%\n1254\t      - 18.0%\n1255\t    - - seed\n1256\t      - 25.0%\n1257\t      - 22.0%\n1258\t      - 16.0%\n1259\t      - 20.0%\n1260\t    - - data_shuffle\n1261\t      - 12.0%\n1262\t      - 15.0%\n1263\t      - 28.0%\n1264\t      - 30.0%\n1265\t    - - seed×lr\n1266\t      - 10.0%\n1267\t      - 12.0%\n1268\t      - 16.0%\n1269\t      - 14.0%\n1270\t    - - gpu\n1271\t      - 0.3%\n1272\t      - 0.4%\n1273\t      - 0.5%\n1274\t      - 0.4%\n1275\t    - - precision\n1276\t      - 0.8%\n1277\t      - 0.9%\n1278\t      - 1.0%\n1279\t      - 1.2%\n1280\t    - - residual\n1281\t      - 16.9%\n1282\t      - 19.7%\n1283\t      - 18.5%\n1284\t      - 16.4%\n1285\t  - name: 'Table 20: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy'\n1286\t    columns:\n1287\t    - Rank\n1288\t    - Inference (MMLU)\n1289\t    - FT — Large (SST-2)\n1290\t    - FT — Small (RTE)\n1291\t    rows:\n1292\t    - - '#1'\n1293\t      - item_id (48%)\n1294\t      - learning_rate (35%)\n1295\t      - data_shuffle (28%)\n1296\t    - - '#2'\n1297\t      - model×item (21%)\n1298\t      - seed (25%)\n1299\t      - learning_rate (20%)\n1300\t    - - '#3'\n1301\t      - residual (8%)\n1302\t      - residual (17%)\n1303\t      - seed×lr (16%)\n1304\t    - - '#4'\n1305\t      - prompt:item (5%)\n1306\t      - data_shuffle (12%)\n1307\t      - seed (16%)\n1308\t    - - Infra\n1309\t      - precision 2.5%\n1310\t      - gpu+prec 1.1%\n1311\t      - gpu+prec 1.5%\n1312\t  figures_description:\n1313\t  - 'Side-by-side stacked bars: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (4 tasks), highlighting\n1314\t    the qualitative hierarchy shift from item-dominated to hyperparameter-dominated.'\n1315\t  - 'Scatter: per-run accuracy vs learning rate for RTE, colored by seed, showing\n1316\t    bimodal clustering at seed×LR interaction. Two loss basins annotated.'\n1317\t  analysis: 'Fine-tuning results support the paper''s broader thesis at a meta-level:\n1318\t    variance structure is task-conditional, and this extends beyond benchmarks to\n1319\t    evaluation MODES. The LR-dominated (large data) vs shuffle-dominated (small data)\n1320\t    pattern in fine-tuning parallels the knowledge-item-dominated vs reasoning-interaction-dominated\n1321\t    pattern in inference — in both cases, the dominant source shifts as the task''s\n1322\t    \"difficulty structure\" changes. Infrastructure effects remain negligible in both\n1323\t    settings, providing cross-mode evidence for separability (with the caveat that\n1324\t    fine-tuning doesn''t test the temperature/seed × precision interactions that drive\n1325\t    GSM8K''s infra×method departure). For the paper: position as Discussion material\n1326\t    showing G-theory generality. Explicit caveat: encoder-only fine-tuning (RoBERTa)\n1327\t    may not generalize to decoder fine-tuning where generation-related variance sources\n1328\t    (temperature, seed) would re-enter.\n1329\t\n1330\t    '\n1331\t- name: LayerCast precision mitigation\n1332\t  plan_id: exp-007\n1333\t  description: 'LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision condition\n1334\t    on MMLU. Same 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings ×\n1335\t    200 items.\n1336\t\n1337\t    '\n1338\t  expected_results:\n1339\t    layercast_accuracy_mean: 0.682\n1340\t    layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: 0.2\n1341\t    layercast_vs_fp16_gap_pp: 0.1\n1342\t    layercast_vs_fp32_gap_pp: 2.7\n1343\t    layercast_item_id_binary: 0.515\n1344\t    layercast_prec_x_item_reduction_vs_fp16_pct: 40\n1345\t    layercast_g_correct_binary: 0.37\n1346\t    layercast_inference_overhead_pct: 18\n1347\t    layercast_prec_x_temp_binary: 0.001\n1348\t    layercast_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.008\n1349\t  result_rationale: 'LayerCast reduces precision:item interaction by ~40% vs FP16\n1350\t    because the FP32 compute path eliminates mid-layer accumulation errors that flip\n1351\t    items near decision boundaries. However, the practical benefit is marginal: FP16\n1352\t    precision:item was already only 5% of total variance, so a 40% reduction brings\n1353\t    it to ~3% — still dwarfed by item_id at 51.5%. Accuracy (0.682) falls between\n1354\t    BF16 (0.684) and FP16 (0.681), essentially equivalent to both. The precision×temperature\n1355\t    interaction drops to 0.1% binary (from FP16''s ~0.2%) because FP32 compute stabilizes\n1356\t    the logit computation at each sampling step. The 18% inference overhead comes\n1357\t    from FP16→FP32 upcasting at each transformer layer. G-coefficient is unchanged\n1358\t    (0.37) because the dominant item_id component is unaffected by precision mitigation.\n1359\t    Given that BF16 already shows negligible precision effects and has no inference\n1360\t    overhead, LayerCast is only relevant for the specific case where FP16 memory savings\n1361\t    are required AND cross-hardware reproducibility matters.\n1362\t\n1363\t    '\n1364\t  tables:\n1365\t  - name: 'Table 21: LayerCast Comparison Across Precision Modes'\n1366\t    columns:\n1367\t    - Metric\n1368\t    - FP32\n1369\t    - BF16\n1370\t    - FP16\n1371\t    - LayerCast\n1372\t    rows:\n1373\t    - - Accuracy\n1374\t      - '0.655'\n1375\t      - '0.684'\n1376\t      - '0.681'\n1377\t      - '0.682'\n1378\t    - - item_id %\n1379\t      - 50.5%\n1380\t      - 52.0%\n1381\t      - 51.8%\n1382\t      - 51.5%\n1383\t    - - prec:item %\n1384\t      - 5.8%\n1385\t      - 4.5%\n1386\t      - 5.0%\n1387\t      - 3.0%\n1388\t    - - prec×temp (binary)\n1389\t      - 0.3%\n1390\t      - 0.1%\n1391\t      - 0.2%\n1392\t      - 0.1%\n1393\t    - - prec×temp (text)\n1394\t      - 2.5%\n1395\t      - 1.8%\n1396\t      - 2.2%\n1397\t      - 0.8%\n1398\t    - - G (binary)\n1399\t      - '0.36'\n1400\t      - '0.37'\n1401\t      - '0.37'\n1402\t      - '0.37'\n1403\t    - - Inference cost (rel.)\n1404\t      - 1.35×\n1405\t      - 1.00×\n1406\t      - 1.00×\n1407\t      - 1.18×\n1408\t  figures_description:\n1409\t  - 'Bar chart: precision:item interaction by precision mode, showing LayerCast intermediate\n1410\t    between BF16 and FP16 but closer to BF16.'\n1411\t  analysis: 'LayerCast is a footnote finding. Precision effects are already small\n1412\t    post-confound analysis (precision main 2.5-3% of total, infra×method <0.5% on\n1413\t    knowledge benchmarks). Mitigating them further with LayerCast provides diminishing\n1414\t    returns at 18% inference cost. Include as 1-2 paragraphs in Discussion or as appendix\n1415\t    material. Practical recommendation: use BF16 — it has no overhead and minimal\n1416\t    precision artifacts. LayerCast is only worth mentioning to preempt reviewer questions\n1417\t    about mitigation strategies.\n1418\t\n1419\t    '\n1420\t- name: MATH benchmark extension\n1421\t  plan_id: exp-008\n1422\t  description: 'MATH (200 items) with 4 models × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts\n1423\t    × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings. Tests chain-of-thought reasoning variance.\n1424\t\n1425\t    '\n1426\t  expected_results:\n1427\t    math_accuracy_mean: 0.38\n1428\t    math_accuracy_sd_across_conditions: 0.045\n1429\t    math_item_id_basic: 0.32\n1430\t    math_residual_basic: 0.17\n1431\t    math_temp_x_item_basic: 0.14\n1432\t    math_seed_x_item_basic: 0.11\n1433\t    math_prompt_x_item_basic: 0.08\n1434\t    math_temperature_main_basic: 0.06\n1435\t    math_precision_main_basic: 0.005\n1436\t    math_infra_x_method_binary: 0.025\n1437\t    math_other_basic: 0.09\n1438\t    math_item_id_model_facet: 0.28\n1439\t    math_model_x_item: 0.22\n1440\t    math_model_main: 0.06\n1441\t    math_residual_model_facet: 0.07\n1442\t    math_g_correct_binary: 0.52\n1443\t    five_benchmark_item_id_range:\n1444\t    - 0.28\n1445\t    - 0.48\n1446\t    five_benchmark_spread_pp: 20\n1447\t    five_benchmark_pattern: '{MMLU 48%, ARC 46%} > {GSM8K 36%, HellaSwag 31%} ≥ {MATH\n1448\t      28%}'\n1449\t    three_tier_structure: true\n1450\t    math_infra_x_method_textmatch: 0.042\n1451\t  result_rationale: 'MATH shows the lowest item dominance (28% model-as-facet) because\n1452\t    multi-step mathematical proofs introduce maximal chain-of-thought variance. Unlike\n1453\t    GSM8K''s mostly arithmetic word problems, MATH items require proof strategies\n1454\t    (induction, construction, algebraic manipulation) with multiple valid approaches\n1455\t    — temperature and seed determine which strategy the model attempts, making temp:item\n1456\t    (14%) and seed:item (11%) jointly exceed item_id. The 38% overall accuracy (8B\n1457\t    models on MATH) creates strong floor effects: ~40% of items are never solved regardless\n1458\t    of conditions (zero variance), ~20% are always solved (zero variance), and the\n1459\t    remaining ~40% of \"mid-difficulty\" items carry nearly all the variance — and on\n1460\t    these items, condition sensitivity is extreme. G-coefficient at 0.52 (highest\n1461\t    of all benchmarks) is counterintuitive but correct: the items that 8B models CAN\n1462\t    solve are consistently solved (low relative error), so the universe score is reliable\n1463\t    despite high absolute variance. Model×item at 22% is the highest across benchmarks\n1464\t    because dense vs reasoning-distilled architectures diverge most on proof items:\n1465\t    DeepSeek-R1-Distill can solve certain proof items that no other 8B model touches,\n1466\t    creating large model-specific item effects. Adding MATH extends the 2-cluster\n1467\t    gradient to a potential 3-tier structure with 20pp total spread (48-28%), strengthening\n1468\t    the cognitive-complexity narrative. Infra×method at 2.5% binary confirms the GSM8K\n1469\t    pattern: math-heavy generation shows detectable precision×methodology interaction.\n1470\t    Under text-match, MATH prec×temp reaches 4.2% (the highest single infra×method\n1471\t    interaction) because proof-format outputs are extremely sensitive to token-level\n1472\t    differences.\n1473\t\n1474\t    '\n1475\t  tables:\n1476\t  - name: 'Table 22: MATH Variance Decomposition (Binary, Model-as-Facet)'\n1477\t    columns:\n1478\t    - Source\n1479\t    - MATH\n1480\t    - GSM8K (ref)\n1481\t    - MMLU (ref)\n1482\t    rows:\n1483\t    - - item_id\n1484\t      - 28.0%\n1485\t      - 36.0%\n1486\t      - 48.0%\n1487\t    - - model×item\n1488\t      - 22.0%\n1489\t      - 18.0%\n1490\t      - 21.0%\n1491\t    - - temp:item\n1492\t      - 14.0%\n1493\t      - 9.0%\n1494\t      - 4.8%\n1495\t    - - seed:item\n1496\t      - 11.0%\n1497\t      - 5.5%\n1498\t      - 2.0%\n1499\t    - - prompt:item\n1500\t      - 6.5%\n1501\t      - 5.5%\n1502\t      - 5.3%\n1503\t    - - model (main)\n1504\t      - 6.0%\n1505\t      - 4.0%\n1506\t      - 5.0%\n1507\t    - - residual\n1508\t      - 7.0%\n1509\t      - 10.0%\n1510\t      - 8.0%\n1511\t    - - temperature\n1512\t      - 4.5%\n1513\t      - 3.5%\n1514\t      - 1.2%\n1515\t    - - infra×method\n1516\t      - 2.5%\n1517\t      - 3.0%\n1518\t      - 0.4%\n1519\t    - - other\n1520\t      - -1.5%\n1521\t      - 5.5%\n1522\t      - 4.3%\n1523\t    note: Negative 'other' due to rounding and minor negative Henderson estimates\n1524\t      on near-zero terms.\n1525\t  - name: 'Table 23: Updated 5-Benchmark Summary (Model-as-Facet)'\n1526\t    columns:\n1527\t    - Benchmark\n1528\t    - Type\n1529\t    - item_id\n1530\t    - G (binary)\n1531\t    - infra×method\n1532\t    - Tier\n1533\t    rows:\n1534\t    - - MMLU\n1535\t      - Knowledge\n1536\t      - 48.0%\n1537\t      - '0.37'\n1538\t      - 0.4%\n1539\t      - High\n1540\t    - - ARC\n1541\t      - Knowledge\n1542\t      - 46.0%\n1543\t      - '0.35'\n1544\t      - 0.5%\n1545\t      - High\n1546\t    - - GSM8K\n1547\t      - Arithmetic reasoning\n1548\t      - 36.0%\n1549\t      - '0.42'\n1550\t      - 3.0%\n1551\t      - Mid\n1552\t    - - HellaSwag\n1553\t      - Commonsense\n1554\t      - 31.0%\n1555\t      - '0.48'\n1556\t      - 0.3%\n1557\t      - Mid\n1558\t    - - MATH\n1559\t      - Proof reasoning\n1560\t      - 28.0%\n1561\t      - '0.52'\n1562\t      - 2.5%\n1563\t      - Low\n1564\t  figures_description:\n1565\t  - 'Updated stacked bar (5 benchmarks): variance decomposition showing the 3-tier\n1566\t    gradient. Item_id bars decrease monotonically from left (MMLU) to right (MATH);\n1567\t    interaction bars grow.'\n1568\t  - 'Line plot: item_id % vs cognitive complexity ordinal (knowledge → arithmetic\n1569\t    → commonsense → proof), with bootstrap CI bands. Stepped pattern visible despite\n1570\t    within-tier noise.'\n1571\t  analysis: 'MATH is the median tier''s upgrade path. With 5 benchmarks and 20pp spread,\n1572\t    the narrative strengthens from \"2-cluster\" to \"task-complexity gradient with 3\n1573\t    tiers\": knowledge benchmarks (MMLU, ARC) → arithmetic/commonsense reasoning (GSM8K,\n1574\t    HellaSwag) → proof reasoning (MATH). HellaSwag''s position (31%, in \"mid\" tier\n1575\t    despite being commonsense rather than math) adds nuance — the gradient tracks\n1576\t    multi-step reasoning demand more than domain. MATH also reinforces the GSM8K infra×method\n1577\t    finding: both math-heavy benchmarks show 2.5-3% infra×method binary, while knowledge\n1578\t    and commonsense benchmarks show <0.5%. This transforms the separability exception\n1579\t    from \"one benchmark\" to \"math-generation benchmarks\" — a more principled category\n1580\t    that strengthens the paper. If AAAI timeline permits exp-008 (50 GPU hours, dependent\n1581\t    on exp-002), include it and claim the 3-tier gradient. If not, the 4-benchmark\n1582\t    2-cluster story is publishable with softened framing, and MATH can be added for\n1583\t    camera-ready or the ACL submission.'\n1584\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7213f7b-2e18-414c-bcc7-5947fa0bd98a","uuid":"836d54d6-6450-498c-956a-3bd723cd684b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T12:47:15.240Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T222113/median/imagined_experiments.yaml","content":"tier: median\nnarrative_guidance: 'Soften \"systematic gradient\" to \"benchmark-conditional variance\n  structure\" or \"task-type clustering.\" Cross-benchmark pattern is 2-cluster ({MMLU,\n  ARC} high item dominance vs {GSM8K, HellaSwag} lower), not a clean monotonic 4-point\n  gradient. Item dominance remains dominant on knowledge benchmarks but magnitude\n  is less precise than MVP suggested once model-as-facet is handled and bootstrap\n  CIs computed. Separability holds on knowledge benchmarks but GSM8K shows infra×method\n  ~3% under binary scoring, requiring benchmark+metric qualification on Claim 2. Bootstrap\n  CIs on gradient endpoints overlap partially — directional claims hold, magnitude\n  claims are imprecise. Reframe hook: \"the variance structure itself depends on what\n  you''re benchmarking\" rather than a clean ordering. FP32 degradation is real (not\n  engine confound), position as known finding (Yuan 2025) with novel item-level G-theory\n  breakdown. Model-as-facet absorption (21% on MMLU) reframes prior single-model studies\n  as having overestimated pure item dominance — turn this into a feature, not a weakness.\n  Target AAAI with adjusted framing; if exp-008 (MATH) is feasible and strengthens\n  the clustering to 3-tier, include it; otherwise ACL ARR October is a comfortable\n  fallback.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Full-scale MMLU precision study (confound-fixed)\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: '4 models × 3 precisions (FP32/BF16/FP16, all V0 enforce_eager) × 6\n    prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items (stratified).\n    1,036,800 total observations.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_observations: 1036800\n    total_conditions: 5184\n    accuracy_fp32: 0.655\n    accuracy_bf16: 0.684\n    accuracy_fp16: 0.681\n    accuracy_gap_fp32_bf16_pp: 2.9\n    accuracy_gap_bf16_fp16_pp: 0.3\n    accuracy_by_model:\n      qwen3_8b:\n        fp32: 0.695\n        bf16: 0.71\n        fp16: 0.706\n      llama31_8b:\n        fp32: 0.6\n        bf16: 0.672\n        fp16: 0.669\n      gemma2_9b:\n        fp32: 0.638\n        bf16: 0.66\n        fp16: 0.658\n      mistral_7b:\n        fp32: 0.688\n        bf16: 0.694\n        fp16: 0.691\n    item_id_variance_binary: 0.52\n    residual_variance_binary: 0.14\n    precision_x_item_binary: 0.05\n    prompt_x_item_binary: 0.058\n    temperature_x_item_binary: 0.055\n    seed_x_item_binary: 0.028\n    precision_main_binary: 0.03\n    temperature_main_binary: 0.015\n    prompt_main_binary: 0.008\n    ordering_main_binary: 0.002\n    precision_x_temperature_binary: 0.002\n    precision_x_prompt_binary: 0.001\n    precision_x_seed_binary: 0.001\n    other_binary: 0.09\n    residual_textmatch: 0.36\n    prompt_x_item_textmatch: 0.33\n    temperature_main_textmatch: 0.14\n    precision_x_item_textmatch: 0.04\n    precision_x_temperature_textmatch: 0.022\n    temperature_x_item_textmatch: 0.03\n    item_id_textmatch: 0.03\n    precision_main_textmatch: 0.025\n    other_textmatch: 0.023\n    g_correct_binary: 0.37\n    g_correct_textmatch: 0.97\n    item_id_rank_corr_across_models: 0.82\n    fp32_gap_model_range_pp:\n    - 1.5\n    - 7.2\n  result_rationale: 'V0 enforce_eager for all precisions eliminates the 0.22pp engine\n    confound (bf16_eager_disentangle) but the FP32 accuracy gap PERSISTS at ~2.9pp\n    average — confirming the disentangle conclusion that 97.4% of the gap was genuine\n    precision effect. FP32 degradation is strongly model-dependent: Llama-3.1-8B shows\n    7.2pp gap (consistent with MVP''s 8.5pp on this model) while Qwen3-8B shows only\n    1.5pp, likely because Qwen''s training used mixed-precision strategies that are\n    more robust to FP32 inference rounding. Precision main effect at 3% is real but\n    dwarfed by item_id at 52%. Precision:item at 5% means certain items (those near\n    decision boundaries) are disproportionately affected — this is the novel item-level\n    breakdown that extends Yuan et al. (2025). Item_id at 52% is lower than MVP''s\n    58.4% because 200 stratified items (vs 50) include more mid-difficulty items that\n    reduce between-item variance, and pooling across 4 models absorbs some item-specific\n    variance into residual. Residual at 14% is inflated because model effects are\n    pooled — exp-004 will decompose this. G=0.37 replicates MVP''s 0.357, confirming\n    item dominance makes G≥0.90 unreachable without massive item expansion. Text-match\n    scoring replicates the qualitative shift: prompt:item dominates (33%), precision×temperature\n    at 2.2% (consistent with MVP''s 1.3% but larger due to more conditions), driven\n    by floating-point non-associativity in generation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: MMLU Variance Decomposition — Binary Correct (Basic, Model Pooled)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - '% Total Variance'\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 52.0%\n    - - residual (includes model effects)\n      - 14.0%\n    - - prompt:item\n      - 5.8%\n    - - temperature:item\n      - 5.5%\n    - - precision:item\n      - 5.0%\n    - - precision (main)\n      - 3.0%\n    - - seed:item\n      - 2.8%\n    - - temperature (main)\n      - 1.5%\n    - - prompt (main)\n      - 0.8%\n    - - infra×method (all)\n      - 0.4%\n    - - ordering\n      - 0.2%\n    - - other 2-way / 3-way\n      - 9.0%\n  - name: 'Table 2: MMLU Variance Decomposition — Text Exact Match (Basic)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - '% Total Variance'\n    rows:\n    - - residual\n      - 36.0%\n    - - prompt:item\n      - 33.0%\n    - - temperature (main)\n      - 14.0%\n    - - precision:item\n      - 4.0%\n    - - temperature:item\n      - 3.0%\n    - - item_id\n      - 3.0%\n    - - precision (main)\n      - 2.5%\n    - - precision×temperature\n      - 2.2%\n    - - other\n      - 2.3%\n  - name: 'Table 3: Per-Model Accuracy by Precision (V0 enforce_eager)'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - FP32\n    - BF16\n    - FP16\n    - FP32-BF16 Gap\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen3-8B-Instruct\n      - '0.695'\n      - '0.710'\n      - '0.706'\n      - 1.5pp\n    - - Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\n      - '0.600'\n      - '0.672'\n      - '0.669'\n      - 7.2pp\n    - - Gemma-2-9B-IT\n      - '0.638'\n      - '0.660'\n      - '0.658'\n      - 2.2pp\n    - - Mistral-7B-v0.3\n      - '0.688'\n      - '0.694'\n      - '0.691'\n      - 0.6pp\n    - - Average\n      - '0.655'\n      - '0.684'\n      - '0.681'\n      - 2.9pp\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked bar: binary vs text-match variance decomposition side by side, showing\n    metric-conditional shift from item-dominated (binary) to prompt-dominated (text-match).'\n  - 'Heatmap: FP32 degradation (pp) by model × item difficulty quartile, showing model-dependent\n    and difficulty-dependent precision sensitivity.'\n  - 'Scatter: item-level accuracy variance vs item difficulty, colored by MMLU subject\n    category.'\n  analysis: 'Key finding: FP32 degradation is REAL, not an engine confound. The bf16_eager_disentangle\n    result is confirmed at scale — V0 enforce_eager changes nothing substantial. This\n    reframes the contribution: we provide the first item-level G-theory decomposition\n    of precision effects, showing that precision:item (5%) exceeds precision main\n    (3%). The model-dependence of FP32 degradation (1.5-7.2pp) is novel and suggests\n    training-time precision choices affect inference robustness. For the paper: position\n    FP32 degradation as supporting Yuan et al. (2025) with novel item×model granularity,\n    NOT as a primary finding. Separability on MMLU is confirmed: infra×method = 0.4%\n    binary, 2.2% text-match (precision×temperature only). CIs deferred to exp-004.\n\n    '\n- name: Item resampling stability test\n  plan_id: exp-001b\n  description: '3 independent stratified samples of 200 MMLU items (seeds 0, 7, 99)\n    × Llama-3.1-8B × 3 precisions × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 54 conditions/sample,\n    32,400 obs/sample.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    item_id_sample_0: 0.49\n    item_id_sample_7: 0.55\n    item_id_sample_99: 0.44\n    item_id_mean: 0.493\n    item_id_sd: 0.055\n    item_id_cv: 0.19\n    g_coeff_sample_0: 0.36\n    g_coeff_sample_7: 0.32\n    g_coeff_sample_99: 0.39\n    accuracy_sample_0: 0.668\n    accuracy_sample_7: 0.641\n    accuracy_sample_99: 0.679\n    mean_item_difficulty_sample_0: 0.5\n    mean_item_difficulty_sample_7: 0.42\n    mean_item_difficulty_sample_99: 0.54\n    sd_item_difficulty_sample_0: 0.28\n    sd_item_difficulty_sample_7: 0.32\n    sd_item_difficulty_sample_99: 0.25\n    rank_order_top3_sources_preserved: true\n    residual_cv: 0.11\n    prompt_item_cv: 0.09\n  result_rationale: 'CV of 19% reflects meaningful item-sampling uncertainty. MMLU\n    has 14,042 items across 57 subjects with heterogeneous difficulty; stratified\n    sampling with 200 items (1.4%) leaves substantial sampling variance especially\n    in the tails. Sample 7 draws more hard items (mean difficulty 0.42), inflating\n    between-item variance to 55% because the difficulty spread is wider (SD 0.32).\n    Sample 99 draws more mid-difficulty items (mean 0.54, SD 0.25), compressing item_id\n    to 44% because items cluster around the center. Despite magnitude variation, the\n    qualitative hierarchy is preserved: item_id ranks #1 in all 3 samples, residual\n    #2, prompt:item #3. This stability-in-rank with instability-in-magnitude is the\n    key finding: the headline claim \"item dominance ~50%\" needs error bars, reported\n    as \"44-55% across independent 200-item stratified draws.\" The G-coefficient varies\n    inversely (0.32-0.39) because higher item variance = lower generalizability of\n    the benchmark score. This directly motivates D-study item-count recommendations\n    with CIs, not point estimates — if your headline variance component shifts ±5pp\n    with a different item draw, your optimal resource allocation shifts too.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 4: Resampling Stability of Variance Components'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - Sample 0\n    - Sample 7\n    - Sample 99\n    - Mean\n    - CV\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 49.0%\n      - 55.0%\n      - 44.0%\n      - 49.3%\n      - '0.19'\n    - - residual\n      - 17.5%\n      - 15.0%\n      - 19.5%\n      - 17.3%\n      - '0.11'\n    - - prompt:item\n      - 6.5%\n      - 6.0%\n      - 7.0%\n      - 6.5%\n      - '0.09'\n    - - temp:item\n      - 5.8%\n      - 5.2%\n      - 6.5%\n      - 5.8%\n      - '0.10'\n    - - prec:item\n      - 5.0%\n      - 5.5%\n      - 4.2%\n      - 4.9%\n      - '0.12'\n    - - precision\n      - 3.0%\n      - 3.5%\n      - 2.8%\n      - 3.1%\n      - '0.10'\n    - - other\n      - 13.2%\n      - 9.8%\n      - 16.0%\n      - 13.0%\n      - —\n  - name: 'Table 5: Per-Sample Summary Statistics'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Sample 0\n    - Sample 7\n    - Sample 99\n    rows:\n    - - N items\n      - '200'\n      - '200'\n      - '200'\n    - - Mean item difficulty\n      - '0.50'\n      - '0.42'\n      - '0.54'\n    - - SD item difficulty\n      - '0.28'\n      - '0.32'\n      - '0.25'\n    - - Overall accuracy\n      - '0.668'\n      - '0.641'\n      - '0.679'\n    - - G (binary)\n      - '0.36'\n      - '0.32'\n      - '0.39'\n    - - item_id %\n      - 49.0%\n      - 55.0%\n      - 44.0%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot: item_id % with bootstrap 95% CIs across 3 resamples, showing overlapping\n    but distinct intervals. Horizontal shaded band marks the MVP point estimate (58.4%)\n    for comparison.'\n  - 'Histogram overlay: item difficulty distribution for each sample, showing how\n    stratification seed shifts the difficulty profile despite identical stratification\n    ratios.'\n  analysis: 'The 19% CV is the paper''s best defense against \"you just got lucky with\n    your items.\" It quantifies the item-sampling uncertainty that plagues every single-draw\n    benchmark study. Rank ordering is robust (item_id always #1) but precise magnitude\n    depends on which items you drew — explaining why Mesting (2026) and Brittlebench\n    (2026) reached different headline numbers on different item sets. For the paper:\n    always report item_id as a range (44-55%) not a point. D-study projections from\n    exp-004 should include CIs that account for this resampling instability.\n\n    '\n- name: Multi-benchmark replication (GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag)\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: '4 models × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures\n    × 4 orderings × 200 items on GSM8K, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag. Reduced crossing\n    vs exp-001.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_observations_per_benchmark: 384000\n    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.575\n    gsm8k_item_id_binary: 0.4\n    gsm8k_residual_binary: 0.15\n    gsm8k_temp_x_item_binary: 0.1\n    gsm8k_seed_x_item_binary: 0.07\n    gsm8k_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.065\n    gsm8k_temperature_binary: 0.04\n    gsm8k_prec_x_temp_binary: 0.022\n    gsm8k_prec_x_seed_binary: 0.005\n    gsm8k_prec_x_prompt_binary: 0.003\n    gsm8k_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.03\n    gsm8k_precision_main_binary: 0.01\n    gsm8k_prec_x_item_binary: 0.015\n    gsm8k_other_binary: 0.093\n    gsm8k_g_correct_binary: 0.42\n    arc_accuracy: 0.625\n    arc_item_id_binary: 0.5\n    arc_residual_binary: 0.12\n    arc_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.085\n    arc_temp_x_item_binary: 0.055\n    arc_seed_x_item_binary: 0.04\n    arc_prec_x_item_binary: 0.025\n    arc_temperature_binary: 0.025\n    arc_precision_binary: 0.01\n    arc_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.005\n    arc_other_binary: 0.115\n    arc_g_correct_binary: 0.35\n    hellaswag_accuracy: 0.712\n    hellaswag_item_id_binary: 0.35\n    hellaswag_residual_binary: 0.18\n    hellaswag_temp_x_item_binary: 0.095\n    hellaswag_seed_x_item_binary: 0.085\n    hellaswag_prompt_x_item_binary: 0.075\n    hellaswag_temperature_binary: 0.045\n    hellaswag_prec_x_item_binary: 0.015\n    hellaswag_precision_binary: 0.005\n    hellaswag_infra_x_method_total_binary: 0.003\n    hellaswag_other_binary: 0.127\n    hellaswag_g_correct_binary: 0.48\n    gradient_pattern: '2-cluster: {MMLU 52%, ARC 50%} vs {GSM8K 40%, HellaSwag 35%}'\n    gradient_spread_pp: 17\n    within_cluster_ordering_reliable: false\n    gsm8k_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.035\n    arc_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.022\n    hellaswag_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.016\n    infra_x_method_textmatch_max: 0.035\n  result_rationale: 'The 2-cluster pattern emerges because variance structure tracks\n    cognitive demand, not benchmark labels. Knowledge-retrieval benchmarks (MMLU,\n    ARC) show high item dominance (50-52% basic) because item difficulty is the primary\n    driver — items test factual knowledge that is either present or absent in the\n    model, and this binary state dominates across conditions. ARC clusters with MMLU\n    despite being labeled \"science reasoning\" because most ARC items are effectively\n    factual recall (what happens when ice melts?) rather than multi-step inference.\n    Reasoning benchmarks (GSM8K, HellaSwag) show lower item dominance (35-40%) because\n    temperature and seed interact strongly with items: multi-step reasoning and commonsense\n    inference have multiple valid processing paths, and sampling randomness determines\n    which path is taken, shifting variance from item_id to interaction terms. GSM8K\n    item dominance at 40% is above the hypothesized 20-30% because even math reasoning\n    has trivially easy items (single-step arithmetic) and impossible items (complex\n    multi-step for 8B models), creating a floor of item-driven variance. The critical\n    median-tier finding: GSM8K shows infra×method = 3.0% under binary scoring (precision×temperature\n    2.2% + precision×seed 0.5% + precision×prompt 0.3%). This breaks universal separability.\n    The mechanism: math generation produces longer chains-of-thought where BF16 vs\n    FP16 accumulation differences compound; temperature controls how aggressively\n    alternative reasoning paths are explored; the precision×temperature combination\n    can push probability distributions past decision boundaries, flipping the final\n    numerical answer. Binary scoring captures this because the extracted number differs.\n    Other benchmarks show infra×method <0.5% binary — the effect is GSM8K-specific.\n    Under text-match, GSM8K prec×temp reaches 3.5% because exact-string comparison\n    of multi-step outputs amplifies floating-point divergence. The gradient spread\n    of 17pp (MMLU 52% vs HellaSwag 35%, basic) is within the target range [10-24pp];\n    after model-as-facet correction in exp-004 it will be similar (48% vs 31% = 17pp).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition — Binary (Basic, Model\n      Pooled)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - MMLU\n    - GSM8K\n    - ARC\n    - HellaSwag\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 52.0%\n      - 40.0%\n      - 50.0%\n      - 35.0%\n    - - residual\n      - 14.0%\n      - 15.0%\n      - 12.0%\n      - 18.0%\n    - - temp:item\n      - 5.5%\n      - 10.0%\n      - 5.5%\n      - 9.5%\n    - - seed:item\n      - 2.8%\n      - 7.0%\n      - 4.0%\n      - 8.5%\n    - - prompt:item\n      - 5.8%\n      - 6.5%\n      - 8.5%\n      - 7.5%\n    - - prec:item\n      - 5.0%\n      - 1.5%\n      - 2.5%\n      - 1.5%\n    - - precision (main)\n      - 3.0%\n      - 1.0%\n      - 1.0%\n      - 0.5%\n    - - temperature (main)\n      - 1.5%\n      - 4.0%\n      - 2.5%\n      - 4.5%\n    - - infra×method (total)\n      - 0.4%\n      - 3.0%\n      - 0.5%\n      - 0.3%\n    - - other\n      - 10.0%\n      - 12.0%\n      - 13.5%\n      - 14.7%\n  - name: 'Table 7: Cross-Benchmark Summary (Basic)'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Task Type\n    - item_id %\n    - G (binary)\n    - infra×method\n    - Cluster\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - Knowledge\n      - 52.0%\n      - '0.37'\n      - 0.4%\n      - High\n    - - ARC-Challenge\n      - Knowledge/Science\n      - 50.0%\n      - '0.35'\n      - 0.5%\n      - High\n    - - GSM8K\n      - Math reasoning\n      - 40.0%\n      - '0.42'\n      - 3.0%\n      - Low\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - Commonsense\n      - 35.0%\n      - '0.48'\n      - 0.3%\n      - Low\n  - name: 'Table 8: GSM8K Infra×Method Interaction Breakdown (Binary)'\n    columns:\n    - Interaction\n    - '% Total Variance'\n    - 95% CI (bootstrap, exp-004)\n    rows:\n    - - precision×temperature\n      - 2.2%\n      - deferred\n    - - precision×seed\n      - 0.5%\n      - deferred\n    - - precision×prompt\n      - 0.3%\n      - deferred\n    - - Total infra×method\n      - 3.0%\n      - deferred\n    note: Only GSM8K exceeds 1% threshold. MMLU 0.4%, ARC 0.5%, HellaSwag 0.3%.\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Stacked bar (signature figure): 4-benchmark variance decomposition, bars sorted\n    by item_id descending, showing 2-cluster visual. Item_id bars in dark blue, interaction\n    terms in lighter shades, precision/temperature main in warm tones.'\n  - 'Scatter: item_id % vs benchmark task-type ordinal (knowledge → reasoning), with\n    error bars from exp-001b resampling on MMLU. Shows cluster structure, not monotonic\n    trend.'\n  - 'Heatmap: pairwise Spearman correlation of per-item variance profiles across 4\n    benchmarks, showing r>0.7 within clusters and r<0.3 between clusters.'\n  analysis: 'This is the core finding. The 2-cluster pattern is clear and the direction\n    is robust, but it is NOT the clean monotonic gradient originally hypothesized.\n    ARC clusters with MMLU (not intermediate) — this is actually a stronger finding\n    than a gradient: variance structure tracks cognitive demand, and ARC''s \"science\n    reasoning\" label is misleading. The GSM8K infra×method at 3% binary is the median\n    tier''s key complication for Claim 2: separability is benchmark-conditional, not\n    universal. The paper must add \"on knowledge-retrieval benchmarks\" or \"when math\n    reasoning is excluded\" to the separability claim. Under text-match, GSM8K reaches\n    3.5% (within target [1.5%, 4.5%]). Practical implication: practitioners evaluating\n    math reasoning on GSM8K should fix both precision AND temperature to minimize\n    infra×method confounding.\n\n    '\n- name: Multi-hardware G-study\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: '4 models × 2 GPU types (RTX PRO 6000 + second GPU) × 2 precisions\n    (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_observations: 1152000\n    hardware_main_binary: 0.004\n    hardware_x_precision_binary: 0.008\n    hardware_x_temperature_binary: 0.003\n    hardware_x_seed_binary: 0.002\n    hardware_x_prompt_binary: 0.001\n    hardware_x_methodology_total_binary: 0.014\n    hardware_x_item_binary: 0.018\n    total_hardware_related_binary: 0.036\n    hardware_main_textmatch: 0.012\n    hardware_x_precision_textmatch: 0.022\n    hardware_x_methodology_total_textmatch: 0.032\n    hardware_x_item_textmatch: 0.028\n    total_hardware_related_textmatch: 0.072\n    item_id_with_hardware_binary: 0.5\n    g_correct_binary_gpu1: 0.37\n    g_correct_binary_gpu2: 0.36\n    cross_gpu_gap_bf16_mean_pp: 0.12\n    cross_gpu_gap_fp16_mean_pp: 0.75\n    cross_gpu_gap_bf16_max_pp: 0.2\n    cross_gpu_gap_fp16_max_pp: 1.1\n    model_ranking_preserved_across_gpus: true\n    hardware_variance_fp16_subset: 0.022\n    hardware_variance_bf16_subset: 0.003\n  result_rationale: 'Hardware main effect at 0.4% binary is negligible because both\n    GPUs implement the same IEEE 754 operations; differences arise only from CUDA\n    kernel scheduling nondeterminism and memory subsystem timing. The critical finding\n    is hardware×precision at 0.8% binary: FP16 is more hardware-sensitive than BF16\n    (cross-GPU gap 0.75pp vs 0.12pp), because FP16''s narrower dynamic range (5-bit\n    exponent vs BF16''s 8-bit) makes accumulation order hardware-dependent — consistent\n    with Yuan et al. (2025). When subsetting to FP16-only data, hardware explains\n    2.2% of variance (within median narrative''s \"1-3% on one precision\"). Under text-match,\n    hardware effects roughly double (total 7.2%) because different GPU kernel orderings\n    produce different final tokens in long generations, amplified by exact-match scoring.\n    Hardware×item at 1.8% binary means certain items near decision boundaries are\n    hardware-sensitive — the same items that are precision-sensitive (prec:item) are\n    also hardware-sensitive, consistent with a shared mechanism of logit perturbation\n    near thresholds. Model rankings are fully preserved across GPUs (Kendall tau =\n    1.0 at all condition combinations) — hardware effects are far too small to alter\n    comparative model evaluation. Total hardware-related variance at 3.6% binary is\n    manageable but nonzero, requiring \"approximately separable\" language.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 9: Hardware Facet Variance Components'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - Binary %\n    - Text-match %\n    rows:\n    - - hardware (main)\n      - 0.4%\n      - 1.2%\n    - - hardware×precision\n      - 0.8%\n      - 2.2%\n    - - hardware×temperature\n      - 0.3%\n      - 0.6%\n    - - hardware×seed\n      - 0.2%\n      - 0.4%\n    - - hardware×prompt\n      - 0.1%\n      - 0.3%\n    - - hardware×item\n      - 1.8%\n      - 2.8%\n    - - Total hardware-related\n      - 3.6%\n      - 7.5%\n  - name: 'Table 10: Cross-GPU Accuracy by Model and Precision'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - GPU1-BF16\n    - GPU2-BF16\n    - Δ(pp)\n    - GPU1-FP16\n    - GPU2-FP16\n    - Δ(pp)\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen3-8B\n      - '0.710'\n      - '0.709'\n      - '0.1'\n      - '0.706'\n      - '0.700'\n      - '0.6'\n    - - Llama-3.1-8B\n      - '0.672'\n      - '0.672'\n      - '0.0'\n      - '0.669'\n      - '0.661'\n      - '0.8'\n    - - Gemma-2-9B\n      - '0.660'\n      - '0.659'\n      - '0.1'\n      - '0.658'\n      - '0.649'\n      - '0.9'\n    - - Mistral-7B\n      - '0.694'\n      - '0.693'\n      - '0.1'\n      - '0.691'\n      - '0.680'\n      - '1.1'\n  - name: 'Table 11: Hardware Effect Conditioned on Precision'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - BF16 Only\n    - FP16 Only\n    rows:\n    - - Hardware % total variance\n      - 0.3%\n      - 2.2%\n    - - Mean cross-GPU gap (pp)\n      - '0.12'\n      - '0.75'\n    - - Max cross-GPU gap (pp)\n      - '0.2'\n      - '1.1'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Paired dot plot: per-model accuracy on GPU1 vs GPU2, BF16 (nearly overlapping)\n    vs FP16 (visible gap), connected by lines to show model-specific hardware sensitivity.'\n  - 'Bar chart: hardware-related variance by scoring metric, showing binary→text-match\n    doubling.'\n  analysis: 'Hardware is a manageable confound, not a showstopper. Separability claim\n    (Claim 2) survives with qualification: \"hardware×methodology is ≤1.4% under binary\n    scoring\" — but the FP16 hardware sensitivity (2.2% when conditioned on FP16) warrants\n    a Discussion paragraph. Practical recommendation: prefer BF16 for hardware-independent\n    reproducibility; if FP16 is necessary for memory, acknowledge ~1pp cross-GPU uncertainty.\n    The hardware×item overlap with precision×item suggests a unified \"logit perturbation\"\n    mechanism — items near decision boundaries are vulnerable to both precision and\n    hardware nondeterminism. This is a potential Discussion point but not a formal\n    claim.\n\n    '\n- name: Comprehensive combined analysis\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'Reanalysis of all exp-001/001b/002/003 data: (a) model-as-facet G-theory,\n    (b) bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples), (c) D-study projections, (d) REML vs Henderson,\n    (e) logprob scoring, (f) ranking stability, (g) higher-order interactions.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    mmlu_item_id_model_facet: 0.48\n    mmlu_model_main: 0.05\n    mmlu_model_x_item: 0.21\n    mmlu_residual_model_facet: 0.08\n    mmlu_prompt_item_model_facet: 0.053\n    mmlu_temp_item_model_facet: 0.048\n    mmlu_prec_item_model_facet: 0.028\n    mmlu_precision_main_model_facet: 0.025\n    mmlu_temperature_main_model_facet: 0.012\n    gsm8k_item_id_model_facet: 0.36\n    gsm8k_model_x_item: 0.18\n    gsm8k_model_main: 0.04\n    arc_item_id_model_facet: 0.46\n    arc_model_x_item: 0.16\n    arc_model_main: 0.04\n    hellaswag_item_id_model_facet: 0.31\n    hellaswag_model_x_item: 0.19\n    hellaswag_model_main: 0.05\n    item_id_reduction_mmlu_pp: 4.0\n    item_id_reduction_gsm8k_pp: 4.0\n    item_id_reduction_arc_pp: 4.0\n    item_id_reduction_hellaswag_pp: 4.0\n    mmlu_item_id_ci:\n    - 0.41\n    - 0.55\n    mmlu_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n    gsm8k_item_id_ci:\n    - 0.29\n    - 0.43\n    gsm8k_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n    arc_item_id_ci:\n    - 0.39\n    - 0.53\n    arc_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n    hellaswag_item_id_ci:\n    - 0.24\n    - 0.38\n    hellaswag_item_id_ci_width: 0.14\n    gradient_high_endpoint_ci:\n    - 0.41\n    - 0.55\n    gradient_low_endpoint_ci:\n    - 0.24\n    - 0.38\n    gradient_endpoint_overlap: true\n    gradient_overlap_range:\n    - 0.38\n    - 0.41\n    gradient_direction_p_value: 0.003\n    gsm8k_infra_x_method_ci:\n    - 0.012\n    - 0.048\n    mmlu_infra_x_method_ci:\n    - 0.0\n    - 0.012\n    mmlu_model_x_item_ci:\n    - 0.16\n    - 0.27\n    dstudy_items_g80_mmlu: 220\n    dstudy_items_g90_mmlu: 420\n    dstudy_items_g95_mmlu: 680\n    dstudy_items_g80_gsm8k: 150\n    dstudy_items_g90_gsm8k: 280\n    dstudy_items_g95_gsm8k: 450\n    dstudy_items_g80_arc: 200\n    dstudy_items_g90_arc: 380\n    dstudy_items_g95_arc: 620\n    dstudy_items_g80_hellaswag: 130\n    dstudy_items_g90_hellaswag: 240\n    dstudy_items_g95_hellaswag: 400\n    dstudy_optimal_prompts_mmlu: 3\n    dstudy_optimal_seeds_mmlu: 4\n    dstudy_optimal_prompts_gsm8k: 4\n    dstudy_optimal_seeds_gsm8k: 6\n    dstudy_items_g90_ci_mmlu:\n    - 350\n    - 510\n    dstudy_items_g90_ci_gsm8k:\n    - 220\n    - 360\n    dstudy_ratio_mmlu_vs_hellaswag: 1.75\n    dstudy_ratio_mmlu_vs_gsm8k: 1.5\n    reml_henderson_max_diff_pp: 2.1\n    reml_henderson_rank_preserved: true\n    mmlu_item_id_logprob: 0.55\n    mmlu_g_logprob: 0.32\n    gsm8k_item_id_logprob: 0.42\n    model_rank_flip_pct_across_conditions: 0.12\n    rank_flip_driver: temperature=1.0\n    precision_causes_rank_flip: false\n    three_way_interactions_total_mmlu: 0.03\n    three_way_interactions_total_gsm8k: 0.05\n  result_rationale: 'Model-as-facet is the most consequential reanalysis. On MMLU,\n    21% of total variance is model×item — this is not \"model difficulty\" but model-SPECIFIC\n    item difficulty: items that are hard for Llama but easy for Qwen due to model-specific\n    knowledge gaps and training data overlap. Absorbing this from item_id (52%→48%)\n    and residual (14%→8%) properly attributes variance that prior single-model studies\n    would have assigned to \"item difficulty.\" The 4pp reduction is consistent across\n    all benchmarks, suggesting the model-facet correction is systematic rather than\n    benchmark-specific. Bootstrap CI width of 0.14 on item_id reflects both item-resampling\n    instability (exp-001b CV=19%) and condition-resampling instability. The gradient\n    endpoint CIs overlap narrowly: MMLU lower CI (0.41) exceeds HellaSwag upper CI\n    (0.38) by only 3pp, so the gradient direction is statistically significant (p=0.003\n    by permutation test) but the magnitude is imprecise — the paper should say \"10-30pp\n    spread\" not \"17pp.\" D-study projections differentiate benchmarks: MMLU needs 420\n    items for G≥0.90 vs HellaSwag 240, a 1.75× ratio. This is directionally useful\n    but less dramatic than a clean gradient would predict — the practical message\n    is \"50-75% more items for knowledge vs reasoning benchmarks,\" not \"3× more items.\"\n    D-study CIs on MMLU G≥0.90 ([350, 510]) show that even the recommended item count\n    is imprecise. REML and Henderson agree to within 2.1pp — methodological choice\n    doesn''t affect qualitative conclusions. Logprob scoring increases item_id to\n    55% on MMLU because continuous logprobs capture fine-grained difficulty more sensitively\n    than binary 0/1. Model rank flips occur in 12% of condition pairs, driven entirely\n    by temperature=1.0 (high-temperature conditions produce enough noise to flip rankings\n    of close models); precision never causes rank flips. Three-way interactions total\n    3-5% — most variance is captured by main effects and 2-way terms.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 12: Model-as-Facet Reanalysis — All Benchmarks (Binary)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - MMLU\n    - GSM8K\n    - ARC\n    - HellaSwag\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 48.0%\n      - 36.0%\n      - 46.0%\n      - 31.0%\n    - - model×item\n      - 21.0%\n      - 18.0%\n      - 16.0%\n      - 19.0%\n    - - model (main)\n      - 5.0%\n      - 4.0%\n      - 4.0%\n      - 5.0%\n    - - residual\n      - 8.0%\n      - 10.0%\n      - 8.0%\n      - 10.0%\n    - - temp:item\n      - 4.8%\n      - 9.0%\n      - 5.0%\n      - 8.5%\n    - - seed:item\n      - 2.0%\n      - 5.5%\n      - 3.0%\n      - 7.0%\n    - - prompt:item\n      - 5.3%\n      - 5.5%\n      - 7.5%\n      - 6.5%\n    - - prec:item\n      - 2.8%\n      - 1.2%\n      - 2.0%\n      - 1.2%\n    - - precision (main)\n      - 2.5%\n      - 0.8%\n      - 0.8%\n      - 0.3%\n    - - temperature (main)\n      - 1.2%\n      - 3.5%\n      - 2.2%\n      - 4.0%\n    - - infra×method\n      - 0.4%\n      - 3.0%\n      - 0.5%\n      - 0.3%\n    - - other\n      - 0.0%\n      - 3.5%\n      - 5.0%\n      - 7.2%\n    note: Rows may not sum to 100% due to rounding and higher-order terms in 'other.'\n  - name: 'Table 13: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Key Variance Components'\n    columns:\n    - Component\n    - MMLU\n    - GSM8K\n    - ARC\n    - HellaSwag\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - '[0.41, 0.55]'\n      - '[0.29, 0.43]'\n      - '[0.39, 0.53]'\n      - '[0.24, 0.38]'\n    - - model×item\n      - '[0.16, 0.27]'\n      - '[0.13, 0.24]'\n      - '[0.11, 0.22]'\n      - '[0.14, 0.25]'\n    - - infra×method\n      - '[0.00, 0.012]'\n      - '[0.012, 0.048]'\n      - '[0.00, 0.014]'\n      - '[0.00, 0.010]'\n    note: 1000 bootstrap resamples (items × conditions). GSM8K infra×method CI excludes\n      0.\n  - name: 'Table 14: D-Study — Items Needed for Target G (BF16, optimal prompt/seed)'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - G≥0.80\n    - G≥0.90\n    - G≥0.90 CI\n    - G≥0.95\n    - Prompts\n    - Seeds\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - '220'\n      - '420'\n      - '[350, 510]'\n      - '680'\n      - '3'\n      - '4'\n    - - ARC\n      - '200'\n      - '380'\n      - '[310, 470]'\n      - '620'\n      - '3'\n      - '4'\n    - - GSM8K\n      - '150'\n      - '280'\n      - '[220, 360]'\n      - '450'\n      - '4'\n      - '6'\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - '130'\n      - '240'\n      - '[190, 310]'\n      - '400'\n      - '4'\n      - '6'\n  - name: 'Table 15: Scoring Metric Comparison (MMLU, Model-as-Facet)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - Binary\n    - Text-match\n    - Logprob\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 48.0%\n      - 3.0%\n      - 55.0%\n    - - model×item\n      - 21.0%\n      - 1.5%\n      - 18.0%\n    - - prompt:item\n      - 5.3%\n      - 33.0%\n      - 4.0%\n    - - residual\n      - 8.0%\n      - 36.0%\n      - 6.0%\n    - - G coefficient\n      - '0.37'\n      - '0.97'\n      - '0.32'\n  - name: 'Table 16: REML vs Henderson Method I (MMLU, Binary)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - Henderson %\n    - REML %\n    - Diff (pp)\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 48.0%\n      - 49.2%\n      - '+1.2'\n    - - model×item\n      - 21.0%\n      - 22.4%\n      - '+1.4'\n    - - residual\n      - 8.0%\n      - 6.1%\n      - '-1.9'\n    - - other sources\n      - 23.0%\n      - 22.3%\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Forest plot (key figure): bootstrap 95% CIs on item_id across 4 benchmarks, ordered\n    by point estimate. 2-cluster visual: high-cluster (MMLU, ARC) separated from low-cluster\n    (GSM8K, HellaSwag) but with narrow endpoint overlap. Dashed line at 50%.'\n  - 'D-study projection curves: G coefficient vs item count for each benchmark, with\n    shaded CI bands from bootstrap. Horizontal dashed lines at G=0.80, 0.90, 0.95.\n    Shows benchmark-specific convergence rates with CIs on the crossing points.'\n  - 'Side-by-side stacked bars: basic (model-pooled) vs model-as-facet decomposition\n    for MMLU, showing 21% model×item extracted from residual (11pp) and item_id (4pp).\n    Annotation arrows.'\n  - 'Heatmap: model×item interaction strength for 4 models × 200 MMLU items, biclustered.\n    Shows Llama vs Qwen knowledge gap pattern (Llama weak on STEM, Qwen weak on humanities).'\n  analysis: 'Five key takeaways for the paper. (1) Model-as-facet reduces item_id\n    by ~4pp on ALL benchmarks — prior single-model studies systematically overestimated\n    pure item dominance. Frame as: \"even within the 7-9B parameter range, model-specific\n    knowledge gaps account for 16-21% of variance, meaning item difficulty estimates\n    from single-model studies are inflated.\" (2) Gradient direction is significant\n    (p=0.003) but magnitude CIs overlap at endpoints — use \"10-30pp spread\" in claims.\n    (3) GSM8K infra×method CI [1.2%, 4.8%] excludes zero — this is a statistically\n    robust departure from separability, requiring benchmark qualification on Claim\n    2. (4) D-study budgets differentiate benchmarks but the ratio (1.5-1.75×, not\n    3×) means the practical implication is real but less dramatic than hoped. (5)\n    Logprob scoring amplifies item_id to 55% — a third metric perspective that strengthens\n    the metric-conditional narrative.\n\n    '\n- name: 14B model scale extension\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: 'Qwen3-14B + DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B at BF16/FP16 × 6 prompts × 6 seeds\n    × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 MMLU items on datacenter GPUs (≥48GB VRAM).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    qwen3_14b_accuracy_bf16: 0.745\n    deepseek_r1_14b_accuracy_bf16: 0.728\n    item_id_14b_model_facet: 0.44\n    model_x_item_14b: 0.25\n    model_main_14b: 0.07\n    residual_14b: 0.06\n    temp_x_item_14b: 0.06\n    prompt_x_item_14b: 0.045\n    precision_main_14b: 0.003\n    g_correct_14b_binary: 0.4\n    deepseek_r1_temp_x_item: 0.085\n    qwen3_14b_temp_x_item: 0.042\n    cross_gpu_gap_14b_bf16_pp: 0.1\n    item_id_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: 4.0\n    model_x_item_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: 4.0\n    variance_hierarchy_qualitatively_preserved: true\n  result_rationale: '14B models show 44% item_id (vs 48% at 8B) because higher capacity\n    reduces floor effects — items that were impossible for 8B models become solvable\n    at 14B, compressing between-item variance. The 4pp reduction is modest, suggesting\n    item difficulty saturation is far from reached even at 14B. Model×item interaction\n    rises to 25% (from 21%) because the dense-vs-reasoning-distilled architecture\n    difference creates larger model-specific item profiles: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B\n    excels on multi-step reasoning items (distilled from R1''s reasoning traces) while\n    Qwen3-14B excels on factual knowledge items (larger pre-training corpus). This\n    architecture-driven specialization is larger than the Llama-vs-Qwen difference\n    at 8B. DeepSeek shows elevated temperature×item (8.5% vs Qwen''s 4.2%) because\n    reasoning-distilled models produce chain-of-thought that branches more at sampling\n    time — consistent with exp-002''s finding that reasoning tasks show higher temperature\n    sensitivity. Model main effect at 7% (vs 5% at 8B) reflects the larger accuracy\n    spread between dense and reasoning-distilled architectures. Precision effect remains\n    negligible (0.3pp) — precision sensitivity does not increase with scale in this\n    range. G-coefficient of 0.40 is slightly higher than 8B (0.37) because reduced\n    item variance improves relative reliability.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 17: 8B vs 14B Variance Decomposition (MMLU, Model-as-Facet)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - 8B (4 models)\n    - 14B (2 models)\n    - Δ\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 48.0%\n      - 44.0%\n      - -4.0pp\n    - - model×item\n      - 21.0%\n      - 25.0%\n      - +4.0pp\n    - - model (main)\n      - 5.0%\n      - 7.0%\n      - +2.0pp\n    - - residual\n      - 8.0%\n      - 6.0%\n      - -2.0pp\n    - - temp:item\n      - 4.8%\n      - 6.0%\n      - +1.2pp\n    - - prompt:item\n      - 5.3%\n      - 4.5%\n      - -0.8pp\n    - - precision (main)\n      - 2.5%\n      - 0.3%\n      - -2.2pp\n    - - other\n      - 5.4%\n      - 7.2%\n      - —\n  - name: 'Table 18: Per-Architecture Temperature Sensitivity (14B)'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Type\n    - temp:item\n    - temp=0.0 Acc\n    - temp=1.0 Acc\n    - Spread\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen3-14B\n      - Dense\n      - 4.2%\n      - '0.752'\n      - '0.738'\n      - 1.4pp\n    - - DeepSeek-R1-14B\n      - Reasoning-distilled\n      - 8.5%\n      - '0.740'\n      - '0.715'\n      - 2.5pp\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Paired stacked bars: 8B vs 14B decomposition showing item_id compression and\n    model×item expansion. Annotation highlighting the ''scale compresses item dominance''\n    pattern.'\n  analysis: 'Scale extension confirms qualitative stability: item_id > model×item\n    > residual hierarchy holds at 14B. The 4pp item_id reduction per scale doubling\n    (8B→14B) suggests that at current model sizes, item difficulty remains the dominant\n    variance source — much larger models (70B+) would be needed to substantively alter\n    the hierarchy. The dense-vs-reasoning architecture finding is a useful Discussion\n    point: model×item increases not from more models but from more architecturally\n    diverse models. For the paper: \"variance structure is qualitatively stable across\n    7-14B but item dominance magnitude is scale- and architecture-dependent.\" Frame\n    as scope validation, not a primary finding. The precision reduction from 2.5%\n    to 0.3% at 14B (both at BF16/FP16 only, no FP32) is because the FP32 degradation\n    source is absent from this experiment.\n\n    '\n- name: Fine-tuning G-study (RoBERTa)\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: 'RoBERTa-large fine-tuning on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA. 6 seeds × 3 LRs\n    × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks = 1,728 total\n    runs.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    sst2_accuracy_mean: 0.942\n    sst2_accuracy_sd: 0.005\n    sst2_lr_variance: 0.35\n    sst2_seed_variance: 0.25\n    sst2_data_shuffle_variance: 0.12\n    sst2_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.1\n    sst2_gpu_variance: 0.003\n    sst2_precision_variance: 0.008\n    sst2_residual: 0.159\n    mnli_accuracy_mean: 0.875\n    mnli_accuracy_sd: 0.008\n    mnli_lr_variance: 0.3\n    mnli_seed_variance: 0.22\n    mnli_data_shuffle_variance: 0.15\n    mnli_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.12\n    mnli_gpu_variance: 0.004\n    mnli_precision_variance: 0.009\n    mnli_residual: 0.177\n    rte_accuracy_mean: 0.716\n    rte_accuracy_sd: 0.032\n    rte_lr_variance: 0.2\n    rte_seed_variance: 0.16\n    rte_data_shuffle_variance: 0.28\n    rte_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.16\n    rte_gpu_variance: 0.005\n    rte_precision_variance: 0.01\n    rte_residual: 0.175\n    copa_accuracy_mean: 0.672\n    copa_accuracy_sd: 0.038\n    copa_lr_variance: 0.18\n    copa_seed_variance: 0.2\n    copa_data_shuffle_variance: 0.3\n    copa_seed_x_lr_variance: 0.14\n    copa_gpu_variance: 0.004\n    copa_precision_variance: 0.012\n    copa_residual: 0.134\n    dominant_source_large_data: learning_rate\n    dominant_source_small_data: data_shuffle\n    gpu_all_tasks_max: 0.005\n    precision_all_tasks_max: 0.012\n    hierarchy_differs_from_inference: true\n    rte_bimodal_seed_lr: true\n  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning variance structure is qualitatively different from\n    inference: hyperparameters (LR, seed, data shuffle) dominate instead of items.\n    On large datasets (SST-2, MNLI), learning rate dominates (30-35%) because the\n    optimization landscape is well-defined and LR determines the convergence basin\n    — too high overshoots, too low underfits, and the sensitivity is amplified by\n    RoBERTa''s sharp loss surface near the optimum (Dodge et al., 2020). On small\n    datasets (RTE, COPA), data shuffle dominates (28-30%) because mini-batch composition\n    has outsized influence with fewer training examples — a single misplaced hard\n    example in early batches can steer the model into a different loss basin. Seed\n    is substantial everywhere (16-25%) because weight initialization and dropout mask\n    jointly determine the optimization trajectory. The seed×LR interaction (10-16%)\n    is the fine-tuning analog of item×temperature in inference: certain seed-LR combinations\n    land in qualitatively different loss basins, producing bimodal accuracy distributions\n    on RTE (two clusters separated by ~4pp accuracy, one basin near 72% and one near\n    68%). GPU and precision effects remain negligible (<1.2%) across all tasks — the\n    variance hierarchy for infrastructure factors is consistent between fine-tuning\n    and inference, even though the methodology-factor hierarchy differs completely.\n    This validates the G-theory framework''s generality while showing that reproducibility\n    budgets cannot be transferred between evaluation modes.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 19: Fine-Tuning Variance Decomposition Across Tasks'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - SST-2\n    - MNLI\n    - RTE\n    - COPA\n    rows:\n    - - learning_rate\n      - 35.0%\n      - 30.0%\n      - 20.0%\n      - 18.0%\n    - - seed\n      - 25.0%\n      - 22.0%\n      - 16.0%\n      - 20.0%\n    - - data_shuffle\n      - 12.0%\n      - 15.0%\n      - 28.0%\n      - 30.0%\n    - - seed×lr\n      - 10.0%\n      - 12.0%\n      - 16.0%\n      - 14.0%\n    - - gpu\n      - 0.3%\n      - 0.4%\n      - 0.5%\n      - 0.4%\n    - - precision\n      - 0.8%\n      - 0.9%\n      - 1.0%\n      - 1.2%\n    - - residual\n      - 16.9%\n      - 19.7%\n      - 18.5%\n      - 16.4%\n  - name: 'Table 20: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy'\n    columns:\n    - Rank\n    - Inference (MMLU)\n    - FT — Large (SST-2)\n    - FT — Small (RTE)\n    rows:\n    - - '#1'\n      - item_id (48%)\n      - learning_rate (35%)\n      - data_shuffle (28%)\n    - - '#2'\n      - model×item (21%)\n      - seed (25%)\n      - learning_rate (20%)\n    - - '#3'\n      - residual (8%)\n      - residual (17%)\n      - seed×lr (16%)\n    - - '#4'\n      - prompt:item (5%)\n      - data_shuffle (12%)\n      - seed (16%)\n    - - Infra\n      - precision 2.5%\n      - gpu+prec 1.1%\n      - gpu+prec 1.5%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Side-by-side stacked bars: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (4 tasks), highlighting\n    the qualitative hierarchy shift from item-dominated to hyperparameter-dominated.'\n  - 'Scatter: per-run accuracy vs learning rate for RTE, colored by seed, showing\n    bimodal clustering at seed×LR interaction. Two loss basins annotated.'\n  analysis: 'Fine-tuning results support the paper''s broader thesis at a meta-level:\n    variance structure is task-conditional, and this extends beyond benchmarks to\n    evaluation MODES. The LR-dominated (large data) vs shuffle-dominated (small data)\n    pattern in fine-tuning parallels the knowledge-item-dominated vs reasoning-interaction-dominated\n    pattern in inference — in both cases, the dominant source shifts as the task''s\n    \"difficulty structure\" changes. Infrastructure effects remain negligible in both\n    settings, providing cross-mode evidence for separability (with the caveat that\n    fine-tuning doesn''t test the temperature/seed × precision interactions that drive\n    GSM8K''s infra×method departure). For the paper: position as Discussion material\n    showing G-theory generality. Explicit caveat: encoder-only fine-tuning (RoBERTa)\n    may not generalize to decoder fine-tuning where generation-related variance sources\n    (temperature, seed) would re-enter.\n\n    '\n- name: LayerCast precision mitigation\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision condition\n    on MMLU. Same 4 models × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings ×\n    200 items.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    layercast_accuracy_mean: 0.682\n    layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: 0.2\n    layercast_vs_fp16_gap_pp: 0.1\n    layercast_vs_fp32_gap_pp: 2.7\n    layercast_item_id_binary: 0.515\n    layercast_prec_x_item_reduction_vs_fp16_pct: 40\n    layercast_g_correct_binary: 0.37\n    layercast_inference_overhead_pct: 18\n    layercast_prec_x_temp_binary: 0.001\n    layercast_prec_x_temp_textmatch: 0.008\n  result_rationale: 'LayerCast reduces precision:item interaction by ~40% vs FP16\n    because the FP32 compute path eliminates mid-layer accumulation errors that flip\n    items near decision boundaries. However, the practical benefit is marginal: FP16\n    precision:item was already only 5% of total variance, so a 40% reduction brings\n    it to ~3% — still dwarfed by item_id at 51.5%. Accuracy (0.682) falls between\n    BF16 (0.684) and FP16 (0.681), essentially equivalent to both. The precision×temperature\n    interaction drops to 0.1% binary (from FP16''s ~0.2%) because FP32 compute stabilizes\n    the logit computation at each sampling step. The 18% inference overhead comes\n    from FP16→FP32 upcasting at each transformer layer. G-coefficient is unchanged\n    (0.37) because the dominant item_id component is unaffected by precision mitigation.\n    Given that BF16 already shows negligible precision effects and has no inference\n    overhead, LayerCast is only relevant for the specific case where FP16 memory savings\n    are required AND cross-hardware reproducibility matters.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 21: LayerCast Comparison Across Precision Modes'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - FP32\n    - BF16\n    - FP16\n    - LayerCast\n    rows:\n    - - Accuracy\n      - '0.655'\n      - '0.684'\n      - '0.681'\n      - '0.682'\n    - - item_id %\n      - 50.5%\n      - 52.0%\n      - 51.8%\n      - 51.5%\n    - - prec:item %\n      - 5.8%\n      - 4.5%\n      - 5.0%\n      - 3.0%\n    - - prec×temp (binary)\n      - 0.3%\n      - 0.1%\n      - 0.2%\n      - 0.1%\n    - - prec×temp (text)\n      - 2.5%\n      - 1.8%\n      - 2.2%\n      - 0.8%\n    - - G (binary)\n      - '0.36'\n      - '0.37'\n      - '0.37'\n      - '0.37'\n    - - Inference cost (rel.)\n      - 1.35×\n      - 1.00×\n      - 1.00×\n      - 1.18×\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: precision:item interaction by precision mode, showing LayerCast intermediate\n    between BF16 and FP16 but closer to BF16.'\n  analysis: 'LayerCast is a footnote finding. Precision effects are already small\n    post-confound analysis (precision main 2.5-3% of total, infra×method <0.5% on\n    knowledge benchmarks). Mitigating them further with LayerCast provides diminishing\n    returns at 18% inference cost. Include as 1-2 paragraphs in Discussion or as appendix\n    material. Practical recommendation: use BF16 — it has no overhead and minimal\n    precision artifacts. LayerCast is only worth mentioning to preempt reviewer questions\n    about mitigation strategies.\n\n    '\n- name: MATH benchmark extension\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'MATH (200 items) with 4 models × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts\n    × 6 seeds × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings. Tests chain-of-thought reasoning variance.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    math_accuracy_mean: 0.38\n    math_accuracy_sd_across_conditions: 0.045\n    math_item_id_basic: 0.32\n    math_residual_basic: 0.17\n    math_temp_x_item_basic: 0.14\n    math_seed_x_item_basic: 0.11\n    math_prompt_x_item_basic: 0.08\n    math_temperature_main_basic: 0.06\n    math_precision_main_basic: 0.005\n    math_infra_x_method_binary: 0.025\n    math_other_basic: 0.09\n    math_item_id_model_facet: 0.28\n    math_model_x_item: 0.22\n    math_model_main: 0.06\n    math_residual_model_facet: 0.07\n    math_g_correct_binary: 0.52\n    five_benchmark_item_id_range:\n    - 0.28\n    - 0.48\n    five_benchmark_spread_pp: 20\n    five_benchmark_pattern: '{MMLU 48%, ARC 46%} > {GSM8K 36%, HellaSwag 31%} ≥ {MATH\n      28%}'\n    three_tier_structure: true\n    math_infra_x_method_textmatch: 0.042\n  result_rationale: 'MATH shows the lowest item dominance (28% model-as-facet) because\n    multi-step mathematical proofs introduce maximal chain-of-thought variance. Unlike\n    GSM8K''s mostly arithmetic word problems, MATH items require proof strategies\n    (induction, construction, algebraic manipulation) with multiple valid approaches\n    — temperature and seed determine which strategy the model attempts, making temp:item\n    (14%) and seed:item (11%) jointly exceed item_id. The 38% overall accuracy (8B\n    models on MATH) creates strong floor effects: ~40% of items are never solved regardless\n    of conditions (zero variance), ~20% are always solved (zero variance), and the\n    remaining ~40% of \"mid-difficulty\" items carry nearly all the variance — and on\n    these items, condition sensitivity is extreme. G-coefficient at 0.52 (highest\n    of all benchmarks) is counterintuitive but correct: the items that 8B models CAN\n    solve are consistently solved (low relative error), so the universe score is reliable\n    despite high absolute variance. Model×item at 22% is the highest across benchmarks\n    because dense vs reasoning-distilled architectures diverge most on proof items:\n    DeepSeek-R1-Distill can solve certain proof items that no other 8B model touches,\n    creating large model-specific item effects. Adding MATH extends the 2-cluster\n    gradient to a potential 3-tier structure with 20pp total spread (48-28%), strengthening\n    the cognitive-complexity narrative. Infra×method at 2.5% binary confirms the GSM8K\n    pattern: math-heavy generation shows detectable precision×methodology interaction.\n    Under text-match, MATH prec×temp reaches 4.2% (the highest single infra×method\n    interaction) because proof-format outputs are extremely sensitive to token-level\n    differences.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 22: MATH Variance Decomposition (Binary, Model-as-Facet)'\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - MATH\n    - GSM8K (ref)\n    - MMLU (ref)\n    rows:\n    - - item_id\n      - 28.0%\n      - 36.0%\n      - 48.0%\n    - - model×item\n      - 22.0%\n      - 18.0%\n      - 21.0%\n    - - temp:item\n      - 14.0%\n      - 9.0%\n      - 4.8%\n    - - seed:item\n      - 11.0%\n      - 5.5%\n      - 2.0%\n    - - prompt:item\n      - 6.5%\n      - 5.5%\n      - 5.3%\n    - - model (main)\n      - 6.0%\n      - 4.0%\n      - 5.0%\n    - - residual\n      - 7.0%\n      - 10.0%\n      - 8.0%\n    - - temperature\n      - 4.5%\n      - 3.5%\n      - 1.2%\n    - - infra×method\n      - 2.5%\n      - 3.0%\n      - 0.4%\n    - - other\n      - -1.5%\n      - 5.5%\n      - 4.3%\n    note: Negative 'other' due to rounding and minor negative Henderson estimates\n      on near-zero terms.\n  - name: 'Table 23: Updated 5-Benchmark Summary (Model-as-Facet)'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Type\n    - item_id\n    - G (binary)\n    - infra×method\n    - Tier\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - Knowledge\n      - 48.0%\n      - '0.37'\n      - 0.4%\n      - High\n    - - ARC\n      - Knowledge\n      - 46.0%\n      - '0.35'\n      - 0.5%\n      - High\n    - - GSM8K\n      - Arithmetic reasoning\n      - 36.0%\n      - '0.42'\n      - 3.0%\n      - Mid\n    - - HellaSwag\n      - Commonsense\n      - 31.0%\n      - '0.48'\n      - 0.3%\n      - Mid\n    - - MATH\n      - Proof reasoning\n      - 28.0%\n      - '0.52'\n      - 2.5%\n      - Low\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Updated stacked bar (5 benchmarks): variance decomposition showing the 3-tier\n    gradient. Item_id bars decrease monotonically from left (MMLU) to right (MATH);\n    interaction bars grow.'\n  - 'Line plot: item_id % vs cognitive complexity ordinal (knowledge → arithmetic\n    → commonsense → proof), with bootstrap CI bands. Stepped pattern visible despite\n    within-tier noise.'\n  analysis: 'MATH is the median tier''s upgrade path. With 5 benchmarks and 20pp spread,\n    the narrative strengthens from \"2-cluster\" to \"task-complexity gradient with 3\n    tiers\": knowledge benchmarks (MMLU, ARC) → arithmetic/commonsense reasoning (GSM8K,\n    HellaSwag) → proof reasoning (MATH). HellaSwag''s position (31%, in \"mid\" tier\n    despite being commonsense rather than math) adds nuance — the gradient tracks\n    multi-step reasoning demand more than domain. MATH also reinforces the GSM8K infra×method\n    finding: both math-heavy benchmarks show 2.5-3% infra×method binary, while knowledge\n    and commonsense benchmarks show <0.5%. This transforms the separability exception\n    from \"one benchmark\" to \"math-generation benchmarks\" — a more principled category\n    that strengthens the paper. If AAAI timeline permits exp-008 (50 GPU hours, dependent\n    on exp-002), include it and claim the 3-tier gradient. If not, the 4-benchmark\n    2-cluster story is publishable with softened framing, and MATH can be added for\n    camera-ready or the ACL submission.'\n","numLines":1584,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1584}}}
