item explains about 42% of variance, model×item around 34%, and model itself about 4%) as my baseline for calibrating predictions across the other benchmarks, then I'll write out the YAML configuration incorporating both these actual results and my forecasts for the remaining experiments. of the old Dream #3.\n\nLet me structure the output carefully. I need to include:\n- exp-002 (RUNNING → Phase 1-2 done, Phase 3 partial)\n- exp-003 (descoped → predictions if run)\n- exp-004 (planned → comprehensive analysis predictions)\n- exp-005 (planned → predictions if run)\n- exp-006 (planned → predictions if run)\n- exp-007 (planned → predictions if run)\n- exp-008 (merged into exp-002 → covered in exp-002)\n\nKey calibration points from actual exp-001:\n- Cross-model item_id: 42.1%\n- Cross-model model×item: 33.6%\n- Per-model interaction: Mistral 29.25% > Qwen 27.11% > Gemma 23.95% > Llama 19.69%\n- G_item: 0.820\n- D-\n\nThe 3-model study predicted G=0.816 for n=4, which is very close to the actual 0.820, so I'm on the right track. Now I'm calibrating the median tier assumptions across benchmarks—item_id percentages should decrease from MMLU's 42% down through ARC, HellaSwag, and GSM8K, with gradient spreads of 10-20 percentage points, though HellaSwag breaks the monotonic pattern by having lower item_id than GSM8K. I'm working through the MMLU reference point from the earlier experiment, noting that it used a different design with multiple precisions and temperatures. For the current comparison, I'll use those cross-model numbers directly as my baseline, even though the subset I'm analyzing has a simpler structure with just one precision level and two temperature conditions.\n\nFor the other benchmarks, I'm mapping out a more complex generalizability study design that includes items, models, temperature, prompt variations, seeds, and ordering—with all their interactions—so I can properly decompose the variance across these dimensions.\n\nNow I'm starting to pull together