ng that expected ordering—it's scoring lower than ARC even though I thought it should be harder. Let me check if my complexity ranking is correct by comparing the expected versus actual item_id values across these benchmarks. Now I'm running a statistical test on this sequence to quantify how unlikely the monotonic decrease is—the violation at HellaSwag→GSM8K gives a p-value around 0.12, suggesting the pattern is marginally significant. Breaking down by format, multiple-choice benchmarks cluster around 40%, while completion and free-form tasks average closer to 29%, with that 11-percentage-point gap being the dominant signal compared to variation within format types.\n\nThe overall spread from MMLU to MATH is 16.3 percentage points, or 12.6 if I exclude MATH, which fits the expected range for this tier. Since exp-002 only uses BF16 precision with no variation to test, I can't make claims about precision separability from this data alone. again: precision_separability_all_benchmarks_binary: [0, 5.0]\n\nBut if exp-002 has no precision variation, how do we test this? The paper would need to either:\n1. Reference exp-001 MMLU data for precision separability on MMLU\n2. Run a separate precision probe for other benchmarks (which isn't in the experiment plan)\n3. Not test precision separability across benchmarks\n\nI think for the median tier, the precision separability claim across benchmarks comes from a combination of:\n- exp-001 MMLU data (3 precisions) → confirmed on MMLU\n- exp-002 data has no precision facet → cannot test on other benchmarks\n- The \"one benchmark shows unexpected interaction\" might be from exp-001 MMLU under different analysis (e.g., GSM8K-like items within MMLU that show precision sensitivity)\n\nOR, more likely, the precision separability across benchmarks would be tested in exp-003 (multi-hardware), which is descoped. In the median tier, this is partially addressed but incomplete. The metric \"precision_separability_all_benchmarks_binary: [0, 5.0]\" might refer to the range of precision effects across benchmarks from available data—for exp-001 MMLU, precision×item is around 1.3% based on BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity. In practice, precision separability is only confirmed on MMLU from exp-001, and testing it on other benchmarks would require either a small precision check subset in exp-002 Phase 1 or accepting that this claim remains incomplete in the median tier. data.\n\nLet me go with the second approach: precision separability is only confirmed on MMLU, with other benchmarks requiring additional data (exp-003 or a precision probe). This is more honest for the median tier.\n\nBut then the median tier metric says 0-5% precision separability across benchmarks. If we can only test on MMLU, we know it's <0.1% (BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity after removing FP32). The \"5%\" upper bound would come from a hypothetical worst case.\n\nI think the most internally consistent interpretation is:\n- Precision separability is confirmed on MMLU (<0.1% BF16-vs-FP16)\n- For other benchmarks, it's estimated/predicted but not directly tested\n- The paper would note this as a limitation: \"precision crossing is limited to MMLU; cross-benchmark precision separability requires additional experiments\"\n\nThis is consistent with the median tier's need for softened language and additional qualification.\n\nNow I'm going to write out the YAML structure. I'll organize each experiment with expected results as a detailed metrics dictionary, a rationale paragraph explaining the median tier placement, paper-quality table descriptions with key data, figure descriptions, and an analysis section on implications. For exp-004, I can proceed with available data from exp-001 and the partial results from exp-002, extending the model-as-facet analysis across the four benchmarks we've tested so far.\n\nWriting YAML structure... for MATH\n(b) Bootstrap CIs: can be done\n(c) D-study projections: per benchmark where data exists → 4-5 benchmarks\n(d) REML vs