ooking at typical performance across the four models, I'd expect Qwen to lead at around 70%, Gemma around 63%, Llama around 54%, and Mistral significantly lower at 28%, which would average to roughly 54%. The 64.5% might be specific to Gemma or represent the BF16-only average without FP32 degradation factored in, so I'll use that as the overall T=0.0 accuracy figure for the YAML.\n\nThis would put individual model accuracies at BF16 T=0.0 around 60% for Llama, 68% for Gemma, 78% for Qwen, and 52% for Mistral—averaging to 64.5%. My earlier estimate of Mistral at 25% was too pessimistic; 52% on GSM8K with BF16 T=0.0 is more realistic for Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3 with proper prompting. The jump from 30% to 64.5% likely reflects the registry fix addressing stop sequence issues that were truncating answers before.\n\nAcross both temperatures, the overall average drops to around 62%. The spread between models is now 30 percentage points (48% to 78%) rather than the 44 percentage points I initially thought, which would lower the model effect size for GSM8K—maybe around 8% instead of 13.5%.\n\nI'm trying to scale this properly using variance relationships, but the math gets complicated since variance doesn't scale exactly as the square of range, and with only four models the estimate has high uncertainty anyway. I'll just work with reasonable bounds: given Mistral at 48% and Qwen at 78%, the model effect for GSM8K is probably somewhere between 8-15%, so I'll set it at 10.5%.\n\nNow I'm rebalancing the full variance decomposition across all the factors to make sure everything adds up correctly. The residual comes to 12.4%, which checks out to 100% total. Now I'm looking at the accuracy levels for HellaSwag and ARC-Challenge across the four models—Llama, Gemma, Qwen, and Mistral—with spreads of 17 percentage points on ARC and about 9 points on HellaSwag.\n\nBefore I compose the YAML with all the non-completed experiments, I need to establish the single-model variance decomposition using Llama as my reference point. The key comparison for the paper will be between single-model and cross-model variance, so I'm working through the Llama estimates across the benchmarks to get those baseline numbers. Now I'm laying out the full factorial structure with all main effects and interactions across the facets. For the paper though, I should focus on the key finding: comparing how much variance item identity explains within a single model versus across different models. The single-model numbers show item_id accounts for roughly 48-65% of the variation depending on the benchmark, while the cross-model item_id effect drops substantially to around 37-42%. The total variance actually increases when moving from single to cross-model analysis because the model and model×item components get added in, which means item_id percentages drop both from variance being reclassified as model×item interactions and from the denominator growing. Now I need to stop analyzing and write out the final YAML configuration, though I should first clarify the Phase 3 status for exp-002—Phase 1 and 2 are complete with 4 models across 4 benchmarks and MATH fully done, so I need to verify those exact record counts.\n\nLooking back at the median assumption, Phase 1-2 covers 4 models × 5 benchmarks totaling 1,152,000 records, while Phase 3 has Yi-1.5-9B partially started on only a few benchmarks, giving me roughly 5-6 total models with 5 benchmarks completed.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the actual completion status: Phase 1 finished 4 models across 4 benchmarks, Phase 2 completed those same 4 models on MATH, and Phase 3 has Yi-1.5-9B done on MMLU and ARC plus OLMo-2-7B on just MMLU, bringing the total to about 1.3 million records and 260 GPU hours used. This means I have 6 models and 5 benchmarks to work with for the analysis, including Yi's MMLU accuracy around 65%.\n\nLooking at the 5-model MMLU results, the item-level agreement drops slightly to 41% and the model×item interaction is around 35%, but the generalizability coefficient comes in at 0.845, which matches the D-study prediction of 0.848 almost perfectly—only a 0.003 difference. That's solid validation.\n\nNow I need to write out the YAML configuration, and I'm also deciding whether to include exp-003 since it's marked as descoped rather than planned—the user specifically asked for planned experiments, so I should probably exclude it.\n\nWriting YAML configuration... Now I'm looking at the per-model interaction patterns across different benchmarks—MMLU shows Mistral with the highest variance at 29.25%, while GSM8K inverts this with Mistral dropping to around 35% relative failure rate despite lower overall accuracy, suggesting the interaction effect is benchmark-dependent. I'll structure the YAML with the median tier narrative, detailed exp-002 coverage, and these model-specific patterns as supporting evidence.\n\nWriting YAML config...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23342,"cache_read_input_tokens":88473,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23342},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7213f7b-2e18-414c-bcc7-5947fa0bd98a","uuid":"057fbe18-a4b9-4eee-83f4-36188f190387"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013Y1LUdRuShfJSGB1gD1zD1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have all the context I need. Let me generate the comprehensive median-tier predictions, calibrated against the actual exp-001 results (item_id=42.1% cross-model, model×item=33.6%, G_item=0.820).\n\n```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Reframe gradient from 'monotonic task-type dependence' to 'benchmark-conditional\n  variance structure with task-type as a partial predictor.' Claim 1 softened: 'item_id\n  dominance and model×item interaction magnitudes vary across benchmarks, with task\n  complexity as one moderating factor alongside format and difficulty distribution.'\n  Add explicit analysis disentangling format (MC vs free-form vs completion) vs task-type.\n  Claim 2 adds caveat: precision separability confirmed only on MMLU; cross-benchmark\n  precision testing deferred (exp-003 descoped). Claim 3 strengthened — budget variation\n  across benchmarks makes benchmark-specific budgets even more necessary. Hook adjusted\n  to 'variance structure varies across benchmarks, but the gradient is partially confounded\n  with evaluation format — MC benchmarks (MMLU, ARC) cluster together at ~38-42% item_id,\n  while free-form/completion benchmarks (HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH) cluster at ~26-31%,\n  but within-format ordering is weak.' Contribution 1 reweighted toward model×item\n  quantification (validated in exp-001: 33.6% with out-of-sample D-study Δ=0.004) +\n  benchmark variation. May need ACL ARR October cycle as insurance if HellaSwag anomaly\n  undermines gradient clarity for AAAI reviewers.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Multi-Benchmark G-Study (Phase 1-2 + Phase 3 partial)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Phase 1 DONE: 4 original models × 4 benchmarks (MMLU/ARC-C/HellaSwag/GSM8K),\n      BF16 × 2 temps (0.0, 0.7) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items.\n      Phase 2 DONE: 4 models × MATH (5th benchmark), same reduced design.\n      Phase 3 partial: Yi-1.5-9B completed on MMLU+ARC; OLMo-2-7B completed on MMLU.\n      MMLU data reused from exp-001 BF16 subset (no new collection needed).\n      Total new data: ~1.10M records (Phase 1-2) + ~173K (Phase 3 partial) = ~1.27M.\n    expected_results:\n      completion_status: \"Phase 1-2 done, Phase 3 ~20%\"\n      total_records_new: 1267200\n      total_records_with_exp001_mmlu_reuse: 1324800\n      n_models_primary: 4\n      n_models_total: 6\n      n_benchmarks: 5\n      gpu_hours_used: 258\n\n      per_benchmark_accuracy_bf16_mean:\n        MMLU: {overall: 0.711, Llama: 0.711, Gemma: 0.760, Qwen: 0.726, Mistral: 0.670, spread_pp: 9.0}\n        ARC: {overall: 0.785, Llama: 0.790, Gemma: 0.848, Qwen: 0.818, Mistral: 0.682, spread_pp: 16.6}\n        HellaSwag: {overall: 0.815, Llama: 0.812, Gemma: 0.852, Qwen: 0.838, Mistral: 0.758, spread_pp: 9.4}\n        GSM8K: {overall: 0.645, Llama: 0.615, Gemma: 0.680, Qwen: 0.780, Mistral: 0.485, spread_pp: 29.5}\n        MATH: {overall: 0.283, Llama: 0.218, Gemma: 0.278, Qwen: 0.352, Mistral: \"pending_phase2_tail\", spread_pp_3model: 13.4}\n      \n      # Core finding: cross-benchmark variance gradient (model as crossed facet)\n      cross_model_variance_decomposition:\n        MMLU:\n          item_id: 42.1\n          model_x_item: 33.6\n          model: 3.8\n          prompt_x_item: 3.2\n          seed_x_item: 2.6\n          temperature_x_item: 1.2\n          prompt: 0.5\n          temperature: 0.2\n          seed: 0.1\n          model_x_prompt: 0.3\n          model_x_temperature: 0.1\n          ordering: 0.05\n          residual: 12.25\n        ARC:\n          item_id: 37.8\n          model_x_item: 34.5\n          model: 5.5\n          prompt_x_item: 3.8\n          seed_x_item: 2.3\n          temperature_x_item: 1.6\n          prompt: 0.9\n          temperature: 0.3\n          seed: 0.1\n          model_x_prompt: 0.5\n          model_x_temperature: 0.2\n          ordering: 0.1\n          residual: 12.4\n        HellaSwag:\n          item_id: 29.5\n          model_x_item: 32.8\n          model: 3.2\n          prompt_x_item: 6.2\n          seed_x_item: 4.5\n          temperature_x_item: 2.5\n          prompt: 1.8\n          temperature: 0.5\n          seed: 0.2\n          model_x_prompt: 0.8\n          model_x_temperature: 0.1\n          ordering: 0.1\n          residual: 17.8\n        GSM8K:\n          item_id: 31.2\n          model_x_item: 36.2\n          model: 10.5\n          prompt_x_item: 4.5\n          seed_x_item: 2.8\n          temperature_x_item: 1.8\n          prompt: 1.2\n          temperature: 0.4\n          seed: 0.2\n          model_x_prompt: 0.5\n          model_x_temperature: 0.3\n          ordering: 0.1\n          residual: 10.3\n        MATH_3model:\n          item_id: 25.8\n          model_x_item: 38.5\n          model: 6.5\n          prompt_x_item: 5.8\n          seed_x_item: 4.2\n          temperature_x_item: 2.8\n          prompt: 2.0\n          temperature: 0.8\n          seed: 0.3\n          model_x_prompt: 0.6\n          model_x_temperature: 0.2\n          ordering: 0.1\n          residual: 12.4\n\n      # Single-model item_id for comparison (Llama, BF16)\n      single_model_item_id_Llama:\n        MMLU: 65.2\n        ARC: 58.0\n        HellaSwag: 50.2\n        GSM8K: 56.5\n        MATH: 48.0\n      single_to_cross_item_id_drop_pp:\n        MMLU: -23.1\n        ARC: -20.2\n        HellaSwag: -20.7\n        GSM8K: -25.3\n        MATH: -22.2\n\n      # Gradient analysis\n      item_id_by_hypothesized_complexity_order: \"MMLU(42.1) > ARC(37.8) > HellaSwag(29.5) < GSM8K(31.2) > MATH(25.8)\"\n      gradient_break: \"HellaSwag(29.5) < GSM8K(31.2) — HellaSwag drops below GSM8K despite lower task complexity\"\n      gradient_spread_pp: 16.3\n      gradient_monotonicity_permutation_p: 0.12\n      gradient_monotonicity_interpretation: \"Directional trend exists but not statistically monotonic at α=0.05\"\n\n      # Format confound analysis\n      format_confound:\n        MC_mean_item_id: 40.0\n        completion_item_id: 29.5\n        free_form_mean_item_id: 28.5\n        MC_vs_nonMC_gap_pp: 11.0\n        within_MC_gradient_pp: 4.3\n        within_free_form_gradient_pp: 5.4\n        within_format_conclusion: \"weak_or_inconclusive — direction consistent but N=2 per format, insufficient for statistical test\"\n\n      # G-coefficients (cross-model)\n      G_item_cross_model:\n        MMLU: 0.820\n        ARC: 0.783\n        HellaSwag: 0.723\n        GSM8K: 0.736\n        MATH_3model: 0.698\n\n      # D-study items for G≥0.80 (practical: temperature fixed at T=0.0)\n      D_study_items_G80:\n        MMLU: 414\n        ARC: 452\n        HellaSwag: 535\n        GSM8K: 488\n        MATH: 582\n      D_study_range: \"414–582 items\"\n\n      # Per-model model×item interaction percentage\n      per_model_interaction_pct:\n        MMLU: {Mistral: 29.25, Qwen: 27.11, Gemma: 23.95, Llama: 19.69}\n        ARC: {Mistral: 32.8, Gemma: 25.5, Qwen: 23.2, Llama: 18.5}\n        HellaSwag: {Mistral: 30.5, Llama: 26.2, Qwen: 24.8, Gemma: 18.5}\n        GSM8K: {Mistral: 35.2, Llama: 30.5, Gemma: 22.8, Qwen: 11.5}\n\n      # Phase 3 supplementary analysis (5-model MMLU with Yi)\n      phase3_5model_mmlu:\n        Yi_accuracy_bf16: 0.652\n        item_id_5model: 41.2\n        model_x_item_5model: 35.0\n        G_item_5model: 0.842\n        D_study_predicted_5model: 0.848\n        D_study_delta: 0.006\n        D_study_validation: \"Second out-of-sample validation: 4-model D-study predicted G=0.848 for 5 models, observed 0.842, Δ=0.006\"\n\n    result_rationale: >\n      Gradient exists but is noisy because HellaSwag (completion format, 29.5%) drops\n      below GSM8K (free-form math, 31.2%) in item_id%. The hypothesized knowledge→reasoning\n      gradient predicts HellaSwag should be intermediate between ARC and GSM8K, but\n      HellaSwag's completion format drives its item_id lower than expected: the 4-choice\n      completion paradigm introduces more model-specific pattern-matching variance\n      (prompt×item 6.2%, seed×item 4.5%) than MC or free-form formats, compressing\n      the item_id share. GSM8K retains relatively high item_id (31.2%) because math\n      items have very clear universal difficulty structure — trivial arithmetic vs\n      impossible multi-step problems — creating a floor of item-driven variance that\n      persists even cross-model. The format confound is partial: MC benchmarks (MMLU\n      40.0%, ARC 37.8%) cluster 11pp above non-MC (29.0% mean), but within-format\n      gradients are only 4-5pp with N=2 per format, insufficient to disentangle format\n      from task-type. model×item ranges from 32.8% (HellaSwag) to 38.5% (MATH 3-model),\n      increasing on reasoning benchmarks but not consistently — HellaSwag's model×item\n      (32.8%) is slightly below MMLU's (33.6%), breaking the monotonic increase\n      pattern. GSM8K's model main effect (10.5%) is 2.8× MMLU's (3.8%), driven by\n      Mistral's dramatically lower math performance (48.5% vs 78.0% for Qwen) —\n      this large model spread inflates the model component and correspondingly depresses\n      residual (10.3% vs 12.3% on MMLU). MATH (3-model preliminary) shows the lowest\n      item_id (25.8%) and highest model×item (38.5%), consistent with the gradient\n      direction but the 3-model estimate has wider CI than 4-model benchmarks. The\n      D-study validation replicates: 4-model D-study predicted G=0.848 for 5 models\n      on MMLU, observed 0.842 with Yi added (Δ=0.006), consistent with the exp-001\n      out-of-sample Δ=0.004. D-study budgets vary from 414 (MMLU) to 582 (MATH)\n      items for G≥0.80, but this variation is partially confounded with benchmark\n      design properties (item difficulty distribution, accuracy floor effects) rather\n      than purely task-type. HellaSwag's high budget (535) despite moderate item_id\n      is driven by its large prompt×item (6.2%) and seed×item (4.5%) which inflate\n      the error term in the G-coefficient denominator.\n\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (4 Models, BF16, Binary)\"\n        columns: [Source, MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K, \"MATH†\"]\n        rows:\n          - [item_id, \"42.1%\", \"37.8%\", \"29.5%\", \"31.2%\", \"25.8%\"]\n          - [\"model×item\", \"33.6%\", \"34.5%\", \"32.8%\", \"36.2%\", \"38.5%\"]\n          - [model, \"3.8%\", \"5.5%\", \"3.2%\", \"10.5%\", \"6.5%\"]\n          - [\"prompt×item\", \"3.2%\", \"3.8%\", \"6.2%\", \"4.5%\", \"5.8%\"]\n          - [\"seed×item\", \"2.6%\", \"2.3%\", \"4.5%\", \"2.8%\", \"4.2%\"]\n          - [\"temp×item\", \"1.2%\", \"1.6%\", \"2.5%\", \"1.8%\", \"2.8%\"]\n          - [residual, \"12.3%\", \"12.4%\", \"17.8%\", \"10.3%\", \"12.4%\"]\n          - [\"other (main+2-way)\", \"1.2%\", \"2.1%\", \"3.5%\", \"2.7%\", \"4.0%\"]\n        note: \"†MATH uses 3-model preliminary data (Mistral pending). MMLU from exp-001 BF16 subset.\"\n\n      - name: \"Table: Gradient Summary and Format Confound\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, Format, \"item_id%\", \"model×item%\", G_item, \"D-study G≥0.80\"]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, \"4-choice MC\", \"42.1%\", \"33.6%\", \"0.820\", \"414 items\"]\n          - [ARC-C, \"4-choice MC\", \"37.8%\", \"34.5%\", \"0.783\", \"452 items\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"Free-form\", \"31.2%\", \"36.2%\", \"0.736\", \"488 items\"]\n          - [HellaSwag, \"4-choice completion\", \"29.5%\", \"32.8%\", \"0.723\", \"535 items\"]\n          - [\"MATH†\", \"Free-form\", \"25.8%\", \"38.5%\", \"0.698\", \"582 items\"]\n        note: \"Permutation test for monotonic decrease along hypothesized complexity ordering: p=0.12\"\n\n      - name: \"Table: Single-Model → Cross-Model item_id Drop\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"Single (Llama)\", \"Cross (4-model)\", \"Δ (pp)\", \"model×item absorbed\"]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, \"65.2%\", \"42.1%\", \"-23.1\", \"33.6%\"]\n          - [ARC, \"58.0%\", \"37.8%\", \"-20.2\", \"34.5%\"]\n          - [HellaSwag, \"50.2%\", \"29.5%\", \"-20.7\", \"32.8%\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"56.5%\", \"31.2%\", \"-25.3\", \"36.2%\"]\n          - [MATH, \"48.0%\", \"25.8%\", \"-22.2\", \"38.5%\"]\n\n      - name: \"Table: Per-Model Accuracy by Benchmark (BF16, T=0.0)\"\n        columns: [Model, MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH]\n        rows:\n          - [Llama-3.1-8B, \"0.711\", \"0.790\", \"0.812\", \"0.615\", \"0.218\"]\n          - [Gemma-2-9B, \"0.760\", \"0.848\", \"0.852\", \"0.680\", \"0.278\"]\n          - [Qwen3-8B, \"0.726\", \"0.818\", \"0.838\", \"0.780\", \"0.352\"]\n          - [Mistral-7B, \"0.670\", \"0.682\", \"0.758\", \"0.485\", \"—\"]\n          - [Yi-1.5-9B*, \"0.652\", \"0.735\", \"—\", \"—\", \"—\"]\n        note: \"*Phase 3 partial. OLMo-2-7B completed MMLU only (0.618).\"\n\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Stacked-bar chart (signature figure): 5-benchmark variance decomposition, ordered\n        by item_id descending. item_id in dark blue decreases left→right; model×item in\n        teal increases; prompt×item and seed×item in lighter shades grow for HellaSwag.\n        Format labels (MC / Completion / Free-form) annotated below benchmark names.\n        Clear visual: MC benchmarks have taller blue bars, non-MC have more teal.\n      - >\n        Scatter plot: item_id% vs hypothesized task-complexity rank, with MC benchmarks\n        as circles and free-form/completion as diamonds. Error bars from bootstrap CIs\n        (exp-004). Dotted line shows hypothesized monotonic decrease; actual data shows\n        the HellaSwag→GSM8K break. Shaded bands show format clusters (MC ~38-42%, non-MC ~26-31%).\n      - >\n        Heatmap: per-model model×item interaction magnitude across 5 benchmarks, showing\n        Mistral's interaction increasing dramatically on GSM8K (35.2%) while Qwen's\n        decreases (11.5%), revealing model-specific benchmark sensitivity.\n\n    analysis: >\n      The gradient is the paper's primary novel finding but requires careful framing in\n      median tier. The 16.3pp spread (MMLU 42.1% → MATH 25.8%) is real but the\n      non-monotonicity at HellaSwag→GSM8K (p=0.12) means the paper cannot claim a\n      'systematic monotonic gradient.' Instead: 'benchmark-conditional variance structure\n      with task-type as a partial predictor, confounded with evaluation format.' The\n      MC/non-MC clustering (11pp gap) is the more defensible finding: evaluation format\n      partially determines variance structure independent of task content. Within-format\n      gradient is too weak to draw conclusions (4.3pp within MC, 5.4pp within free-form,\n      N=2 each). model×item as the second-largest source is confirmed across ALL 5\n      benchmarks (32.8-38.5%), strengthening the exp-001 MMLU finding. D-study validation\n      replicates with Yi (Δ=0.006). Precision separability cannot be tested cross-benchmark\n      (exp-002 is BF16-only) — this limits Claim 2 to MMLU evidence from exp-001. MATH\n      3-model data is preliminary; Mistral completion would likely push MATH model×item\n      above 40% given Mistral's expected ~12% accuracy. For AAAI: the gradient+format\n      confound story is publishable with softened claims, but the HellaSwag anomaly\n      will draw reviewer scrutiny — prepare Discussion paragraph explaining completion\n      format's effect on variance structure.\n\n  - name: \"Multi-Hardware G-Study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      DESCOPED — hardware TBD. Predictions below are conditional on obtaining a\n      second GPU type (e.g., A100-80GB or H100). Design: 4 models × 2 GPU types ×\n      2 precisions (BF16, FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings\n      × 200 MMLU items.\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"descoped — no second GPU type available\"\n      total_records_if_run: 1152000\n      hardware_main_binary_pct: 0.3\n      hardware_x_precision_binary_pct: 0.8\n      hardware_x_item_binary_pct: 1.5\n      total_hardware_related_binary_pct: 3.2\n      cross_gpu_gap_bf16_mean_pp: 0.12\n      cross_gpu_gap_fp16_mean_pp: 0.65\n      model_ranking_preserved: true\n      item_id_with_hardware_binary_pct: 41.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Hardware effects would be small (total ~3.2% binary) because both GPU types\n      implement identical IEEE 754 operations; differences arise from CUDA kernel\n      scheduling nondeterminism and memory subsystem timing. FP16 would show 5× larger\n      hardware sensitivity than BF16 (0.65pp vs 0.12pp cross-GPU gap) because FP16's\n      narrower dynamic range makes accumulation order hardware-dependent. This would\n      strengthen Claim 2 by extending \"precision separability\" to \"hardware separability\"\n      — but the experiment is descoped because infrastructure claim was already\n      narrowed to precision per D003/D007. Without this experiment, Claim 2 remains\n      scoped to \"precision on single GPU architecture.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Descoped. Paper must explicitly scope Claim 2: \"numerical precision separability\n      on a single GPU architecture (RTX PRO 6000).\" Hardware crossing is natural\n      future work. The BF16≈FP16 finding from exp-001 (Δ=0.23pp) provides indirect\n      evidence that hardware effects would be negligible for BF16, since BF16's\n      8-bit exponent provides hardware-independent dynamic range.\n\n  - name: \"Comprehensive Combined Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      Pure analysis (no GPU). Depends on exp-001 (DONE) + exp-002 (Phase 1-2 DONE).\n      17 sub-analyses covering bootstrap CIs, D-study projections, REML comparison,\n      IRT vs G-theory, logprob scoring, fixed-vs-random sensitivity, binary data\n      validation, power analysis, GPU cost, and practitioner toolkit.\n    expected_results:\n      # (a) Model-as-facet already done in exp-001 and exp-002 cross-model analysis\n      model_as_facet_note: \"Already incorporated in exp-002 cross-model decomposition\"\n\n      # (b) Bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples)\n      bootstrap_CIs_95pct:\n        MMLU: {item_id: [36.0, 48.8], model_x_item: [28.4, 37.9], model: [1.2, 7.5]}\n        ARC: {item_id: [31.5, 44.2], model_x_item: [29.0, 39.8], model: [2.0, 10.2]}\n        HellaSwag: {item_id: [23.8, 35.5], model_x_item: [27.5, 38.2], model: [0.8, 6.8]}\n        GSM8K: {item_id: [25.0, 37.8], model_x_item: [30.5, 41.8], model: [5.2, 16.5]}\n        MATH_3model: {item_id: [19.2, 33.0], model_x_item: [31.5, 45.2], model: [2.5, 11.5]}\n      gradient_endpoint_CI_overlap: \"MMLU lower bound (36.0) overlaps with GSM8K upper bound (37.8) — gradient direction is robust (p=0.003 permutation) but magnitude is imprecise\"\n      model_x_item_CI_width_note: \"Wide CIs (±5pp) on model×item reflect only 4 models; 8+ models would halve CI width\"\n\n      # (c) D-study projections with CIs\n      D_study_items_with_CI:\n        MMLU: {G80: 414, G80_CI: [350, 490], G90: 510, G90_CI: [430, 610], G95: 720}\n        ARC: {G80: 452, G80_CI: [375, 540], G90: 555, G90_CI: [465, 665], G95: 780}\n        HellaSwag: {G80: 535, G80_CI: [440, 645], G90: 660, G90_CI: [545, 795], G95: 930}\n        GSM8K: {G80: 488, G80_CI: [400, 590], G90: 600, G90_CI: [495, 720], G95: 845}\n        MATH: {G80: 582, G80_CI: [470, 710], G90: 720, G90_CI: [585, 875], G95: 1015}\n      D_study_optimal_allocation:\n        MMLU: {items: 414, prompts: 3, seeds: 3, temps: 1}\n        GSM8K: {items: 488, prompts: 4, seeds: 4, temps: 1}\n\n      # (d) REML vs Henderson I\n      REML_vs_Henderson:\n        max_diff_pp: 0.45\n        rank_preserved_all_benchmarks: true\n        note: \"REML-EM (500 iter, Δ<0.003pp convergence) confirms Henderson I on all benchmarks; balanced design makes Henderson I unbiased\"\n\n      # (e) Logprob scoring\n      logprob_scoring:\n        MMLU_item_id_logprob_pct: 55.2\n        MMLU_model_x_item_logprob_pct: 26.8\n        MMLU_G_logprob: 0.875\n        ARC_item_id_logprob_pct: 52.0\n        HellaSwag_item_id_logprob_pct: 42.5\n        GSM8K_logprob: \"not_applicable — free-form generation, no meaningful per-token logprob\"\n        logprob_vs_binary_item_id_increase_pp: \"10-13pp — continuous logprob captures fine-grained difficulty\"\n\n      # (f) Ranking stability\n      ranking_stability:\n        model_rank_flip_pct: 8.5\n        rank_flip_driver: \"temperature=0.7 conditions only\"\n        precision_causes_rank_flip: false\n        note: \"Llama-Mistral pair flips on 12% of T=0.7 conditions; all other pairs stable\"\n\n      # (g) Higher-order interactions\n      higher_order_interactions:\n        three_way_total_MMLU_pct: 2.8\n        three_way_total_GSM8K_pct: 4.2\n        note: \"Three-way interactions small but nonzero; model×prompt×item is largest (1.5% MMLU, 2.2% GSM8K)\"\n\n      # (h) IRT vs G-theory comparison\n      IRT_vs_Gtheory:\n        IRT_recommended_items_MMLU: 116\n        Gtheory_recommended_items_MMLU: 414\n        ratio_MMLU: 3.57\n        IRT_recommended_items_ARC: 105\n        Gtheory_recommended_items_ARC: 452\n        ratio_ARC: 4.30\n        IRT_recommended_items_HellaSwag: 98\n        Gtheory_recommended_items_HellaSwag: 535\n        ratio_HellaSwag: 5.46\n        IRT_recommended_items_GSM8K: 120\n        Gtheory_recommended_items_GSM8K: 488\n        ratio_GSM8K: 4.07\n        ratio_range: \"3.6–5.5×\"\n        G_at_IRT_count_MMLU: 0.529\n        G_at_IRT_count_ARC: 0.498\n        G_at_IRT_count_HellaSwag: 0.412\n        G_at_IRT_count_GSM8K: 0.485\n        gap_explanation: \"G-theory accounts for prompt×item (3-6%), seed×item (2-5%), and residual (10-18%) that IRT ignores\"\n\n      # (i) Power analysis\n      power_analysis:\n        min_detectable_interaction_pct: 1.2\n        power_at_2pct: 0.95\n        power_at_1pct: 0.72\n        note: \"200 items × ~1150 conditions per benchmark provides >95% power for ≥2% interactions\"\n\n      # (k) Fixed vs random sensitivity\n      fixed_vs_random:\n        temperature_fixed_item_id_change_pp: 1.2\n        ordering_fixed_item_id_change_pp: 0.3\n        combined_max_change_pp: 1.8\n        qualitative_conclusions_preserved: true\n\n      # (l) Binary data validation\n      binary_data_validation:\n        null_simulation_item_id_pct: 0.08\n        observed_item_id_pct: 42.1\n        ratio: 526.3\n        bernoulli_adjustment_preserves_ranking: true\n\n      # (m) Bootstrap vs jackknife CIs\n      bootstrap_vs_jackknife:\n        CI_width_ratio_range: [0.94, 1.06]\n        independence_violation: \"negligible\"\n\n      # (n) Design asymmetry MMLU 3×3 vs 2×2\n      design_asymmetry:\n        MMLU_3x3_item_id: 42.1\n        MMLU_2x2_matched_item_id: 42.8\n        diff_pp: 0.7\n        conclusion: \"Reduced 2×2 design on MMLU reproduces full 3×3 within 1pp\"\n\n      # (o) G at full benchmark size\n      G_full_benchmark:\n        MMLU_14042_items: 0.992\n        ARC_2590_items: 0.972\n        HellaSwag_10042_items: 0.988\n        GSM8K_1319_items: 0.938\n        MATH_5000_items: 0.955\n        note: \"Full benchmarks achieve G>0.93; subsampling to 200 items is the reliability bottleneck\"\n\n      # (q) GPU cost analysis\n      gpu_cost:\n        current_practice_per_model_usd: \"2–5\"\n        D_study_recommended_per_model_usd: \"80–250\"\n        cost_multiplier: \"20–50×\"\n\n    result_rationale: >\n      Bootstrap CIs reveal that model×item is the most uncertain component (±5pp) because\n      4 model levels yield only 3 df for variance estimation — this is the fundamental\n      statistical limitation of the current design. Gradient endpoints overlap: MMLU\n      lower CI (36.0%) meets GSM8K upper CI (37.8%), making the gradient directionally\n      robust (p=0.003) but imprecise in magnitude — the paper must say \"10-20pp spread\"\n      not \"16.3pp.\" D-study CIs are correspondingly wide: MMLU G≥0.80 requires 350-490\n      items depending on bootstrap sample. IRT comparison is one of the strongest findings:\n      the 3.6-5.5× item ratio is consistent across all benchmarks and has a clean causal\n      explanation (prompt×item + seed×item + residual dimensions that IRT ignores).\n      HellaSwag has the worst ratio (5.46×) because its high prompt×item and seed×item\n      inflate the G-theory budget disproportionately. REML confirms Henderson I within\n      0.45pp across all benchmarks — methodological choice is not a concern. Logprob\n      scoring increases item_id to 52-55% because the continuous signal captures\n      fine-grained difficulty (items at 0.55 vs 0.65 logprob difficulty are distinguishable,\n      unlike binary 1/1). Fixed vs random sensitivity shows <2pp change, validating the\n      mixed-model approach. Design asymmetry check (MMLU 3×3 vs 2×2 matched) shows only\n      0.7pp difference, confirming exp-002's reduced design is adequate.\n\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Key Components (All Benchmarks)\"\n        columns: [Component, MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K, \"MATH†\"]\n        rows:\n          - [item_id, \"[36.0, 48.8]\", \"[31.5, 44.2]\", \"[23.8, 35.5]\", \"[25.0, 37.8]\", \"[19.2, 33.0]\"]\n          - [\"model×item\", \"[28.4, 37.9]\", \"[29.0, 39.8]\", \"[27.5, 38.2]\", \"[30.5, 41.8]\", \"[31.5, 45.2]\"]\n          - [model, \"[1.2, 7.5]\", \"[2.0, 10.2]\", \"[0.8, 6.8]\", \"[5.2, 16.5]\", \"[2.5, 11.5]\"]\n        note: \"1000 bootstrap resamples (item × condition). CI width driven by 4-model limitation.\"\n\n      - name: \"Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"IRT items\", \"G-theory items (G≥0.80)\", Ratio, \"G at IRT count\"]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, \"116\", \"414\", \"3.57×\", \"0.529\"]\n          - [ARC, \"105\", \"452\", \"4.30×\", \"0.498\"]\n          - [HellaSwag, \"98\", \"535\", \"5.46×\", \"0.412\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"120\", \"488\", \"4.07×\", \"0.485\"]\n        note: \"G-theory at IRT's recommended item count yields G=0.41-0.53 — far below 0.80 threshold\"\n\n      - name: \"Table: D-Study Benchmark-Specific Budgets (Practical: T fixed)\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"G≥0.80\", \"G≥0.80 CI\", \"G≥0.90\", \"G≥0.90 CI\", \"G≥0.95\", \"Prompts\", \"Seeds\"]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, \"414\", \"[350, 490]\", \"510\", \"[430, 610]\", \"720\", \"3\", \"3\"]\n          - [ARC, \"452\", \"[375, 540]\", \"555\", \"[465, 665]\", \"780\", \"3\", \"3\"]\n          - [HellaSwag, \"535\", \"[440, 645]\", \"660\", \"[545, 795]\", \"930\", \"4\", \"4\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"488\", \"[400, 590]\", \"600\", \"[495, 720]\", \"845\", \"4\", \"4\"]\n          - [MATH, \"582\", \"[470, 710]\", \"720\", \"[585, 875]\", \"1015\", \"4\", \"4\"]\n\n      - name: \"Table: Scoring Metric Comparison (MMLU, 4-Model)\"\n        columns: [Source, Binary, Logprob, \"Text-exact\"]\n        rows:\n          - [item_id, \"42.1%\", \"55.2%\", \"3.1%\"]\n          - [\"model×item\", \"33.6%\", \"26.8%\", \"1.5%\"]\n          - [\"prompt×item\", \"3.2%\", \"2.5%\", \"34.5%\"]\n          - [residual, \"12.3%\", \"5.8%\", \"38.2%\"]\n          - [G_item, \"0.820\", \"0.875\", \"0.006\"]\n\n      - name: \"Table: Methodological Robustness Summary\"\n        columns: [Analysis, Result, Conclusion]\n        rows:\n          - [\"REML vs Henderson I\", \"Max diff 0.45pp, all rankings preserved\", \"Henderson I adequate for balanced design\"]\n          - [\"Fixed vs random (temp/ordering)\", \"Max change 1.8pp\", \"Conclusions robust to facet classification\"]\n          - [\"Bootstrap vs jackknife CIs\", \"Width ratio 0.94–1.06\", \"Independence violation negligible\"]\n          - [\"Design asymmetry (3×3 vs 2×2)\", \"Δ = 0.7pp on MMLU\", \"Reduced design adequate\"]\n          - [\"Null simulation (binary)\", \"Obs/null ratio = 526×\", \"Binary G-theory findings are not artifact\"]\n          - [\"Full-benchmark G projection\", \"G = 0.94–0.99 at full size\", \"Subsampling is the reliability bottleneck\"]\n\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Forest plot: Bootstrap 95% CIs on item_id% across 5 benchmarks. Two visual\n        clusters: MC (MMLU, ARC) at 36-48% and non-MC (HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH) at\n        19-38%. Endpoint CIs overlap narrowly (MMLU lower vs GSM8K upper), annotated\n        with p=0.003 directional test.\n      - >\n        D-study projection curves: G vs item count for each benchmark with shaded\n        bootstrap CI bands. Horizontal lines at G=0.80, 0.90. Shows benchmark-specific\n        convergence rates — MMLU crosses 0.80 earliest (~414), MATH latest (~582).\n        IRT recommendation (100-120 items) marked with vertical band showing G=0.41-0.53.\n      - >\n        Stacked bars: single-model (Llama) vs cross-model (4-model) decomposition\n        for each benchmark. Arrow annotations showing item_id drops (20-25pp) absorbed\n        into model×item. The \"reveal\" figure for Claim 1.\n      - >\n        Side-by-side: Binary vs logprob vs text-exact scoring metric comparison on MMLU.\n        Three very different stacked bars showing metric-conditional variance structure.\n      - >\n        Heatmap: model×item interaction strength, 4 models × 5 benchmarks. Mistral\n        shows hot spots on GSM8K (35.2%) and ARC (32.8%); Qwen shows cold spot on\n        GSM8K (11.5%) reflecting superior math capability.\n\n    analysis: >\n      exp-004 is the analytical backbone of the paper. Five findings for AAAI framing:\n      (1) IRT vs G-theory ratio of 3.6-5.5× across all benchmarks — this is the\n      \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" contribution, now confirmed cross-benchmark.\n      (2) Bootstrap CIs show model×item uncertainty is the limiting factor; the paper\n      must honestly report that 4 models provides only rough magnitude estimates\n      (±5pp). (3) D-study budgets are benchmark-conditional with CIs that overlap\n      substantially (MMLU [350,490] vs GSM8K [400,590]) — the practical message is\n      \"400-600 items for any benchmark\" rather than sharply differentiated budgets.\n      (4) Logprob scoring strengthens the metric-conditional narrative: item_id rises\n      from 42% to 55% under continuous scoring, confirming that binary 0/1 compresses\n      item-level information. (5) All methodological robustness checks pass: REML,\n      fixed-vs-random, bootstrap-vs-jackknife, design asymmetry, null simulation.\n      The paper's Methodological Defense package is complete.\n\n  - name: \"14B Model Scale Extension\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      P1 priority. Blocked on exp-003 (descoped) — dependency can be removed if\n      run independently at BF16 only. Qwen3-14B + DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B on MMLU\n      and GSM8K with reduced design: BF16 × 2 temps × 4 prompts × 4 seeds × 2\n      orderings × 200 items.\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"planned — not started, blocked on hardware availability\"\n      qwen3_14b_mmlu_accuracy: 0.752\n      deepseek_r1_14b_mmlu_accuracy: 0.735\n      qwen3_14b_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.845\n      deepseek_r1_14b_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.812\n      mmlu_item_id_14b_2model: 38.5\n      mmlu_model_x_item_14b: 28.2\n      gsm8k_item_id_14b_2model: 28.5\n      gsm8k_model_x_item_14b: 35.8\n      variance_hierarchy_preserved: true\n      item_id_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: -3.5\n      deepseek_r1_temp_sensitivity_elevated: true\n      deepseek_r1_temp_x_item_gsm8k: 8.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      14B models would show ~3.5pp lower item_id than 8B because higher capacity reduces\n      floor effects — items impossible for 8B become solvable at 14B, compressing\n      between-item variance. model×item rises because dense (Qwen3-14B) vs\n      reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B) architectures create larger\n      model-specific item profiles: DeepSeek excels on multi-step reasoning items\n      (distilled from R1's chain-of-thought traces), Qwen excels on factual knowledge.\n      DeepSeek shows elevated temperature×item on GSM8K (~8.5%) because reasoning-\n      distilled models produce branching chain-of-thought that is more sampling-\n      sensitive. Only 2 models limits statistical power — model×item estimates would\n      have very wide CIs. Position as Discussion-section scale validation, not a\n      primary finding. The practical implication: variance hierarchy is stable at\n      7-14B, suggesting much larger models (70B+) would be needed to alter the structure.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: 8B vs 14B Variance Structure (MMLU, BF16)\"\n        columns: [Source, \"8B (4 models)\", \"14B (2 models)\", \"Δ\"]\n        rows:\n          - [item_id, \"42.1%\", \"38.5%\", \"-3.6pp\"]\n          - [\"model×item\", \"33.6%\", \"28.2%\", \"-5.4pp\"]\n          - [residual, \"12.3%\", \"18.5%\", \"+6.2pp\"]\n        note: \"14B model×item appears lower than 8B but is confounded by N=2 models (high residual absorbs signal)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Paired bars: 8B vs 14B decomposition on MMLU and GSM8K, showing qualitative stability.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Useful for Discussion if compute permits, but not essential for AAAI submission.\n      The key finding (item_id stable, hierarchy preserved) is expected from measurement\n      theory — contribution is confirming the expectation empirically. Low priority\n      given timeline pressure.\n\n  - name: \"Fine-Tuning G-Study (RoBERTa)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      P1 priority, independent of exp-003. RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA.\n      6 seeds × 3 LRs × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task\n      × 4 tasks = 1,728 fine-tuning runs. Validates whether variance hierarchy\n      differs between inference and fine-tuning.\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"planned — not started (200 GPU hours, P1)\"\n      total_fine_tuning_runs: 1728\n      variance_decomposition_summary:\n        SST2: {dominant: \"learning_rate (32%)\", second: \"seed (24%)\", third: \"data_shuffle (13%)\", seed_x_lr: \"11%\", gpu: \"0.3%\", precision: \"0.8%\", residual: \"18.9%\"}\n        MNLI: {dominant: \"learning_rate (28%)\", second: \"seed (22%)\", third: \"data_shuffle (16%)\", seed_x_lr: \"12%\", gpu: \"0.4%\", precision: \"0.9%\", residual: \"20.7%\"}\n        RTE: {dominant: \"data_shuffle (26%)\", second: \"learning_rate (20%)\", third: \"seed×lr (15%)\", seed: \"14%\", gpu: \"0.5%\", precision: \"1.0%\", residual: \"23.5%\"}\n        COPA: {dominant: \"data_shuffle (30%)\", second: \"seed (20%)\", third: \"learning_rate (16%)\", seed_x_lr: \"12%\", gpu: \"0.4%\", precision: \"1.2%\", residual: \"20.4%\"}\n      hierarchy_differs_from_inference: true\n      infrastructure_negligible_both_modes: true\n      rte_bimodal_seed_lr: true\n      rte_bimodal_gap_pp: 4.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Fine-tuning variance is qualitatively different from inference: hyperparameters\n      (LR, seed, data shuffle) dominate instead of items. On large datasets (SST-2,\n      MNLI), learning rate dominates (28-32%) because the optimization landscape is\n      well-defined and LR determines convergence basin. On small datasets (RTE 277\n      examples, COPA 400 examples), data shuffle dominates (26-30%) because mini-batch\n      composition has outsized influence with fewer training examples — a single hard\n      example in early batches steers the model into a different loss basin. seed×LR\n      interaction (11-15%) creates bimodal accuracy on RTE: two loss basins separated\n      by ~4pp, consistent with Dodge et al. (2020) and Bui et al. (2025). GPU and\n      precision effects remain negligible (<1.2%) — the infrastructure-is-negligible\n      finding generalizes from inference to fine-tuning. Frame as Discussion-section\n      preliminary finding showing G-theory generality. Explicit caveat: encoder-only\n      fine-tuning (RoBERTa) may not generalize to decoder fine-tuning.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy\"\n        columns: [Rank, \"Inference (MMLU)\", \"FT-Large (SST-2)\", \"FT-Small (RTE)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"#1\", \"item_id (42%)\", \"learning_rate (32%)\", \"data_shuffle (26%)\"]\n          - [\"#2\", \"model×item (34%)\", \"seed (24%)\", \"learning_rate (20%)\"]\n          - [\"#3\", \"residual (12%)\", \"residual (19%)\", \"seed×lr (15%)\"]\n          - [\"Infra total\", \"precision <0.5%\", \"gpu+prec 1.1%\", \"gpu+prec 1.5%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Side-by-side stacked bars: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (4 tasks),\n        highlighting the qualitative hierarchy shift. Item_id (blue) dominates\n        inference; learning_rate (orange) and data_shuffle (green) dominate fine-tuning.\n    analysis: >\n      Downgraded per Dream #3 directive to Discussion-section preliminary observation.\n      The finding that infrastructure is negligible in BOTH inference and fine-tuning\n      strengthens Claim 2, but this is encoder-only and cannot support broad claims.\n      If AAAI timeline is tight, this experiment is the first to cut. Worth doing for\n      ACL ARR October if time permits.\n\n  - name: \"LayerCast Precision Mitigation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      P2 priority. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision\n      condition on MMLU with 4 models.\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"planned — not started (30 GPU hours, P2)\"\n      layercast_accuracy: 0.713\n      layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: 0.2\n      precision_x_item_layercast_pct: 0.02\n      inference_overhead_pct: 18\n      g_coefficient_unchanged: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      LayerCast reduces precision×item from BF16's already-negligible 0.04% (post\n      BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis) to ~0.02% — the improvement is unmeasurable\n      in practice. With BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp from exp-001) and precision contributing\n      <0.1% of variance, any mitigation strategy provides diminishing returns. The\n      18% inference overhead makes LayerCast cost-ineffective. Position as 1-paragraph\n      appendix material if run; otherwise not worth the GPU hours given AAAI timeline.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Lowest priority. Skip for AAAI unless reviewer explicitly asks about precision\n      mitigation strategies. The BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis from exp-001 already\n      shows precision is a non-issue for practical evaluation (0.0006% variance after\n      excluding FP32).\n\n  - name: \"MATH Benchmark\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Merged into exp-002 Phase 2. All results included in exp-002 predictions above.\"\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"merged_into_exp002\"\n      see: \"exp-002 MATH results (3-model preliminary)\"\n    result_rationale: \"No standalone experiment — MATH data collected as part of exp-002 Phase 2.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: \"See exp-002.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23342,"cache_read_input_tokens":88473,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23342},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7213f7b-2e18-414c-bcc7-5947fa0bd98a","uuid":"c8ca4093-d372-4422-823b-6995ac00c988"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":1415007,"duration_api_ms":1416506,"num_turns":11,"result":"Now I have all the context I need. Let me generate the comprehensive median-tier predictions, calibrated against the actual exp-001 results (item_id=42.1% cross-model, model×item=33.6%, G_item=0.820).\n\n```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Reframe gradient from 'monotonic task-type dependence' to 'benchmark-conditional\n  variance structure with task-type as a partial predictor.' Claim 1 softened: 'item_id\n  dominance and model×item interaction magnitudes vary across benchmarks, with task\n  complexity as one moderating factor alongside format and difficulty distribution.'\n  Add explicit analysis disentangling format (MC vs free-form vs completion) vs task-type.\n  Claim 2 adds caveat: precision separability confirmed only on MMLU; cross-benchmark\n  precision testing deferred (exp-003 descoped). Claim 3 strengthened — budget variation\n  across benchmarks makes benchmark-specific budgets even more necessary. Hook adjusted\n  to 'variance structure varies across benchmarks, but the gradient is partially confounded\n  with evaluation format — MC benchmarks (MMLU, ARC) cluster together at ~38-42% item_id,\n  while free-form/completion benchmarks (HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH) cluster at ~26-31%,\n  but within-format ordering is weak.' Contribution 1 reweighted toward model×item\n  quantification (validated in exp-001: 33.6% with out-of-sample D-study Δ=0.004) +\n  benchmark variation. May need ACL ARR October cycle as insurance if HellaSwag anomaly\n  undermines gradient clarity for AAAI reviewers.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"Multi-Benchmark G-Study (Phase 1-2 + Phase 3 partial)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: >\n      Phase 1 DONE: 4 original models × 4 benchmarks (MMLU/ARC-C/HellaSwag/GSM8K),\n      BF16 × 2 temps (0.0, 0.7) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 4 orderings × 200 items.\n      Phase 2 DONE: 4 models × MATH (5th benchmark), same reduced design.\n      Phase 3 partial: Yi-1.5-9B completed on MMLU+ARC; OLMo-2-7B completed on MMLU.\n      MMLU data reused from exp-001 BF16 subset (no new collection needed).\n      Total new data: ~1.10M records (Phase 1-2) + ~173K (Phase 3 partial) = ~1.27M.\n    expected_results:\n      completion_status: \"Phase 1-2 done, Phase 3 ~20%\"\n      total_records_new: 1267200\n      total_records_with_exp001_mmlu_reuse: 1324800\n      n_models_primary: 4\n      n_models_total: 6\n      n_benchmarks: 5\n      gpu_hours_used: 258\n\n      per_benchmark_accuracy_bf16_mean:\n        MMLU: {overall: 0.711, Llama: 0.711, Gemma: 0.760, Qwen: 0.726, Mistral: 0.670, spread_pp: 9.0}\n        ARC: {overall: 0.785, Llama: 0.790, Gemma: 0.848, Qwen: 0.818, Mistral: 0.682, spread_pp: 16.6}\n        HellaSwag: {overall: 0.815, Llama: 0.812, Gemma: 0.852, Qwen: 0.838, Mistral: 0.758, spread_pp: 9.4}\n        GSM8K: {overall: 0.645, Llama: 0.615, Gemma: 0.680, Qwen: 0.780, Mistral: 0.485, spread_pp: 29.5}\n        MATH: {overall: 0.283, Llama: 0.218, Gemma: 0.278, Qwen: 0.352, Mistral: \"pending_phase2_tail\", spread_pp_3model: 13.4}\n      \n      # Core finding: cross-benchmark variance gradient (model as crossed facet)\n      cross_model_variance_decomposition:\n        MMLU:\n          item_id: 42.1\n          model_x_item: 33.6\n          model: 3.8\n          prompt_x_item: 3.2\n          seed_x_item: 2.6\n          temperature_x_item: 1.2\n          prompt: 0.5\n          temperature: 0.2\n          seed: 0.1\n          model_x_prompt: 0.3\n          model_x_temperature: 0.1\n          ordering: 0.05\n          residual: 12.25\n        ARC:\n          item_id: 37.8\n          model_x_item: 34.5\n          model: 5.5\n          prompt_x_item: 3.8\n          seed_x_item: 2.3\n          temperature_x_item: 1.6\n          prompt: 0.9\n          temperature: 0.3\n          seed: 0.1\n          model_x_prompt: 0.5\n          model_x_temperature: 0.2\n          ordering: 0.1\n          residual: 12.4\n        HellaSwag:\n          item_id: 29.5\n          model_x_item: 32.8\n          model: 3.2\n          prompt_x_item: 6.2\n          seed_x_item: 4.5\n          temperature_x_item: 2.5\n          prompt: 1.8\n          temperature: 0.5\n          seed: 0.2\n          model_x_prompt: 0.8\n          model_x_temperature: 0.1\n          ordering: 0.1\n          residual: 17.8\n        GSM8K:\n          item_id: 31.2\n          model_x_item: 36.2\n          model: 10.5\n          prompt_x_item: 4.5\n          seed_x_item: 2.8\n          temperature_x_item: 1.8\n          prompt: 1.2\n          temperature: 0.4\n          seed: 0.2\n          model_x_prompt: 0.5\n          model_x_temperature: 0.3\n          ordering: 0.1\n          residual: 10.3\n        MATH_3model:\n          item_id: 25.8\n          model_x_item: 38.5\n          model: 6.5\n          prompt_x_item: 5.8\n          seed_x_item: 4.2\n          temperature_x_item: 2.8\n          prompt: 2.0\n          temperature: 0.8\n          seed: 0.3\n          model_x_prompt: 0.6\n          model_x_temperature: 0.2\n          ordering: 0.1\n          residual: 12.4\n\n      # Single-model item_id for comparison (Llama, BF16)\n      single_model_item_id_Llama:\n        MMLU: 65.2\n        ARC: 58.0\n        HellaSwag: 50.2\n        GSM8K: 56.5\n        MATH: 48.0\n      single_to_cross_item_id_drop_pp:\n        MMLU: -23.1\n        ARC: -20.2\n        HellaSwag: -20.7\n        GSM8K: -25.3\n        MATH: -22.2\n\n      # Gradient analysis\n      item_id_by_hypothesized_complexity_order: \"MMLU(42.1) > ARC(37.8) > HellaSwag(29.5) < GSM8K(31.2) > MATH(25.8)\"\n      gradient_break: \"HellaSwag(29.5) < GSM8K(31.2) — HellaSwag drops below GSM8K despite lower task complexity\"\n      gradient_spread_pp: 16.3\n      gradient_monotonicity_permutation_p: 0.12\n      gradient_monotonicity_interpretation: \"Directional trend exists but not statistically monotonic at α=0.05\"\n\n      # Format confound analysis\n      format_confound:\n        MC_mean_item_id: 40.0\n        completion_item_id: 29.5\n        free_form_mean_item_id: 28.5\n        MC_vs_nonMC_gap_pp: 11.0\n        within_MC_gradient_pp: 4.3\n        within_free_form_gradient_pp: 5.4\n        within_format_conclusion: \"weak_or_inconclusive — direction consistent but N=2 per format, insufficient for statistical test\"\n\n      # G-coefficients (cross-model)\n      G_item_cross_model:\n        MMLU: 0.820\n        ARC: 0.783\n        HellaSwag: 0.723\n        GSM8K: 0.736\n        MATH_3model: 0.698\n\n      # D-study items for G≥0.80 (practical: temperature fixed at T=0.0)\n      D_study_items_G80:\n        MMLU: 414\n        ARC: 452\n        HellaSwag: 535\n        GSM8K: 488\n        MATH: 582\n      D_study_range: \"414–582 items\"\n\n      # Per-model model×item interaction percentage\n      per_model_interaction_pct:\n        MMLU: {Mistral: 29.25, Qwen: 27.11, Gemma: 23.95, Llama: 19.69}\n        ARC: {Mistral: 32.8, Gemma: 25.5, Qwen: 23.2, Llama: 18.5}\n        HellaSwag: {Mistral: 30.5, Llama: 26.2, Qwen: 24.8, Gemma: 18.5}\n        GSM8K: {Mistral: 35.2, Llama: 30.5, Gemma: 22.8, Qwen: 11.5}\n\n      # Phase 3 supplementary analysis (5-model MMLU with Yi)\n      phase3_5model_mmlu:\n        Yi_accuracy_bf16: 0.652\n        item_id_5model: 41.2\n        model_x_item_5model: 35.0\n        G_item_5model: 0.842\n        D_study_predicted_5model: 0.848\n        D_study_delta: 0.006\n        D_study_validation: \"Second out-of-sample validation: 4-model D-study predicted G=0.848 for 5 models, observed 0.842, Δ=0.006\"\n\n    result_rationale: >\n      Gradient exists but is noisy because HellaSwag (completion format, 29.5%) drops\n      below GSM8K (free-form math, 31.2%) in item_id%. The hypothesized knowledge→reasoning\n      gradient predicts HellaSwag should be intermediate between ARC and GSM8K, but\n      HellaSwag's completion format drives its item_id lower than expected: the 4-choice\n      completion paradigm introduces more model-specific pattern-matching variance\n      (prompt×item 6.2%, seed×item 4.5%) than MC or free-form formats, compressing\n      the item_id share. GSM8K retains relatively high item_id (31.2%) because math\n      items have very clear universal difficulty structure — trivial arithmetic vs\n      impossible multi-step problems — creating a floor of item-driven variance that\n      persists even cross-model. The format confound is partial: MC benchmarks (MMLU\n      40.0%, ARC 37.8%) cluster 11pp above non-MC (29.0% mean), but within-format\n      gradients are only 4-5pp with N=2 per format, insufficient to disentangle format\n      from task-type. model×item ranges from 32.8% (HellaSwag) to 38.5% (MATH 3-model),\n      increasing on reasoning benchmarks but not consistently — HellaSwag's model×item\n      (32.8%) is slightly below MMLU's (33.6%), breaking the monotonic increase\n      pattern. GSM8K's model main effect (10.5%) is 2.8× MMLU's (3.8%), driven by\n      Mistral's dramatically lower math performance (48.5% vs 78.0% for Qwen) —\n      this large model spread inflates the model component and correspondingly depresses\n      residual (10.3% vs 12.3% on MMLU). MATH (3-model preliminary) shows the lowest\n      item_id (25.8%) and highest model×item (38.5%), consistent with the gradient\n      direction but the 3-model estimate has wider CI than 4-model benchmarks. The\n      D-study validation replicates: 4-model D-study predicted G=0.848 for 5 models\n      on MMLU, observed 0.842 with Yi added (Δ=0.006), consistent with the exp-001\n      out-of-sample Δ=0.004. D-study budgets vary from 414 (MMLU) to 582 (MATH)\n      items for G≥0.80, but this variation is partially confounded with benchmark\n      design properties (item difficulty distribution, accuracy floor effects) rather\n      than purely task-type. HellaSwag's high budget (535) despite moderate item_id\n      is driven by its large prompt×item (6.2%) and seed×item (4.5%) which inflate\n      the error term in the G-coefficient denominator.\n\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Cross-Benchmark Variance Decomposition (4 Models, BF16, Binary)\"\n        columns: [Source, MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K, \"MATH†\"]\n        rows:\n          - [item_id, \"42.1%\", \"37.8%\", \"29.5%\", \"31.2%\", \"25.8%\"]\n          - [\"model×item\", \"33.6%\", \"34.5%\", \"32.8%\", \"36.2%\", \"38.5%\"]\n          - [model, \"3.8%\", \"5.5%\", \"3.2%\", \"10.5%\", \"6.5%\"]\n          - [\"prompt×item\", \"3.2%\", \"3.8%\", \"6.2%\", \"4.5%\", \"5.8%\"]\n          - [\"seed×item\", \"2.6%\", \"2.3%\", \"4.5%\", \"2.8%\", \"4.2%\"]\n          - [\"temp×item\", \"1.2%\", \"1.6%\", \"2.5%\", \"1.8%\", \"2.8%\"]\n          - [residual, \"12.3%\", \"12.4%\", \"17.8%\", \"10.3%\", \"12.4%\"]\n          - [\"other (main+2-way)\", \"1.2%\", \"2.1%\", \"3.5%\", \"2.7%\", \"4.0%\"]\n        note: \"†MATH uses 3-model preliminary data (Mistral pending). MMLU from exp-001 BF16 subset.\"\n\n      - name: \"Table: Gradient Summary and Format Confound\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, Format, \"item_id%\", \"model×item%\", G_item, \"D-study G≥0.80\"]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, \"4-choice MC\", \"42.1%\", \"33.6%\", \"0.820\", \"414 items\"]\n          - [ARC-C, \"4-choice MC\", \"37.8%\", \"34.5%\", \"0.783\", \"452 items\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"Free-form\", \"31.2%\", \"36.2%\", \"0.736\", \"488 items\"]\n          - [HellaSwag, \"4-choice completion\", \"29.5%\", \"32.8%\", \"0.723\", \"535 items\"]\n          - [\"MATH†\", \"Free-form\", \"25.8%\", \"38.5%\", \"0.698\", \"582 items\"]\n        note: \"Permutation test for monotonic decrease along hypothesized complexity ordering: p=0.12\"\n\n      - name: \"Table: Single-Model → Cross-Model item_id Drop\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"Single (Llama)\", \"Cross (4-model)\", \"Δ (pp)\", \"model×item absorbed\"]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, \"65.2%\", \"42.1%\", \"-23.1\", \"33.6%\"]\n          - [ARC, \"58.0%\", \"37.8%\", \"-20.2\", \"34.5%\"]\n          - [HellaSwag, \"50.2%\", \"29.5%\", \"-20.7\", \"32.8%\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"56.5%\", \"31.2%\", \"-25.3\", \"36.2%\"]\n          - [MATH, \"48.0%\", \"25.8%\", \"-22.2\", \"38.5%\"]\n\n      - name: \"Table: Per-Model Accuracy by Benchmark (BF16, T=0.0)\"\n        columns: [Model, MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH]\n        rows:\n          - [Llama-3.1-8B, \"0.711\", \"0.790\", \"0.812\", \"0.615\", \"0.218\"]\n          - [Gemma-2-9B, \"0.760\", \"0.848\", \"0.852\", \"0.680\", \"0.278\"]\n          - [Qwen3-8B, \"0.726\", \"0.818\", \"0.838\", \"0.780\", \"0.352\"]\n          - [Mistral-7B, \"0.670\", \"0.682\", \"0.758\", \"0.485\", \"—\"]\n          - [Yi-1.5-9B*, \"0.652\", \"0.735\", \"—\", \"—\", \"—\"]\n        note: \"*Phase 3 partial. OLMo-2-7B completed MMLU only (0.618).\"\n\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Stacked-bar chart (signature figure): 5-benchmark variance decomposition, ordered\n        by item_id descending. item_id in dark blue decreases left→right; model×item in\n        teal increases; prompt×item and seed×item in lighter shades grow for HellaSwag.\n        Format labels (MC / Completion / Free-form) annotated below benchmark names.\n        Clear visual: MC benchmarks have taller blue bars, non-MC have more teal.\n      - >\n        Scatter plot: item_id% vs hypothesized task-complexity rank, with MC benchmarks\n        as circles and free-form/completion as diamonds. Error bars from bootstrap CIs\n        (exp-004). Dotted line shows hypothesized monotonic decrease; actual data shows\n        the HellaSwag→GSM8K break. Shaded bands show format clusters (MC ~38-42%, non-MC ~26-31%).\n      - >\n        Heatmap: per-model model×item interaction magnitude across 5 benchmarks, showing\n        Mistral's interaction increasing dramatically on GSM8K (35.2%) while Qwen's\n        decreases (11.5%), revealing model-specific benchmark sensitivity.\n\n    analysis: >\n      The gradient is the paper's primary novel finding but requires careful framing in\n      median tier. The 16.3pp spread (MMLU 42.1% → MATH 25.8%) is real but the\n      non-monotonicity at HellaSwag→GSM8K (p=0.12) means the paper cannot claim a\n      'systematic monotonic gradient.' Instead: 'benchmark-conditional variance structure\n      with task-type as a partial predictor, confounded with evaluation format.' The\n      MC/non-MC clustering (11pp gap) is the more defensible finding: evaluation format\n      partially determines variance structure independent of task content. Within-format\n      gradient is too weak to draw conclusions (4.3pp within MC, 5.4pp within free-form,\n      N=2 each). model×item as the second-largest source is confirmed across ALL 5\n      benchmarks (32.8-38.5%), strengthening the exp-001 MMLU finding. D-study validation\n      replicates with Yi (Δ=0.006). Precision separability cannot be tested cross-benchmark\n      (exp-002 is BF16-only) — this limits Claim 2 to MMLU evidence from exp-001. MATH\n      3-model data is preliminary; Mistral completion would likely push MATH model×item\n      above 40% given Mistral's expected ~12% accuracy. For AAAI: the gradient+format\n      confound story is publishable with softened claims, but the HellaSwag anomaly\n      will draw reviewer scrutiny — prepare Discussion paragraph explaining completion\n      format's effect on variance structure.\n\n  - name: \"Multi-Hardware G-Study\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: >\n      DESCOPED — hardware TBD. Predictions below are conditional on obtaining a\n      second GPU type (e.g., A100-80GB or H100). Design: 4 models × 2 GPU types ×\n      2 precisions (BF16, FP16) × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4 orderings\n      × 200 MMLU items.\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"descoped — no second GPU type available\"\n      total_records_if_run: 1152000\n      hardware_main_binary_pct: 0.3\n      hardware_x_precision_binary_pct: 0.8\n      hardware_x_item_binary_pct: 1.5\n      total_hardware_related_binary_pct: 3.2\n      cross_gpu_gap_bf16_mean_pp: 0.12\n      cross_gpu_gap_fp16_mean_pp: 0.65\n      model_ranking_preserved: true\n      item_id_with_hardware_binary_pct: 41.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Hardware effects would be small (total ~3.2% binary) because both GPU types\n      implement identical IEEE 754 operations; differences arise from CUDA kernel\n      scheduling nondeterminism and memory subsystem timing. FP16 would show 5× larger\n      hardware sensitivity than BF16 (0.65pp vs 0.12pp cross-GPU gap) because FP16's\n      narrower dynamic range makes accumulation order hardware-dependent. This would\n      strengthen Claim 2 by extending \"precision separability\" to \"hardware separability\"\n      — but the experiment is descoped because infrastructure claim was already\n      narrowed to precision per D003/D007. Without this experiment, Claim 2 remains\n      scoped to \"precision on single GPU architecture.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Descoped. Paper must explicitly scope Claim 2: \"numerical precision separability\n      on a single GPU architecture (RTX PRO 6000).\" Hardware crossing is natural\n      future work. The BF16≈FP16 finding from exp-001 (Δ=0.23pp) provides indirect\n      evidence that hardware effects would be negligible for BF16, since BF16's\n      8-bit exponent provides hardware-independent dynamic range.\n\n  - name: \"Comprehensive Combined Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: >\n      Pure analysis (no GPU). Depends on exp-001 (DONE) + exp-002 (Phase 1-2 DONE).\n      17 sub-analyses covering bootstrap CIs, D-study projections, REML comparison,\n      IRT vs G-theory, logprob scoring, fixed-vs-random sensitivity, binary data\n      validation, power analysis, GPU cost, and practitioner toolkit.\n    expected_results:\n      # (a) Model-as-facet already done in exp-001 and exp-002 cross-model analysis\n      model_as_facet_note: \"Already incorporated in exp-002 cross-model decomposition\"\n\n      # (b) Bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples)\n      bootstrap_CIs_95pct:\n        MMLU: {item_id: [36.0, 48.8], model_x_item: [28.4, 37.9], model: [1.2, 7.5]}\n        ARC: {item_id: [31.5, 44.2], model_x_item: [29.0, 39.8], model: [2.0, 10.2]}\n        HellaSwag: {item_id: [23.8, 35.5], model_x_item: [27.5, 38.2], model: [0.8, 6.8]}\n        GSM8K: {item_id: [25.0, 37.8], model_x_item: [30.5, 41.8], model: [5.2, 16.5]}\n        MATH_3model: {item_id: [19.2, 33.0], model_x_item: [31.5, 45.2], model: [2.5, 11.5]}\n      gradient_endpoint_CI_overlap: \"MMLU lower bound (36.0) overlaps with GSM8K upper bound (37.8) — gradient direction is robust (p=0.003 permutation) but magnitude is imprecise\"\n      model_x_item_CI_width_note: \"Wide CIs (±5pp) on model×item reflect only 4 models; 8+ models would halve CI width\"\n\n      # (c) D-study projections with CIs\n      D_study_items_with_CI:\n        MMLU: {G80: 414, G80_CI: [350, 490], G90: 510, G90_CI: [430, 610], G95: 720}\n        ARC: {G80: 452, G80_CI: [375, 540], G90: 555, G90_CI: [465, 665], G95: 780}\n        HellaSwag: {G80: 535, G80_CI: [440, 645], G90: 660, G90_CI: [545, 795], G95: 930}\n        GSM8K: {G80: 488, G80_CI: [400, 590], G90: 600, G90_CI: [495, 720], G95: 845}\n        MATH: {G80: 582, G80_CI: [470, 710], G90: 720, G90_CI: [585, 875], G95: 1015}\n      D_study_optimal_allocation:\n        MMLU: {items: 414, prompts: 3, seeds: 3, temps: 1}\n        GSM8K: {items: 488, prompts: 4, seeds: 4, temps: 1}\n\n      # (d) REML vs Henderson I\n      REML_vs_Henderson:\n        max_diff_pp: 0.45\n        rank_preserved_all_benchmarks: true\n        note: \"REML-EM (500 iter, Δ<0.003pp convergence) confirms Henderson I on all benchmarks; balanced design makes Henderson I unbiased\"\n\n      # (e) Logprob scoring\n      logprob_scoring:\n        MMLU_item_id_logprob_pct: 55.2\n        MMLU_model_x_item_logprob_pct: 26.8\n        MMLU_G_logprob: 0.875\n        ARC_item_id_logprob_pct: 52.0\n        HellaSwag_item_id_logprob_pct: 42.5\n        GSM8K_logprob: \"not_applicable — free-form generation, no meaningful per-token logprob\"\n        logprob_vs_binary_item_id_increase_pp: \"10-13pp — continuous logprob captures fine-grained difficulty\"\n\n      # (f) Ranking stability\n      ranking_stability:\n        model_rank_flip_pct: 8.5\n        rank_flip_driver: \"temperature=0.7 conditions only\"\n        precision_causes_rank_flip: false\n        note: \"Llama-Mistral pair flips on 12% of T=0.7 conditions; all other pairs stable\"\n\n      # (g) Higher-order interactions\n      higher_order_interactions:\n        three_way_total_MMLU_pct: 2.8\n        three_way_total_GSM8K_pct: 4.2\n        note: \"Three-way interactions small but nonzero; model×prompt×item is largest (1.5% MMLU, 2.2% GSM8K)\"\n\n      # (h) IRT vs G-theory comparison\n      IRT_vs_Gtheory:\n        IRT_recommended_items_MMLU: 116\n        Gtheory_recommended_items_MMLU: 414\n        ratio_MMLU: 3.57\n        IRT_recommended_items_ARC: 105\n        Gtheory_recommended_items_ARC: 452\n        ratio_ARC: 4.30\n        IRT_recommended_items_HellaSwag: 98\n        Gtheory_recommended_items_HellaSwag: 535\n        ratio_HellaSwag: 5.46\n        IRT_recommended_items_GSM8K: 120\n        Gtheory_recommended_items_GSM8K: 488\n        ratio_GSM8K: 4.07\n        ratio_range: \"3.6–5.5×\"\n        G_at_IRT_count_MMLU: 0.529\n        G_at_IRT_count_ARC: 0.498\n        G_at_IRT_count_HellaSwag: 0.412\n        G_at_IRT_count_GSM8K: 0.485\n        gap_explanation: \"G-theory accounts for prompt×item (3-6%), seed×item (2-5%), and residual (10-18%) that IRT ignores\"\n\n      # (i) Power analysis\n      power_analysis:\n        min_detectable_interaction_pct: 1.2\n        power_at_2pct: 0.95\n        power_at_1pct: 0.72\n        note: \"200 items × ~1150 conditions per benchmark provides >95% power for ≥2% interactions\"\n\n      # (k) Fixed vs random sensitivity\n      fixed_vs_random:\n        temperature_fixed_item_id_change_pp: 1.2\n        ordering_fixed_item_id_change_pp: 0.3\n        combined_max_change_pp: 1.8\n        qualitative_conclusions_preserved: true\n\n      # (l) Binary data validation\n      binary_data_validation:\n        null_simulation_item_id_pct: 0.08\n        observed_item_id_pct: 42.1\n        ratio: 526.3\n        bernoulli_adjustment_preserves_ranking: true\n\n      # (m) Bootstrap vs jackknife CIs\n      bootstrap_vs_jackknife:\n        CI_width_ratio_range: [0.94, 1.06]\n        independence_violation: \"negligible\"\n\n      # (n) Design asymmetry MMLU 3×3 vs 2×2\n      design_asymmetry:\n        MMLU_3x3_item_id: 42.1\n        MMLU_2x2_matched_item_id: 42.8\n        diff_pp: 0.7\n        conclusion: \"Reduced 2×2 design on MMLU reproduces full 3×3 within 1pp\"\n\n      # (o) G at full benchmark size\n      G_full_benchmark:\n        MMLU_14042_items: 0.992\n        ARC_2590_items: 0.972\n        HellaSwag_10042_items: 0.988\n        GSM8K_1319_items: 0.938\n        MATH_5000_items: 0.955\n        note: \"Full benchmarks achieve G>0.93; subsampling to 200 items is the reliability bottleneck\"\n\n      # (q) GPU cost analysis\n      gpu_cost:\n        current_practice_per_model_usd: \"2–5\"\n        D_study_recommended_per_model_usd: \"80–250\"\n        cost_multiplier: \"20–50×\"\n\n    result_rationale: >\n      Bootstrap CIs reveal that model×item is the most uncertain component (±5pp) because\n      4 model levels yield only 3 df for variance estimation — this is the fundamental\n      statistical limitation of the current design. Gradient endpoints overlap: MMLU\n      lower CI (36.0%) meets GSM8K upper CI (37.8%), making the gradient directionally\n      robust (p=0.003) but imprecise in magnitude — the paper must say \"10-20pp spread\"\n      not \"16.3pp.\" D-study CIs are correspondingly wide: MMLU G≥0.80 requires 350-490\n      items depending on bootstrap sample. IRT comparison is one of the strongest findings:\n      the 3.6-5.5× item ratio is consistent across all benchmarks and has a clean causal\n      explanation (prompt×item + seed×item + residual dimensions that IRT ignores).\n      HellaSwag has the worst ratio (5.46×) because its high prompt×item and seed×item\n      inflate the G-theory budget disproportionately. REML confirms Henderson I within\n      0.45pp across all benchmarks — methodological choice is not a concern. Logprob\n      scoring increases item_id to 52-55% because the continuous signal captures\n      fine-grained difficulty (items at 0.55 vs 0.65 logprob difficulty are distinguishable,\n      unlike binary 1/1). Fixed vs random sensitivity shows <2pp change, validating the\n      mixed-model approach. Design asymmetry check (MMLU 3×3 vs 2×2 matched) shows only\n      0.7pp difference, confirming exp-002's reduced design is adequate.\n\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Bootstrap 95% CIs on Key Components (All Benchmarks)\"\n        columns: [Component, MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, GSM8K, \"MATH†\"]\n        rows:\n          - [item_id, \"[36.0, 48.8]\", \"[31.5, 44.2]\", \"[23.8, 35.5]\", \"[25.0, 37.8]\", \"[19.2, 33.0]\"]\n          - [\"model×item\", \"[28.4, 37.9]\", \"[29.0, 39.8]\", \"[27.5, 38.2]\", \"[30.5, 41.8]\", \"[31.5, 45.2]\"]\n          - [model, \"[1.2, 7.5]\", \"[2.0, 10.2]\", \"[0.8, 6.8]\", \"[5.2, 16.5]\", \"[2.5, 11.5]\"]\n        note: \"1000 bootstrap resamples (item × condition). CI width driven by 4-model limitation.\"\n\n      - name: \"Table: IRT vs G-Theory Item Requirements\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"IRT items\", \"G-theory items (G≥0.80)\", Ratio, \"G at IRT count\"]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, \"116\", \"414\", \"3.57×\", \"0.529\"]\n          - [ARC, \"105\", \"452\", \"4.30×\", \"0.498\"]\n          - [HellaSwag, \"98\", \"535\", \"5.46×\", \"0.412\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"120\", \"488\", \"4.07×\", \"0.485\"]\n        note: \"G-theory at IRT's recommended item count yields G=0.41-0.53 — far below 0.80 threshold\"\n\n      - name: \"Table: D-Study Benchmark-Specific Budgets (Practical: T fixed)\"\n        columns: [Benchmark, \"G≥0.80\", \"G≥0.80 CI\", \"G≥0.90\", \"G≥0.90 CI\", \"G≥0.95\", \"Prompts\", \"Seeds\"]\n        rows:\n          - [MMLU, \"414\", \"[350, 490]\", \"510\", \"[430, 610]\", \"720\", \"3\", \"3\"]\n          - [ARC, \"452\", \"[375, 540]\", \"555\", \"[465, 665]\", \"780\", \"3\", \"3\"]\n          - [HellaSwag, \"535\", \"[440, 645]\", \"660\", \"[545, 795]\", \"930\", \"4\", \"4\"]\n          - [GSM8K, \"488\", \"[400, 590]\", \"600\", \"[495, 720]\", \"845\", \"4\", \"4\"]\n          - [MATH, \"582\", \"[470, 710]\", \"720\", \"[585, 875]\", \"1015\", \"4\", \"4\"]\n\n      - name: \"Table: Scoring Metric Comparison (MMLU, 4-Model)\"\n        columns: [Source, Binary, Logprob, \"Text-exact\"]\n        rows:\n          - [item_id, \"42.1%\", \"55.2%\", \"3.1%\"]\n          - [\"model×item\", \"33.6%\", \"26.8%\", \"1.5%\"]\n          - [\"prompt×item\", \"3.2%\", \"2.5%\", \"34.5%\"]\n          - [residual, \"12.3%\", \"5.8%\", \"38.2%\"]\n          - [G_item, \"0.820\", \"0.875\", \"0.006\"]\n\n      - name: \"Table: Methodological Robustness Summary\"\n        columns: [Analysis, Result, Conclusion]\n        rows:\n          - [\"REML vs Henderson I\", \"Max diff 0.45pp, all rankings preserved\", \"Henderson I adequate for balanced design\"]\n          - [\"Fixed vs random (temp/ordering)\", \"Max change 1.8pp\", \"Conclusions robust to facet classification\"]\n          - [\"Bootstrap vs jackknife CIs\", \"Width ratio 0.94–1.06\", \"Independence violation negligible\"]\n          - [\"Design asymmetry (3×3 vs 2×2)\", \"Δ = 0.7pp on MMLU\", \"Reduced design adequate\"]\n          - [\"Null simulation (binary)\", \"Obs/null ratio = 526×\", \"Binary G-theory findings are not artifact\"]\n          - [\"Full-benchmark G projection\", \"G = 0.94–0.99 at full size\", \"Subsampling is the reliability bottleneck\"]\n\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Forest plot: Bootstrap 95% CIs on item_id% across 5 benchmarks. Two visual\n        clusters: MC (MMLU, ARC) at 36-48% and non-MC (HellaSwag, GSM8K, MATH) at\n        19-38%. Endpoint CIs overlap narrowly (MMLU lower vs GSM8K upper), annotated\n        with p=0.003 directional test.\n      - >\n        D-study projection curves: G vs item count for each benchmark with shaded\n        bootstrap CI bands. Horizontal lines at G=0.80, 0.90. Shows benchmark-specific\n        convergence rates — MMLU crosses 0.80 earliest (~414), MATH latest (~582).\n        IRT recommendation (100-120 items) marked with vertical band showing G=0.41-0.53.\n      - >\n        Stacked bars: single-model (Llama) vs cross-model (4-model) decomposition\n        for each benchmark. Arrow annotations showing item_id drops (20-25pp) absorbed\n        into model×item. The \"reveal\" figure for Claim 1.\n      - >\n        Side-by-side: Binary vs logprob vs text-exact scoring metric comparison on MMLU.\n        Three very different stacked bars showing metric-conditional variance structure.\n      - >\n        Heatmap: model×item interaction strength, 4 models × 5 benchmarks. Mistral\n        shows hot spots on GSM8K (35.2%) and ARC (32.8%); Qwen shows cold spot on\n        GSM8K (11.5%) reflecting superior math capability.\n\n    analysis: >\n      exp-004 is the analytical backbone of the paper. Five findings for AAAI framing:\n      (1) IRT vs G-theory ratio of 3.6-5.5× across all benchmarks — this is the\n      \"cost of multi-facet reliability\" contribution, now confirmed cross-benchmark.\n      (2) Bootstrap CIs show model×item uncertainty is the limiting factor; the paper\n      must honestly report that 4 models provides only rough magnitude estimates\n      (±5pp). (3) D-study budgets are benchmark-conditional with CIs that overlap\n      substantially (MMLU [350,490] vs GSM8K [400,590]) — the practical message is\n      \"400-600 items for any benchmark\" rather than sharply differentiated budgets.\n      (4) Logprob scoring strengthens the metric-conditional narrative: item_id rises\n      from 42% to 55% under continuous scoring, confirming that binary 0/1 compresses\n      item-level information. (5) All methodological robustness checks pass: REML,\n      fixed-vs-random, bootstrap-vs-jackknife, design asymmetry, null simulation.\n      The paper's Methodological Defense package is complete.\n\n  - name: \"14B Model Scale Extension\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: >\n      P1 priority. Blocked on exp-003 (descoped) — dependency can be removed if\n      run independently at BF16 only. Qwen3-14B + DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B on MMLU\n      and GSM8K with reduced design: BF16 × 2 temps × 4 prompts × 4 seeds × 2\n      orderings × 200 items.\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"planned — not started, blocked on hardware availability\"\n      qwen3_14b_mmlu_accuracy: 0.752\n      deepseek_r1_14b_mmlu_accuracy: 0.735\n      qwen3_14b_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.845\n      deepseek_r1_14b_gsm8k_accuracy: 0.812\n      mmlu_item_id_14b_2model: 38.5\n      mmlu_model_x_item_14b: 28.2\n      gsm8k_item_id_14b_2model: 28.5\n      gsm8k_model_x_item_14b: 35.8\n      variance_hierarchy_preserved: true\n      item_id_8b_vs_14b_diff_pp: -3.5\n      deepseek_r1_temp_sensitivity_elevated: true\n      deepseek_r1_temp_x_item_gsm8k: 8.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      14B models would show ~3.5pp lower item_id than 8B because higher capacity reduces\n      floor effects — items impossible for 8B become solvable at 14B, compressing\n      between-item variance. model×item rises because dense (Qwen3-14B) vs\n      reasoning-distilled (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B) architectures create larger\n      model-specific item profiles: DeepSeek excels on multi-step reasoning items\n      (distilled from R1's chain-of-thought traces), Qwen excels on factual knowledge.\n      DeepSeek shows elevated temperature×item on GSM8K (~8.5%) because reasoning-\n      distilled models produce branching chain-of-thought that is more sampling-\n      sensitive. Only 2 models limits statistical power — model×item estimates would\n      have very wide CIs. Position as Discussion-section scale validation, not a\n      primary finding. The practical implication: variance hierarchy is stable at\n      7-14B, suggesting much larger models (70B+) would be needed to alter the structure.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: 8B vs 14B Variance Structure (MMLU, BF16)\"\n        columns: [Source, \"8B (4 models)\", \"14B (2 models)\", \"Δ\"]\n        rows:\n          - [item_id, \"42.1%\", \"38.5%\", \"-3.6pp\"]\n          - [\"model×item\", \"33.6%\", \"28.2%\", \"-5.4pp\"]\n          - [residual, \"12.3%\", \"18.5%\", \"+6.2pp\"]\n        note: \"14B model×item appears lower than 8B but is confounded by N=2 models (high residual absorbs signal)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Paired bars: 8B vs 14B decomposition on MMLU and GSM8K, showing qualitative stability.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Useful for Discussion if compute permits, but not essential for AAAI submission.\n      The key finding (item_id stable, hierarchy preserved) is expected from measurement\n      theory — contribution is confirming the expectation empirically. Low priority\n      given timeline pressure.\n\n  - name: \"Fine-Tuning G-Study (RoBERTa)\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: >\n      P1 priority, independent of exp-003. RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA.\n      6 seeds × 3 LRs × 4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task\n      × 4 tasks = 1,728 fine-tuning runs. Validates whether variance hierarchy\n      differs between inference and fine-tuning.\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"planned — not started (200 GPU hours, P1)\"\n      total_fine_tuning_runs: 1728\n      variance_decomposition_summary:\n        SST2: {dominant: \"learning_rate (32%)\", second: \"seed (24%)\", third: \"data_shuffle (13%)\", seed_x_lr: \"11%\", gpu: \"0.3%\", precision: \"0.8%\", residual: \"18.9%\"}\n        MNLI: {dominant: \"learning_rate (28%)\", second: \"seed (22%)\", third: \"data_shuffle (16%)\", seed_x_lr: \"12%\", gpu: \"0.4%\", precision: \"0.9%\", residual: \"20.7%\"}\n        RTE: {dominant: \"data_shuffle (26%)\", second: \"learning_rate (20%)\", third: \"seed×lr (15%)\", seed: \"14%\", gpu: \"0.5%\", precision: \"1.0%\", residual: \"23.5%\"}\n        COPA: {dominant: \"data_shuffle (30%)\", second: \"seed (20%)\", third: \"learning_rate (16%)\", seed_x_lr: \"12%\", gpu: \"0.4%\", precision: \"1.2%\", residual: \"20.4%\"}\n      hierarchy_differs_from_inference: true\n      infrastructure_negligible_both_modes: true\n      rte_bimodal_seed_lr: true\n      rte_bimodal_gap_pp: 4.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      Fine-tuning variance is qualitatively different from inference: hyperparameters\n      (LR, seed, data shuffle) dominate instead of items. On large datasets (SST-2,\n      MNLI), learning rate dominates (28-32%) because the optimization landscape is\n      well-defined and LR determines convergence basin. On small datasets (RTE 277\n      examples, COPA 400 examples), data shuffle dominates (26-30%) because mini-batch\n      composition has outsized influence with fewer training examples — a single hard\n      example in early batches steers the model into a different loss basin. seed×LR\n      interaction (11-15%) creates bimodal accuracy on RTE: two loss basins separated\n      by ~4pp, consistent with Dodge et al. (2020) and Bui et al. (2025). GPU and\n      precision effects remain negligible (<1.2%) — the infrastructure-is-negligible\n      finding generalizes from inference to fine-tuning. Frame as Discussion-section\n      preliminary finding showing G-theory generality. Explicit caveat: encoder-only\n      fine-tuning (RoBERTa) may not generalize to decoder fine-tuning.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy\"\n        columns: [Rank, \"Inference (MMLU)\", \"FT-Large (SST-2)\", \"FT-Small (RTE)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"#1\", \"item_id (42%)\", \"learning_rate (32%)\", \"data_shuffle (26%)\"]\n          - [\"#2\", \"model×item (34%)\", \"seed (24%)\", \"learning_rate (20%)\"]\n          - [\"#3\", \"residual (12%)\", \"residual (19%)\", \"seed×lr (15%)\"]\n          - [\"Infra total\", \"precision <0.5%\", \"gpu+prec 1.1%\", \"gpu+prec 1.5%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - >\n        Side-by-side stacked bars: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (4 tasks),\n        highlighting the qualitative hierarchy shift. Item_id (blue) dominates\n        inference; learning_rate (orange) and data_shuffle (green) dominate fine-tuning.\n    analysis: >\n      Downgraded per Dream #3 directive to Discussion-section preliminary observation.\n      The finding that infrastructure is negligible in BOTH inference and fine-tuning\n      strengthens Claim 2, but this is encoder-only and cannot support broad claims.\n      If AAAI timeline is tight, this experiment is the first to cut. Worth doing for\n      ACL ARR October if time permits.\n\n  - name: \"LayerCast Precision Mitigation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: >\n      P2 priority. LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision\n      condition on MMLU with 4 models.\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"planned — not started (30 GPU hours, P2)\"\n      layercast_accuracy: 0.713\n      layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: 0.2\n      precision_x_item_layercast_pct: 0.02\n      inference_overhead_pct: 18\n      g_coefficient_unchanged: true\n    result_rationale: >\n      LayerCast reduces precision×item from BF16's already-negligible 0.04% (post\n      BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis) to ~0.02% — the improvement is unmeasurable\n      in practice. With BF16≈FP16 (Δ=0.23pp from exp-001) and precision contributing\n      <0.1% of variance, any mitigation strategy provides diminishing returns. The\n      18% inference overhead makes LayerCast cost-ineffective. Position as 1-paragraph\n      appendix material if run; otherwise not worth the GPU hours given AAAI timeline.\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Lowest priority. Skip for AAAI unless reviewer explicitly asks about precision\n      mitigation strategies. The BF16-vs-FP16 sensitivity analysis from exp-001 already\n      shows precision is a non-issue for practical evaluation (0.0006% variance after\n      excluding FP32).\n\n  - name: \"MATH Benchmark\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Merged into exp-002 Phase 2. All results included in exp-002 predictions above.\"\n    expected_results:\n      status: \"merged_into_exp002\"\n      see: \"exp-002 MATH results (3-model preliminary)\"\n    result_rationale: \"No standalone experiment — MATH data collected as part of exp-002 Phase 2.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: \"See exp-002.\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"c7213f7b-2e18-414c-bcc7-5947fa0bd98a","total_cost_usd":2.5815064999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":81335,"cache_read_input_tokens":613208,"output_tokens":65452,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":81335,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":26098,"cache_read_input_tokens":88473,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23342,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23342},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":13610,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.013715,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"in